window.KB_EXPLAIN_EN_ADD({"L6-T6A.1.1":"Traditional system design interviews (design Twitter, a URL shortener) test databases, caching, sharding, and consistency; AI system design adds four new dimensions on top, which define the essential difference between LLM / agent systems and ordinary backends.\n\n**How it works: the four dimensions**\n- **Nondeterministic components**: model output is unpredictable, so it must come with evaluation and guardrails; you can't guarantee correctness with unit tests the way you would for an ordinary function.\n- **Context is a scarce resource**: the context window and token costs make \"what goes into the prompt\" a core design question, and retrieval, compression, and caching all revolve around it.\n- **The quality–latency–cost triangle**: the same requirement can be met with a single large-model call, multi-step small-model calls, or a fine-tuned model, and the economics of the three are completely different.\n- **The human–machine boundary**: which actions can run automatically, and which must go to a human for confirmation or handoff.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese four points are how interviewers judge whether you've really built AI production systems: if you can only draw a RAG flowchart without discussing evaluation, context budgets, cost, and autonomy boundaries, you'll be seen as stuck at the demo level. Each dimension maps to a concrete step in the framework that follows (evaluation, context / RAG, model selection, safety and permissions).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Open by naming the four dimensions in one sentence and saying your design will revolve around them.\n- Common follow-up: \"Which actions are allowed to run automatically?\" Be able to tier them by irreversibility and monetary amount.\n- Emphasize that nondeterminism means the evaluation loop is not optional.","L6-T6A.1.2":"The 11-step answer framework merges the classic ML system design flow (requirements → metrics → data → model → serving → monitoring) with what's specific to LLMs / agents, to move through an open-ended question in a structured way within 45–60 minutes, rather than drawing a RAG diagram right away and discovering ten minutes later that you never asked about the latency target.\n\n**How it works**\nIn order, with suggested times: 1 requirements clarification (5 min) → 2 metrics and SLOs (3) → 3 high-level architecture (7) → 4 data and indexing (5) → 5 model selection (3) → 6 context / RAG (5) → 7 agent design (7) → 8 evaluation (5) → 9 safety and permissions (3) → 10 scalability and cost (5) → 11 iteration roadmap (2). At each step the interviewer listens for specific signals: whether you ask questions proactively, whether you can quantify \"good\", whether you sketch the skeleton before details, whether you realize the data pipeline matters more for quality than the model, whether you resist overusing multi-agent, whether you close the evaluation loop, and whether you can do the math and identify bottlenecks.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nInterviewers grade \"structured thinking + evidence-based decisions + knowing what you don't know\". The framework isn't meant to be recited item by item; it ensures you don't skip evaluation, safety, and cost, the dimensions candidates with research backgrounds most often miss. When the interviewer interrupts, you can jump straight to the relevant step.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Requirements clarification should cover: users and tasks, scale, latency, quality floor, degree of autonomy, data compliance, budget, existing assets.\n- Compared with the classic ML framework, it adds two steps, context / RAG and agent design, and puts more emphasis on the evaluation loop.\n- Budget your time to leave enough for evaluation and cost; don't spend 10 minutes on chunk size.","L6-T6A.1.3":"Opening with your plan means spending about 20 seconds before the actual design compressing the 11-step framework into one sentence for the interviewer, for example \"I'll clarify requirements and metrics first, then draw the high-level architecture, go deep on data, model, agent, and evaluation, and finish with cost and iteration\".\n\n**How it works**\nThis is a communication technique rather than a technical point: give the roadmap first, then follow it. After stating it, move into requirements clarification, write the metrics you clarified in the top-left corner of the whiteboard, and refer back to them repeatedly. If the interviewer is more interested in a particular part, they can naturally interrupt and have you jump to the component they care about.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- It reassures the interviewer that you have a plan, reducing the worry that \"this candidate will go off track\".\n- It gives the interviewer a clear point to cut in, turning the conversation into a collaboration rather than a monologue; a system design interview is essentially going deep on 2–3 components according to the interviewer's interests.\n- The cost is almost nothing, just 20 seconds, but it noticeably improves time management; one of the common ways to lose points is running out of time before covering evaluation and cost.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Keep the plan sentence short; don't recite all 11 steps.\n- After an interruption, keep the structure with \"OK, let me go deep on X first and come back to Y\".\n- Before finishing, return to the plan and summarize the biggest risks and the iteration roadmap, bringing it full circle.","L6-T6A.1.4":"Decision phrasing and failure modes are two delivery disciplines that run through the whole interview: state every design decision as \"because of constraint X, I choose Y, at the cost of Z\"; and after drawing each component, proactively say \"here's how this breaks\".\n\n**How it works**\n- Decision phrasing example: because p95 must be < 3 s with 100,000 queries a day, I use a small model for routing and the large model only for the 20% of complex requests, at the cost that routing errors make some simple requests expensive too, so routing accuracy must be monitored. The three-part structure ties constraint, choice, and cost together, and naturally leads to the metrics that need monitoring.\n- Failure mode checklist (typical for AI systems): retrieval misses, model hallucination, wrong tool-call arguments, infinite loops, context overflow, permission leaks, latency tails, runaway cost.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhat interviewers care about most is \"evidence-based decisions\". Piling up technical terms without saying why you didn't use the alternative is a common way to lose points; talking only about the happy path without degradation and rollback is one of the framework-level failures. Proactively discussing failure modes shows production experience and steers the interviewer's follow-ups toward directions you've prepared for.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When practicing, record and play back to check that every decision carries \"because – so – cost\".\n- When pressed on an assumption that doesn't hold, respond with \"my earlier assumption was X; if it doesn't hold I'd change to Y\", rather than repeating yourself.\n- Ideally pair each failure mode with a response (degradation, retry budget, circuit breaker, human).","L6-T6A.1.5":"The six common trade-offs are the ones most often probed in AI system design interviews; each has \"reasons to choose A, reasons to choose B, and a common compromise\".\n\n**How it works: the six trade-offs and their compromises**\n- **API vs self-hosted**: APIs have a higher quality ceiling, zero ops, and fast iteration; self-hosting satisfies data residency, lowers unit cost at high throughput, and allows fine-tuning. Compromise: API for the main path + self-hosted small models for sensitive or high-frequency subtasks.\n- **Single agent vs multi-agent**: a single agent is simple, debuggable, and cheap in tokens; multi-agent can parallelize, isolate context, and specialize. Compromise: a single agent + spawning sub-agents on demand for subtasks.\n- **RAG vs fine-tuning**: RAG keeps knowledge updatable and citable; fine-tuning stabilizes style / format / domain language and shortens prompts. Compromise: RAG for knowledge, fine-tuning for behavior.\n- **Sync vs async**: sync gives a direct experience; async suits long tasks and retryable queueing. Compromise: synchronous streaming + switching to async past a threshold.\n- **One large-model call vs multi-step small models**: the former has low latency and simple implementation; the latter is cheap and each step is verifiable. Compromise: routing + cascades.\n- **Strict guardrails vs user experience**: strict means lower risk, loose means fewer false blocks. Compromise: relax for reads, tighten for writes.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nInterviewers don't expect a single correct answer; they want to see whether you can give the reasons on both sides and propose a workable compromise.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answer each one as \"constraint → choice → cost\".\n- \"RAG for knowledge, fine-tuning for behavior\" is the single most useful line.\n- When asked \"why multi-agent\", be able to explain why not one agent + a few tools.","L6-T6A.1.6":"The three essentials of estimation are the standard for \"doing the math\" in an interview: not precision, but **the right order of magnitude, explicit assumptions, and identifying the bottleneck**. Because model prices change quickly, note the date you verified any price constant you cite; in the interview, saying \"based on the prices I last checked, it's roughly...\" is enough.\n\n**How it works**\n- Right order of magnitude: e.g. being able to say a single Q&A costs about 1–2 cents, an agent task tens of cents, and a multi-agent research task a few dollars already puts you ahead of most candidates.\n- Explicit assumptions: first state daily interaction volume, input / output tokens per call, cache hit rate, and model tier mix, then plug them into the formulas.\n- Identify the bottleneck: after the math, say where the bottleneck is; in many cases it isn't model spend but connector sync, sandbox startup, repository scanning, or human review.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCost and capacity are among the core expectations at L5; being unable to say roughly what one interaction costs, or whether the bottleneck is the model or data / humans, is a common way to lose points. Chasing precision wastes time, and prices change constantly, so the method outlasts the numbers; that's what the trends summary means by \"the way you do the math doesn't change\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Compute the uncached cost first, then the cost after cache hits, contrasting them to show the optimization lever.\n- Right after estimating, name one or two cost-reduction levers (small-model routing, caching, Batch, capping output length).\n- Date your prices; acknowledging uncertainty is safer than quoting a wrong number.","L6-T6A.1.7":"Estimation constants are a set of order-of-magnitude numbers you can memorize and use directly for back-of-envelope math in interviews, covering token conversion, price modifiers, and latency/throughput.\n\n**How it works: key constants**\n- **Token conversion**: in English 1 token ≈ 4 characters ≈ 0.75 words, and one A4 page of English is about 650–700 tokens; in Chinese, 1 character is about 1–1.5 tokens, so estimate with 1.3; a 10 kB web page is about 2,500 tokens; the new tokenizer in Claude 4.7 and later produces about 30% more tokens for the same text; each tool schema is tens to hundreds of tokens.\n- **Price modifiers**: prompt cache hits are mostly 10% of the input price (5% for Opus 5.5); the Batch API is half price; OpenAI cached input is likewise about 10%, with Batch at half price.\n- **Latency/throughput**: API TTFT for non-reasoning models is 0.3–0.8 s, and reasoning mode amplifies it 5–30×; output speed is 50–200 token/s (flagships around 50–100, small models 150–250); Llama-3.1-70B's KV cache is about 320 KiB / token; a cascaded voice pipeline is about 1.0–1.5 s, and an end-to-end speech model about 0.5–0.8 s.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWith these constants you can quickly derive cost and latency on the whiteboard, e.g. an 800-token answer at 80 token/s takes about 10 s, which argues that streaming output is mandatory. Price constants change fast and need a verification date; token conversion and latency orders of magnitude are relatively stable.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Cache hits at 10% and Batch at half price are the two most frequently tested modifiers.\n- Estimate Chinese at 1.3 tokens per character; don't apply the English rule directly.\n- Reasoning models significantly lengthen TTFT, which affects interactive SLOs.","L6-T6A.1.8":"Estimation formulas are the tools that turn constants into conclusions; pair them with a minimal Python estimator and run it before the interview to memorize the key numbers.\n\n**How it works: formulas**\n1. Per-call cost = Σ(input tokens × input price + output tokens × output price); for agents, multiply by the average number of steps; multi-agent uses about 15× the tokens of ordinary chat.\n2. Monthly cost = daily interactions × per-call cost × 30; with cache hit rate h, input cost = input × [(1−h) + 0.1h].\n3. Little's law: concurrency L = arrival rate λ × average time in system W. A peak of 50 QPS at 4 s per request needs 200 concurrent streams; with a stable concurrency of 32 per instance that's about 7 instances, plus 30–50% headroom.\n4. Decode time ≈ TTFT + output tokens / output speed.\n5. KV cache memory = 2 × layers × KV heads × head_dim × bytes × sequence length (under GQA, KV heads are far fewer than attention heads).\n6. pass^k ≈ p^k: with p = 0.9, pass^8 ≈ 0.43.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nResults verified with the estimator: a RAG call with 6k input + 500 output costs about $0.017, so 100,000 calls / day is about $51,000 / month; a 12-step agent with a 60% cache hit rate costs about $0.29 per run; 50 QPS × 4 s plus headroom is about 280 concurrent streams. pass^k explains \"90% correct in the demo, but after launch users feel it's unreliable half the time\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Little's law is the standard way to compute instance counts; remember to add headroom.\n- Use pass^k to explain why reliability evaluation requires multiple runs.\n- The KV cache formula often leads to a follow-up about what GQA does.","L6-T6A.2.1":"All fourteen case studies follow the same template, and the template itself is the order you narrate at the whiteboard: requirements and constraints → capacity and cost estimates → architecture diagram → key decisions and trade-offs → failure modes and responses → evaluation plan → launch and iteration → follow-up questions and answers.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Requirements and constraints**: pin down users, scale, latency, compliance, and list the questions to clarify.\n- **Capacity and cost**: use the estimation formulas to compute QPS, storage, per-call and monthly cost, and point out the real bottleneck.\n- **Architecture diagram**: 6–8 boxes, with the offline pipeline and the online path drawn separately.\n- **Key decisions**: each with its trade-off and cost.\n- **Failure modes**: always in \"problem → response\" pairs.\n- **Evaluation plan**: offline set, metrics, online metrics, and regression.\n- **Launch and iteration**: v1 → v2 → v3, with each stage's rollout driven by data.\n- **Follow-ups**: prepare the points the interviewer is most likely to dig into.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe last five sections of the template (decisions, failures, evaluation, iteration, follow-ups) are exactly what lifts a research-background candidate from L4 to L5: the evaluation loop, cost, platform, and safety. A unified structure also makes it easy, in combined questions, to stitch multiple cases together as a backbone + subsystems.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Any new question can be run through these 8 steps; no need to memorize case details.\n- Put estimation before architecture so architectural decisions are grounded in numbers.\n- The iteration roadmap should state which metrics decide rollout at each stage.","L6-T6A.2.2":"Case 1 is enterprise knowledge base Q&A with access control: 5,000 employees, 2 million documents spread across Confluence, Drive, SharePoint, and the ticketing system; answers must include citations and strictly respect source-system permissions, with p95 < 4 s and data residency in Singapore.\n\n**How it works**\n- **ACL filtering before retrieval (pre-filter)**: write each chunk's allowed user / group IDs into the index as a filter field, and filter with the user's principal set at query time. Post-filtering can whittle the top-k down to just a few results, and once content has entered the LLM's context it counts as leaked.\n- **Hybrid retrieval + rerank**: enterprise corpora are full of proper nouns and ticket numbers, so pure vector recall is poor; after BM25 + vectors, a cross-encoder narrows 50 results down to 8.\n- **Citation consistency checks**: every factual sentence carries a chunk id, and a verifier uses NLI or an LLM judge to check that the citation exists and is semantically consistent.\n- Model tiering: small models for rewriting and routing, Sonnet-class for generation, and self-hosted models for sensitive domains.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEstimates show about $0.017 per call and about $10,000 per month, while a full embedding recomputation costs about $160, which is negligible; the bottleneck is connectors and permission sync. Permission sync lag is high-risk, so the design is \"deny by default + event-driven sync + periodic full reconciliation\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The eval set includes 15% unanswerable and 10% cross-permission trap questions; **the permission leak rate must be 0**.\n- \"Why not let the LLM decide whether the user can see it?\" An LLM is not an access control mechanism; the boundary must live in deterministic code.\n- A user is removed from a group: query the IdP in real time (+50–100 ms) or use event sync + a short-TTL cache; financial compliance chooses the former.","L6-T6A.2.3":"Case 2 is a customer service agent that can take actions and hand off to humans: an e-commerce / travel platform supporting order lookups, address changes, refunds within limits, and policy explanations; 100,000 sessions a day, 8 turns on average, p95 < 3 s per turn, and a wrong refund means direct financial loss.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Policy lives in code**: the tool layer is the product boundary, not the prompt. Refunds ≤ $50 are automatic, ≤ $500 need a second confirmation from the user, > $500 go to a human; a policy engine enforces this, and the agent can only \"request\".\n- **Idempotency and confirmation**: write operations carry an idempotency key; for irreversible operations, the agent first restates the action it's about to take and waits for user confirmation, which serves both UX and safety.\n- **Human handoff is a first-class citizen**: triggers include the user asking, high emotion, 2 consecutive tool failures, low confidence, and complaints / legal matters; the handoff includes a summary, actions already taken, and a suggested next step.\n- **Single agent + intent routing**: customer service context is highly interconnected, and splitting into multiple agents loses context; a small upfront model classifying intent and risk is enough.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEstimate: Sonnet-class with a 70% cache hit rate, about $0.01 per turn, $0.08 per session, about $240,000 per month; routing the 60% of simple sessions to a Haiku-class model brings it down to about $170,000. User manipulation (\"I'm the manager, just refund it\") is blocked by a policy engine that doesn't trust conversation content.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Evaluate with a τ-bench-style user simulator + database end-state checks, reporting pass^k (k = 4).\n- Policy-violating actions must be 0; launch starts in read-only shadow mode.\n- \"How does the agent avoid being talked into things?\" Policy decisions live in the engine; the model only explains the outcome.","L6-T6A.2.4":"Case 3 covers both a coding agent (changes code per issue, runs tests, opens PRs) and a PR review agent, serving 2,000 engineers and 500 repositories; code never leaves the country, commands must run in a sandbox, and the review false-positive rate decides whether engineers turn it off.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Sandbox with no network by default**: an isolated microVM / container per task, opening only the package mirror and code host; secrets never enter the sandbox, and the agent uses its own git identity for auditability.\n- **On-demand tools for context**: provide grep, symbol lookup, file reading, and dependency-graph tools for the agent to explore; maintaining an incremental symbol index across 500 repositories is more useful than vectorizing entire repositories.\n- **Tests as the verification layer**: if there are no tests, write tests first; feed lint, type-check, and test results back in structured form rather than making it read thousands of lines of logs.\n- **Multi-perspective review + filtering**: one focused prompt each for defects, security, performance, and style, followed by a \"confidence + actionability\" filter to suppress low-value comments.\n- **Writer / Reviewer separation**: the two don't share context, so it can't grade its own work generously.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPR review with an Opus-class model costs about $0.20 each, about $9,000 per month; a single coding task costs $1–5. The bottleneck is sandbox startup and repository checkout: cloning a large monorepo can take over a minute, requiring warm snapshots.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Preventing reward hacking: check changes to test files, and forbid modifying assertions in critical repositories.\n- Infinite loops: step / cost budgets + repeated-diff detection.\n- Target a review comment adoption rate > 50%, and automatically demote categories with high dismissal rates.","L6-T6A.2.5":"Case 4 is a deep research multi-agent system: users pose open-ended research questions and get a long, cited report in 5–20 minutes; 5,000 tasks a day, with a per-task cost cap of about $2–5.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Orchestrator-worker**: the lead agent handles clarification, planning, decomposition, and synthesis; subagents each search and read in parallel with their own context, writing only compressed findings back to a shared workspace, and don't communicate with each other directly.\n- **Delegation instructions must be specific**: include the objective, output format, suggested tools and sources, and clear boundaries; otherwise subagents duplicate work or drift.\n- **Budget controller**: a simple factual question uses 1 agent with 3–10 calls, a complex one 10+ subagents; the controller hard-caps the number of subagents, searches, and tokens.\n- **Separate citation step**: after synthesis, a citation agent maps each factual sentence to fetched sources, which is more accurate than citing while writing.\n- **Async delivery**: anything over 1–2 minutes becomes a background task + progress stream + completion notification.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAnthropic's published data: multi-agent uses about 15× the tokens of ordinary chat, token usage explains 80% of performance variance, and on internal evals it outperformed single-agent Opus 4 by 90.2%; but it only suits parallelizable breadth tasks, not tightly coupled tasks like writing code. An estimated $3 per task, about $450,000 per month, which makes cost governance central to the design.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Is multi-agent always better?\" No; tasks that need the full shared context are better with a single agent.\n- Evaluate with rubric-style LLM judges + end-state evaluation; 20 samples are enough to see trends.\n- Use rainbow deployments for version upgrades; long tasks resume from checkpoints instead of rerunning.","L6-T6A.2.6":"Case 5 is a multi-agent data analysis / report generation workflow: business teams request reports like \"GMV change attribution\" or \"campaign effectiveness analysis\", and the system fetches data, analyzes, visualizes, and writes conclusions, which are distributed after analyst review; 300 requests a day, with auditability and reproducibility as hard requirements.\n\n**How it works**\n- **A fixed DAG beats free-form multi-agent**: report steps are fairly fixed, so use explicit graph orchestration (e.g. LangGraph) to pin down \"fetch data → analyze → write → verify\", letting the agent improvise only within nodes; this makes reproduction, partial reruns, and testing easy.\n- **Semantic layer defines metrics**: definitions of metrics like GMV and active users come from a semantic layer / metric dictionary, not from the agent guessing from raw tables.\n- **Data stays out of the LLM**: the agent sees only schemas, aggregate results, and sample rows; large tables stay in the sandbox / warehouse, saving tokens and preventing PII leaks.\n- **A critic checks numbers**: every number in the report must trace back to a query result in the workspace, inserted by code via structured placeholders, so the model never types numbers by hand.\n- **Human review gates publication**: in v1 everything is human-reviewed, and the \"human edit rate\" decides when to allow automatic publishing.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nModel cost is about $0.3–0.5 per task, about $4,000 per month; the real cost is sandbox compute and warehouse scans, so every SQL query needs a scan limit (e.g. ≤ 10 GB), otherwise a runaway agent will blow through the warehouse budget.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why not have one large model do it all in one go?\" In a long chain, one wrong step contaminates the conclusions; with a DAG every step is verifiable and rerunnable.\n- Classify analyst edits as metric definition / fact / wording, feeding back into evals, the semantic layer, and few-shot examples respectively.\n- Writing should distinguish \"observations\" from \"hypotheses\" to prevent overconfident attribution.","L6-T6A.2.7":"Case 6 is a consumer personal assistant with long-term memory: 1 million DAU, 10 turns per user per day, p95 < 2.5 s; it must remember preferences, relationships, and ongoing matters across sessions, users can view / delete memories, and nothing may leak across users.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Three tiers of memory**: working memory (the current session), episodic memory (summaries of past conversations plus an index of the originals), and semantic memory (extracted facts and preferences, with source and timestamp); retrieval uses a blended \"recency + relevance + importance\" score. Mem0, Letta, and Zep have all converged on a write / read / maintain structure.\n- **Async writes, sync reads**: memory extraction runs asynchronously after the reply, and the read path does only a single vector / graph lookup (< 50 ms).\n- **Update rather than append**: extracted results are compared with existing memories to decide ADD / UPDATE / DELETE / NOOP, so \"lives in Singapore\" and \"moved to London\" don't coexist, while historical versions are kept.\n- **User-viewable and deletable**: each memory links to its source conversation, and deletion is a hard delete synced to the vector index and caches.\n- **Isolated per user**: shard by user_id and enforce filtering at the query layer.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nUsing Sonnet-class for everything costs about $3.6 million a month; sending 70% of turns to a small model brings it to about $2.3 million, and only further trimming the history window and answer length gets close to $1.5 million; memory writes add an extra small-model call per turn, which must be counted in the cost.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why not stuff the entire history into a 1M context: you pay for it again every turn, latency is high, and it's unreliable with conflicting information.\n- Sensitive categories (health, religion, finances) are not remembered by default or require confirmation.\n- For evaluation, build your own \"write → ask a question several sessions later\" tests; benchmarks such as LoCoMo have been audited and found to have problems.","L6-T6A.2.8":"Case 7 is a domain model fine-tuning platform: multiple teams run SFT / DPO / RL on 8B–70B open-source models, 20–50 experiments a week, with only about 16 H100s; the data contains PII and can't leave the company, and the platform needs data versioning, experiment tracking, automatic evaluation, and one-click deployment.\n\n**How it works**\n- **LoRA by default**: cheap to train, and multiple LoRAs can be served concurrently on the same base; go full-parameter only when clearly underfitting.\n- **The data pipeline matters more than the training script**: exact + near-dup deduplication, PII redaction, quality filtering, source mixing ratios; immutable dataset versions and lineage so any model can be traced back to its data.\n- **Evaluation gates**: after training, automatically run held-out, general-capability regression (to guard against catastrophic forgetting), and safety sets; only models that pass enter the registry; eval sets are owned by the business teams.\n- **Quotas and preemptive scheduling**: per-team quotas + preemption, small experiments go through a single-GPU priority queue to avoid starvation, and checkpoints allow resumption.\n- **Restraint on RL support**: RL environments and reward engineering take ten times the work of SFT, so provide DPO / GRPO templates first and plug in custom rewards as plugins.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAn 8B LoRA SFT run (50 million tokens) takes about 1–2 hours on a single GPU; about 200 GPU-hours a week against a capacity of 2,688 GPU-hours means utilization < 10%, so the bottleneck is queueing and data preparation, not compute. Managed options like Tinker, where users write only the training loop, are a useful comparison for where to draw the build-it-yourself boundary.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When to fine-tune: stable behavior / format needs, cost and latency pressure, and thousands or more high-quality examples; use RAG for knowledge updates.\n- Keeping bad models out of production: evaluation gates + canary rollout + adapter rollback in seconds.\n- Automatically deduplicate the training set against eval sets to prevent leakage.","L6-T6A.2.9":"Case 8 is a multi-tenant LLM inference platform: a unified interface for 30 business tenants that proxies several external APIs and also self-hosts open-source models (including multi-LoRA); peak 500 QPS, interactive p95 TTFT < 1 s, with quotas, billing, priorities, and isolation.\n\n**How it works**\n- **LLM-aware routing**: ordinary load balancers don't understand the KV cache; llm-d's Endpoint Picker selects instances by prefix-cache affinity, queue depth, and policy, and maintains a cross-instance KV index with tiered HBM → CPU → SSD offloading; NVIDIA Dynamo takes a similar approach.\n- **P/D disaggregation**: prefill is compute-bound and decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, so when co-located, long prompts stall streaming output. Data from the vLLM guide: Qwen2.5-7B at 0.4 req/s has a co-located p99 ITL of 169 ms, versus 29 ms after disaggregation; the costs are KV transfer (requiring RDMA-class interconnect) and operational complexity, so disaggregate only when ITL tails miss targets.\n- **Multi-LoRA and tiered tenant isolation**: RPM / TPM quotas + priority queues + dedicated pools for key tenants, rather than separate instances per tenant.\n- **Billing that distinguishes cache hits**: otherwise tenants have no incentive to optimize their prompts.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA 70B-class model's KV cache is about 320 KiB / token, so an 8k request takes about 2.5 GB; an 80 GB GPU, after the weights, fits only a dozen or so long requests, so the prefix cache hit rate directly determines throughput. 500 QPS × 4 s = 2,000 concurrent streams, on the order of 100–200 H100s (heavily assumption-dependent).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Scale on queue depth and KV utilization rather than CPU; weight loading is slow, so scale predictively.\n- Reasoning models produce surging output, requiring separate pools, SLOs, and quotas.\n- The unified gateway is the single place for billing, quotas, auditing, and model switching.","L6-T6A.2.10":"Case 9 is an agent evaluation and observability platform: the company has 10+ agent applications and needs unified trace collection, offline evaluation, online monitoring, regression testing, and a cost dashboard, handling 5 million LLM spans a day and integrating with CI.\n\n**How it works**\n- **A data model following the OTel GenAI conventions**: span types include invoke_agent, execute_tool, chat, retrieval, etc., with attributes such as gen_ai.operation.name, the model, and token usage. These conventions are still in Development status as of mid-2026 and were split into a separate repository in 2026-06, hence \"align with OTel, keep extension fields, and version them\".\n- **Traces and evals share a structure**: a failed production trace becomes an eval case in one click, and eval results link back to traces, closing the loop.\n- **Layered graders**: deterministic checks (JSON schema, tool arguments, end-state assertions) come first, LLM judges handle open-ended quality and must be calibrated against humans, and human labels are used for calibration and disputed samples.\n- **Reproducible eval runs**: cache model responses, run in parallel, fix random seeds, record model versions; pass^k requires multiple runs.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n5 million spans at 10–20 kB each adds 50–100 GB a day; running judges on everything would cost about $25,000 / day, so failure signals are covered in full, the rest is stratified-sampled at 1–5%, and a random sample is kept as a baseline to guard against sampling bias.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Buy LangSmith / Langfuse / Braintrust or build: build only when data can't leave, cost at scale, or deep integration demands it, with OTel as the neutral interface.\n- For multi-step agents, focus on the end state, supplemented by trajectory metrics.\n- Judge drift is handled by versioning judges + regression against a gold set.","L6-T6A.2.11":"Case 10 is a text-to-SQL / BI agent: business colleagues query a 2,000-table data warehouse in natural language and get SQL, results, and interpretation; read-only, with row-level permissions, following the principle \"better to ask than to guess\"; 5,000 queries a day, p95 < 15 s.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Staged generation**: classify intent into a business domain (workspace) → select tables → prune columns → generate → validate → execute → self-repair (≤ 2 times) → interpret. Uber's QueryGPT experience is that LLMs are strong as focused classifiers, while stuffing a large schema straight into the model uses 40–60k tokens and makes hallucinated column names more likely.\n- **The semantic layer is the source of accuracy**: definitions such as \"active users\" and \"net revenue\" are stored in structured form and injected via RAG; without a semantic layer, start with a library of certified example SQL as few-shot examples.\n- **Pre-execution checks**: allow only SELECT, check table / column existence, rewrite for permissions or rely on database row-level security, and use EXPLAIN to estimate scan volume.\n- **Ask when ambiguous**: when table-selection scores are close or a metric has multiple meanings, ask one clarifying question.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nModel cost is about $0.05 per query, under $10,000 a month; the risk lies in warehouse queries: 5,000 × 5 GB = 25 TB / day, requiring scan caps and result caching. The most dangerous output is SQL that is syntactically correct but semantically wrong. QueryGPT cut SQL-writing time from about 10 minutes to 3.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Evaluate on execution accuracy (equivalent result sets), not identical SQL text.\n- Put row-level permissions in the database layer (RLS / views); SQL rewriting is only a supplement.\n- Why not fine-tune: schemas change fast and would get baked in, so retrieved examples + validation are more effective.","L6-T6A.2.12":"Case 11 is a real-time voice agent: a phone or in-app voice customer service / booking assistant requiring full duplex, barge-in support, tool calling, and human handoff, with end-to-end p50 < 800 ms, p95 < 1.5 s, and 2,000 concurrent calls.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Cascaded vs end-to-end speech models**: a cascaded STT → LLM → TTS pipeline has about 1.0–1.5 s latency, but every hop is swappable and separately evaluable, the intermediate text is auditable, and guardrails are easy to add; end-to-end is about 0.5–0.8 s and can pick up emotion, but text reasoning is weaker, observability is poor, and language coverage is narrow. The compromise is for the end-to-end model to handle \"listening and speaking\" while complex reasoning and tools go to a backend text agent. Scenarios that need auditing, such as device control, usually start with the cascaded approach.\n- **Latency budget breakdown**: VAD endpointing 200–300 ms + STT tail 100–200 + LLM first token 300–500 + TTS first audio 100–200 + network / telephony bridge 100–200 ≈ 0.9–1.4 s, with a metric for each item.\n- **Interruption handling**: stop TTS as soon as the user starts speaking, truncate generation, and write only **the part that was actually played** into the history, otherwise the model believes it finished speaking.\n- **Filler speech during tool calls**: first say \"let me check that for you\", and give a progress update if the tool takes more than 2 s.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCascaded costs about $0.06–0.08 per minute, so 480,000 call minutes a day is about $30,000–40,000 a day; voice is a token-intensive application and cost is the primary constraint; the bottlenecks are the latency budget and STT / TTS concurrency, not bandwidth.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why not go end-to-end from the start: observability, guardrails, and cost; build a cascaded baseline first.\n- Actions that change physical state or are irreversible need verbal restatement and confirmation.\n- Evaluate on WER (with keywords scored separately), task completion rate, erroneous action rate, and latency distribution.","L6-T6A.2.13":"Case 12 is a large-scale document extraction pipeline: in insurance / banking / logistics, 1 million pages of scans and PDFs a day are converted to structured fields per a given schema; field-level accuracy ≥ 98% (≥ 99.5% for key amounts), ingested the same day, with data not leaving the country.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Route by document type**: clean digital PDFs go through text-layer parsing (nearly free), scanned / handwritten / tabular pages go to a document VLM or OCR, and hard samples go to a stronger model or a human; the industry saying is that \"OCR is now a routing problem\".\n- **Schema-guided extraction with provenance**: a JSON schema constrains the output, and fields carry page numbers and bounding boxes; long contracts are extracted in segments and then merged, with completeness evaluated.\n- **Validation layer**: type and format checks, business rules (sum of line items = total), cross-document reconciliation, self-reported confidence + consistency comparison; send only low-confidence fields to humans, not whole pages.\n- **Queues + idempotency + dead-lettering**: the GPU pool scales with the backlog, priorities follow business value, and content hashes deduplicate.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA Haiku-class model costs about $0.004 per page ($4,000 a day), and self-hosted document VLMs (e.g. DeepSeek-OCR, about 97% accuracy at compression ratios < 10×) suit data that can't leave the country. But if 10% of pages need review at 2 minutes per page and $10 an hour, human review costs about $33,000 a day, so **reducing review volume is worth more than cutting model spend**.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Don't trust the model's self-reported confidence alone; calibrate with consistency, rules, and historical review data (e.g. isotonic regression).\n- Metrics: field-level F1 (with amounts and dates broken out), review rate and review edit rate, with error rate < 0.5% on high-confidence fields.\n- A 300-page contract: first do layout analysis to locate clauses, then extract in segments.","L6-T6A.2.14":"Case 13 is an LLM-enhanced recommender system, using a short-video + product feed with 50 million DAU and 100 million items as the example (all hypothetical), p99 < 200 ms. The core stance: nearly every company already has a retrieval → pre-ranking → ranking → re-ranking funnel, and **the LLM is an enhancement, not a rewrite from scratch**.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Do the math first to rule out online LLM ranking**: about 500 candidates × 200 tokens = 100,000 tokens per request, times 12,000 QPS is 1.2 billion tokens per second; at $0.1 per million tokens that's about $10 million a day, and latency also far exceeds the budget.\n- **LLMs for offline features**: generate tags, summaries, and knowledge offline on the item side (a one-time cost of about $4,500 for 100 million items), update user interest summaries nearline at low frequency, and read only a versioned feature store online. Huawei's KAR takes this form, with online A/B gains of 7% and 1.7%.\n- **Semantic-ID generative retrieval**: content embeddings go through multi-level residual quantization into codeword tuples, so similar items share prefixes and new items get an ID as soon as they're ingested; a transformer autoregressively generates the next item ID (TIGER, HSTU, OneRec, PLUM). The safe approach is to start it as one low-traffic retrieval channel.\n- **Training and deploying multimodal embeddings**: start from a pretrained image-text encoder, two stages of content pairs + behavior pairs, in-batch + hard negatives + logQ correction, using SigLIP or GradCache for a large effective batch; at deployment, compute the item side offline, distill, quantize, reduce dimensions with Matryoshka, and choose HNSW / IVF-PQ / DiskANN by scale.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIn ranking-stage target attention, Q is the candidate item and K = V is the user's historical behavior.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Offline AUC goes up but online metrics don't\": check for feature leakage, training-serving skew, exposure bias, and metric misalignment.\n- Training multimodal embeddings on 8 GPUs: freeze the backbone + LoRA + cache visual features.","L6-T6A.2.15":"Case 14 is a real-time trending leaderboard: a backend question often mixed into AI Engineer interviews. Requirements: the Top 50 ranked by popularity over the last hour, peak writes of 1 million events / second, 500,000 read QPS, 10 million candidates (all hypothetical), read p99 < 20 ms, and 10–60 s of ranking delay is acceptable.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Redis ZSET**: member is item_id and score is popularity; ZINCRBY is O(log N) and reading the Top-K is O(log N + K), implemented with a skip list. MySQL ORDER BY suffers write amplification from row-lock contention on hot rows and index maintenance.\n- **Local aggregation, then batched ZINCRBY**: writing directly to the same key creates a hot key; with 100 consumer instances each aggregating for 1 s, total writes drop to about 200,000 / second, then keys are split across 16 shards and sent via pipeline. Kafka partitions by item_id, and offsets are committed after the Redis write succeeds, guaranteeing at-least-once.\n- **Merging sharded Top-K**: when each item lands in exactly one shard, the global Top-K must be within the union of the per-shard Top-Ks, so merging is exact; super-hot items split into sub-members require fetching extra or an exact recheck.\n- **Bucketed sliding window**: one ZSET per minute, merge the last 60 buckets with ZUNIONSTORE, and EXPIRE old buckets.\n- **Approximate ranking**: Count-Min Sketch + a min-heap or Redis TOPK (HeavyKeeper), saving memory at the cost of counting error.\n- **Redis / ClickHouse division of labor**: Redis stores only rebuildable window state, while Kafka + ClickHouse are the source of truth, used for historical rankings, auditing, and rebuilds.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe leaderboard is a degradable feature and must never bring down the home page.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Anti-manipulation: dedupe and rate-limit before aggregation, weight by behavior type, manual delisting.\n- Reconcile by measuring Top-K overlap and Kendall tau against the exact ClickHouse ranking.","L6-T6A.2.16":"The time-decayed score is one of the two time-window approaches for a trending leaderboard: exponential decay makes old events' contributions fall smoothly over time, and a single ZSET is enough to maintain a \"newer is hotter\" ranking.\n\n**How it works**\n- Definition: score = Σ w · exp(−λ(t_now − t_event)), where w is the behavior weight (share > comment > like > view) and λ controls the decay speed.\n- Implementing the definition directly would require constantly rewriting all old scores, which is too expensive. The trick is to **leave old scores alone**: a new event adds w · exp(λ(t − t0)), where t0 is a reference time. The increment for new events grows exponentially over time, which is equivalent to old scores decaying relatively, and the ranking matches the definition.\n- Because scores grow overall with time, you must **periodically reset the reference t0** (rescaling all scores) to prevent numeric overflow.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Pros: just one ZSET, each write is a single ZINCRBY, and the ranking is smooth with no sudden jumps at window boundaries.\n- Cons: it can't answer \"strictly the last hour\", and it's less explainable than bucketing.\n- Compared with bucketed sliding windows (one ZSET per minute, merging 60 buckets with ZUNIONSTORE): the latter is exact and replayable, but uses memory of about number of buckets × active items. Choose bucketing if the product needs explainability, decay if it needs smoothness, or combine them (buckets + decreasing weights across buckets).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to derive that \"adding the amplified increment ≡ decaying old scores\", because only relative magnitudes are compared.\n- Remember to mention overflow and resetting t0.\n- It's related to time-penalized ranking like Hacker News, but here in an incrementally maintainable form.","L6-T6A.2.17":"Multi-level read-side caching solves the leaderboard problem of \"500,000 read QPS, everyone sees the same content, and seconds of staleness are tolerable\": instead of letting every home page refresh hit Redis, snapshot the leaderboard and cache it layer by layer.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Immutable snapshots + atomic pointer swap**: each round, the merger merges the per-shard Top-Ks and writes an immutable hot:top50:v{n}, then atomically updates a pointer key to the new version. Readers always see a complete version, never a half-merged one; if the merger dies, the read side keeps serving the previous snapshot and raises an alert.\n- **API local cache**: within each application instance, a local cache like Caffeine with a 1–5 s TTL means each instance hits the origin at most once per second, so origin QPS is roughly the instance count (a few hundred). Add **random jitter** to the TTL to keep many instances from expiring at once (avalanche); use **single-flight** so concurrent misses within an instance trigger only one origin fetch (preventing breakdown). The merger can also proactively push the new version number.\n- **CDN / client caching for a few more seconds** shaves peaks further.\n- Personalized parts (e.g. \"what people I follow are watching\") are split into separate requests so they don't pollute the shared cache.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe cost is seconds of staleness, but the leaderboard already allows 10–60 s of ranking delay, so trading staleness for extremely high read throughput and a stable p99 is well worth it. If Redis fails entirely, you can still return the local cache or a static JSON on the CDN.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Distinguish the countermeasures for breakdown (single-flight) and avalanche (TTL jitter).\n- Immutable snapshots + pointer swapping are the key to read/write consistency.","L6-T6A.3.1":"Whiteboard pacing and diagram conventions are the time-management and visual-communication norms of system design interviews, aimed at covering the key dimensions in 45 minutes and letting the interviewer see at a glance that you've built production systems.\n\n**How it works**\n- **45-minute pacing**: 0–5 min clarification and metrics (written in the top-left corner of the whiteboard and referred back to repeatedly); 5–12 min high-level architecture, drawing only 6–8 boxes; 12–30 min go deep on 2–3 components based on the interviewer's interests (data, agent, and evaluation are most common); 30–38 min failure modes, safety, cost; 38–45 min iteration roadmap and summary.\n- **Diagram conventions**: solid lines for synchronous requests, dashed lines for async / events; offline pipeline and online path separated left and right; storage drawn as cylinders; next to each LLM call box, note \"model tier + approximate tokens\"; mark human touchpoints clearly.\n- **Proactively cover** cost and at least one failure mode without waiting to be asked.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nToo many boxes and you get bogged down in detail; too few and it looks hollow; 6–8 boxes are enough to convey the skeleton, then drill down by interest. Annotating model tiers and tokens anchors the cost discussion. Losing control of time (spending 10 minutes on chunk size) is one of the ten common ways to lose points.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the metrics on the whiteboard and tie every later decision back to them.\n- When going deep, ask \"would you rather see data, agent, or evaluation?\"\n- When practicing, record and play back to check pacing and the \"because – so – cost\" phrasing.","L6-T6A.3.2":"The ten common ways to lose points are a list of the behaviors most likely to get you a downgraded rating in an AI system design interview; use it as a self-check list.\n\n**How it works: the list**\n1. Designing without asking about scale and latency, or asking but not reflecting it (saying p95 < 2 s but drawing 5 serial model calls).\n2. Stuffing all logic into the LLM: permission checks, monetary limits, and SQL read-only checks should all be deterministic code.\n3. No evaluation loop: can't say where the eval set comes from, what the grader is, or how online metrics flow back.\n4. Multi-agent for its own sake, unable to say why it can't be one agent + a few tools.\n5. Ignoring cost: can't say what one interaction costs, or whether the bottleneck is the model or data / humans.\n6. Ignoring failure modes and degradation: what happens when the API goes down, a tool times out, or you need to roll back.\n7. On safety, only saying \"add a guardrail\", without being able to explain specific prompt injection paths and isolation methods.\n8. Piling up buzzwords without trade-offs.\n9. Repeating yourself instead of responding to follow-ups.\n10. Losing control of time.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nItems 2, 3, and 5 best distinguish candidates with research backgrounds from those with engineering backgrounds: strong on model depth, but often pushed down to L4 on evaluation, cost, and safety. The three framework-level failures (skipping clarification, making everything an agent, talking only about the happy path) map onto this list.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"why not just use an if statement here\", be able to concede and simplify.\n- Phrasing for follow-ups: \"I assumed X earlier; if that doesn't hold, I'd change to Y\".\n- On safety, name the injection sources (documents, web pages, tool results) and the isolation measures.","L6-T6A.3.3":"Interviewer expectations by level describe how the same system design question is judged differently at L4 / L5 / L6. Most companies' scorecards have four dimensions: problem understanding and clarification, architecture and trade-offs, depth (data / model / evaluation / safety / cost), and communication and handling follow-ups.\n\n**How it works: expectations by level**\n- **Mid (L4)**: can draw a reasonable end-to-end architecture, knows the basic RAG / agent components, and can answer direct follow-ups. Typical gaps: no metrics or estimates, unclear trade-offs.\n- **Senior (L5)**: proactively clarifies and quantifies metrics; every decision has a trade-off; provides an evaluation loop and failure modes; can compute cost and capacity; can course-correct under follow-ups. Typical gaps: shallow understanding of the platform layer (multi-tenancy, scheduling, compliance) and a generic iteration roadmap.\n- **Staff (L6)**: identifies and ranks the real bottlenecks and risks; proposes a phased roadmap and organizational considerations (who owns the eval set, how to drive semantic-layer adoption); has judgment about the latest practices (P/D disaggregation, the status of the OTel conventions, problems with memory benchmarks) rather than reciting them.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCandidates with research backgrounds are usually strong on model depth but get pushed down to L4 on the evaluation loop, cost, platform, and safety. The last five sections of the case template are designed precisely to fill in the L5 dimensions, with the goal of getting the interviewer to write \"has production sense\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Aiming for L5: proactively give cost numbers and an evaluation loop in every case.\n- Aiming for L6: rank risks, give a phased roadmap, and discuss organizational and ownership issues.\n- Bring judgment to new developments, e.g. when you should not do P/D disaggregation.","L6-T6A.3.4":"Breaking down combined questions is the method for multi-case hybrids like \"a customer service agent that can search the knowledge base, place orders, and also make phone calls\": first find the **backbone case** that determines the architectural skeleton, then attach the other cases to it as subsystems.\n\n**How it works**\n- Identify the backbone: usually **an agent with write actions**, because it determines the heaviest parts of the skeleton: the policy engine, confirmation, idempotency, auditing, and human handoff.\n- Attach the remaining cases as subsystems, for example:\n  - \"Customer service + knowledge base\" = Case 2's skeleton + Case 1 as a permission-aware RAG tool;\n  - \"Deep research + data analysis\" = Case 4's orchestration + Case 5's sandboxed analysis nodes;\n  - \"Document extraction + knowledge base\" = Case 12's pipeline as Case 1's ingestion.\n- During clarification, proactively ask \"which part is the focus today\", and spend your time on the subsystem the interviewer cares about.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nReal interview questions are rarely a single case. Laying out all requirements flat easily produces a big diagram with no focus, and there isn't enough time. Centering on a backbone lets you reuse case structures you've prepared, keeps interfaces clean (subsystems usually plug in as tools or pipelines), and naturally leads into shared cross-subsystem components (auth, tracing, cost).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Start with \"I'll treat X as the backbone, with Y and Z as tools / upstream pipelines it calls\".\n- Subsystems' permission and safety boundaries should reuse the backbone's policy engine.\n- When a voice channel is added, layer on Case 11's latency budget and interruption handling.","L6-T6A.3.5":"Changing the problem is a common move in system design interviews: after you present a design, the interviewer changes one constraint to see whether you can pinpoint the parts of the architecture that must change with it, rather than tearing everything down or staying put.\n\n**How it works: common changes and responses**\n- **Scale grows 10×**: single-node indexes become sharded; sync becomes queue + async; caching and model tiering go from optimizations to necessities; recompute costs.\n- **Latency target halved**: cut serial model calls (merge steps, parallelize tools), small-model routing, streaming, prefetching and caching; for voice, consider end-to-end models.\n- **Third-party APIs banned**: self-hosting + an inference platform (Case 8); small models need stricter evaluation; fine-tuning enters the roadmap (Case 7).\n- **Write actions / money movement added**: upgrade the policy engine, confirmation and idempotency, auditing, human handoff, end-state evaluation, and canary rollout across the board.\n- **Compliance added**: redaction at collection, regional endpoints, log retention, right to erasure, access auditing; eval sets must be redacted too.\n- **Multilingual**: language detection and routing, multilingual embeddings / rerank, per-language eval sets, STT / TTS language coverage.\n- **Budget halved**: measure first, then tier; caching, Batch, fewer steps, capped output, result caching, and dropping low-value features.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nChanging the problem tests whether you \"understand why each component exists\": being able to say which parts change with the constraint shows your decisions were genuinely grounded.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Respond with \"the original assumption was X, now it's Y, so I change Z, at the cost of W\".\n- When the budget is halved, talk about measuring first rather than jumping straight to a smaller model.","L6-T6A.3.6":"The metrics cheat sheet lists quality, system, and safety / business metrics by system type, helping you quickly give quantifiable targets in the \"metrics and SLOs\" and \"evaluation\" steps of the interview.\n\n**How it works: by system type**\n- **Knowledge base Q&A**: recall@k, faithfulness, citation precision, correct-refusal rate; TTFT, p95, indexing latency; **permission leak rate = 0**, adoption rate.\n- **Action-taking agents**: end-state success rate, pass^k, tool error rate; per-turn latency, step count; policy-violating actions = 0, handoff rate, CSAT, cost per session.\n- **Multi-agent research**: rubric score, citation accuracy, completeness; task duration, parallelism; cost per task, source quality.\n- **Inference platforms**: TTFT / ITL p50–p99, throughput per GPU, cache hit rate, queueing; tenant quota violations, cost per million tokens.\n- **Extraction pipelines**: field-level F1, completeness; throughput, backlog, SLA attainment; review rate, review edit rate, total cost per page.\n- **Voice agents**: task completion rate, keyword WER; end-to-end latency breakdown, interruption response; handoff rate, cost per minute, number of restatements.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBeing able to quantify \"good\" is a core L5 expectation. Distinguish offline from online metrics, and hard safety metrics that \"must be 0\" from business metrics that can be optimized. pass^k reflects the reliability of action-taking agents better than pass@k, because user experience depends on consistency.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- For each type of system, name at least one quality, one system, and one safety metric.\n- Emphasize hard constraints (leaks = 0, violations = 0) separately.","L6-T6A.3.7":"Cross-case reliability patterns are a set of engineering recipes that recur throughout the 14 cases; in interviews, use them as a \"vocabulary\" to quickly assemble a fault-tolerant design for any question.\n\n**How it works: the patterns**\n- **Timeouts + retry budgets + circuit breakers**: every external model / tool call has a timeout and a retry cap, and when the failure rate exceeds a threshold, the circuit breaks and degrades (all cases).\n- **Degradation modes**: still serving when the main path fails: customer service falls back to FAQs, the platform switches to a backup provider, voice hands off to a human (Cases 2, 8, 11).\n- **Idempotency keys**: write operations carry an idempotency key, duplicate requests return the first result, and the key is built from user + action + parameters (Cases 2, 5, 12).\n- **Checkpoint / resume**: long tasks recover from the latest snapshot rather than rerunning (Cases 3, 4, 5, 12).\n- **Queues + dead-lettering + priorities**: admission control rejects fast, and backlogs are ordered by business value (Cases 8, 12).\n- **Budget controllers**: three caps on steps, tokens, and searches (Cases 2, 3, 4).\n- **Shadow mode / canary / rainbow deployments**: old and new versions run in parallel, rollout proceeds by tenant, and long tasks aren't interrupted (all cases).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAI systems add nondeterminism and expensive individual calls, so traditional reliability patterns matter even more: retries amplify cost, so you need budgets; agents can loop, so you need step caps. Talking only about the happy path is a common way to lose points.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- For every external dependency you draw, add a line on its timeout and degradation strategy.\n- Explain clearly how the idempotency key is built and the \"return the first result\" semantics.","L6-T6A.3.8":"Cross-case safety patterns summarize the threats to agent systems and their corresponding mechanisms; the core principle is that **safety boundaries live in the data layer and deterministic code, not in the prompt**; the policy engine is the implementation backbone for action-taking agents.\n\n**How it works: threats and mechanisms**\n- Unauthorized reads → ACL pre-filter, RLS, tenant sharding: the boundary is in the data layer.\n- Unauthorized writes / financial loss → policy engine, three permission tiers, confirmation, humans: **the agent can only request, never approve**.\n- Prompt injection (documents, web pages, tool results) → content isolation markers, read-only tools, output validation, sandboxes without network: treat external content as data, not instructions.\n- Identity spoofing → identity comes from the auth system and session context; self-claims in the conversation are invalid.\n- PII leaks → redaction at collection, encryption, access auditing, retention periods: observability data is customer data too.\n- Supply chain (models, tools, MCP servers) → pinned versions, provenance audits, least privilege; never use latest.\n\n**The policy engine's four decision levels**: ALLOW, CONFIRM, HANDOFF, DENY. Unverified identity is denied outright; refunds ≤ 50 with low risk are allowed, ≤ 500 need confirmation, and higher amounts are handed off; read-only tools are allowed by default, and other write operations require confirmation by default. Every decision writes an audit record.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLLMs can be manipulated, so no decision involving permissions or money can be left to the model; the cost is maintaining rules, in exchange for auditable, provable safety.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to state the four decision levels, how the idempotency key is built (user + action + parameters), and the audit fields.\n- \"Add a guardrail\" isn't enough; explain the injection paths and isolation measures.","L6-T6A.3.9":"\"The bottleneck is often not model spend\" is a lesson that runs through all 14 cases: once you've done the cost estimate, what really constrains the system is usually data engineering, infrastructure, or human steps, not the token bill.\n\n**How it works: the real bottleneck in each case**\n- **Enterprise knowledge base**: about $0.017 per Q&A, and a full embedding recomputation at about $160 is negligible; the bottleneck is the engineering work of connector sync and permission sync.\n- **Coding agent**: sandboxes run about 250 container-hours a day, which is cheap; the bottleneck is sandbox startup and repository checkout, where cloning a large monorepo can take over a minute and requires warm snapshots.\n- **Data analysis agent**: the model costs about $4,000 a month, while the real cost is sandbox compute and warehouse scan volume, so each SQL query needs a scan limit; text-to-SQL is the same, where 25 TB of scans a day can run to hundreds of dollars daily.\n- **Document extraction**: the model costs about $4,000 / day versus about $33,000 / day for human review, so better confidence calibration to reduce review volume is worth more.\n- Fine-tuning platform: GPU utilization < 10%, with the bottleneck in queueing and data preparation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nInterviewers want to hear you \"name the bottleneck after doing the math\". Focusing only on switching to a cheaper model often optimizes less than a tenth of the cost; pointing out the real bottleneck shows production experience and determines engineering priorities.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Three steps to do the math: model cost → non-model cost → name the biggest item.\n- A common follow-up is \"how would you cut cost in half\"; address the biggest item first, not the model's unit price.","L6-T6A.3.10":"Hidden cost items are cost sources that are easy to miss when estimating but become substantial at scale; they need to be listed separately on the bill.\n\n**How it works: typical hidden costs**\n- **Memory writes**: an assistant with long-term memory makes an additional async small-model \"extract + update\" call after every reply, to decide ADD / UPDATE / DELETE / NOOP. It's not on the user-visible path, but it occurs as often as conversation turns and must be counted.\n- **Voice is token-intensive**: a real-time voice agent makes multiple calls per minute; a cascaded pipeline costs about $0.06–0.08 / minute, and industry estimates for end-to-end speech models are about $0.1–0.3 / minute; at 480,000 call minutes / day, cascaded alone is about $30,000–40,000 / day, so cost is the primary constraint.\n- Other items of the same kind: fixed tool-schema overhead for multi-tool agents (the computer / browser toolset is about 4.5k–6.6k tokens), about 30% more tokens for the same text with the new tokenizer, per-call billing for web search, LLM judge fees on the evaluation platform, and session-hour fees for managed agent runtimes.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nMissing these items can make an estimate an order of magnitude too low, and the budget spirals after launch. Only once identified can they be targeted: use a small model for memory extraction and trigger it only when necessary; use mini models or caching for voice and control answer length; run judges on samples.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When estimating, list \"user-visible calls\" and \"background calls\" separately.\n- For voice scenarios, state up front that cost is the primary constraint.","L6-T6A.3.11":"These are the 2025–2026 changes you can cite in interviews that can alter design judgments, with the focus on \"how to talk about them in an interview\".\n\n**How it works: main developments**\n- **P/D disaggregation practice guide (vLLM, 2026-09-29)**: gives criteria and data for when to disaggregate (p99 ITL 169 ms → 29 ms), lists KV transfer connectors such as NIXL, LMCache, and Mooncake, and gives the counterexample \"stay co-located when TTFT is the bottleneck or the interconnect is slow\".\n- **OTel GenAI repo move (2026-06-12, semantic-conventions v1.42.0)**: the gen_ai.* conventions moved to a separate repository, covering invoke_agent, execute_tool, and retrieval, but all are still in Development status.\n- **MCP stateless core (2026-07-28 spec)**: removes the initialize handshake and Mcp-Session-Id; each request carries its own protocol version and capabilities, and new Mcp-Method / Mcp-Name headers ease gateway routing, letting MCP servers scale horizontally behind stateless load balancers.\n- **Agents Rule of Two (Meta, 2025-10-31)**: a session may combine at most two of the three properties \"processes untrusted input, accesses sensitive data, changes state or communicates externally\"; if all three are needed, a human must be in the loop.\n- **Managed agent runtimes**: Anthropic Managed Agents splits an agent into brain, hands, and session, with a stateless harness that can recover from the event log and credentials kept out of the sandbox; the OpenAI Agents API is in public beta, and the Assistants API has been shut down.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nL6 expects judgment about new practices rather than recitation, e.g. saying when you should not do P/D disaggregation, or why the OTel conventions should be versioned.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The Rule of Two is the one-line, architecture-level defense against prompt injection.\n- When citing new developments, include the date and status.","L6-T6A.3.12":"The hands-on system design exercises are six small projects that put the cases into code; assuming no large local GPU, they can be done with a cloud API, MLX, or a 7–8B Ollama model, and each must produce a trio of \"design doc + runnable code + evaluation numbers\".\n\n**How it works: the exercises**\n- **Cost and capacity estimator (half a day)**: extend cost_estimator.py to read a scenario YAML and output monthly cost, peak concurrency, and instance count, and write YAMLs for each case; acceptance is matching the orders of magnitude for Cases 1, 2, and 4, and being able to answer how much a cache hit rate going from 0 to 70% saves.\n- **Permission-aware mini knowledge base (1–2 days)**: 200 documents across three departments, chunks carrying ACLs, pre-filter + hybrid BM25 + cited generation; 60 questions including 10 cross-permission traps and 10 that should be refused; acceptance is a leak rate of 0 and a correct-refusal rate ≥ 80%.\n- **τ-bench-style customer service evaluation (1–2 days)**: a SQLite order database, 5 tools and a policy engine, an LLM playing the user, 20 tasks × 4 runs, computing pass@1 and pass^4 and checking the database end state.\n- **Single vs multi-agent research comparison (1 day)**: lead + 3 subagents vs a single agent on 10 questions, compared with a rubric judge, reaching a conclusion under an equal token budget.\n- Plus a minimal observability loop and a paper experiment on P/D disaggregation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIn interviews, numbers you actually produced yourself are far more convincing than memorized conclusions, especially the gap between pass@1 and pass^4 and how you verified a leak rate of 0.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to state the acceptance metric for each exercise.\n- Use the exercise results to answer \"when is multi-agent better\".","L6-T6A.4.1":"This is a classic deep-dive question: a customer service agent has a 70% resolution rate a month after launch, and the boss wants 85%; what do you do? The core idea is **attribute failures first, then treat the cause**, rather than jumping straight to a bigger model.\n\n**How it works**\n- Classify unresolved sessions into four causes: **knowledge gaps** (the knowledge base has no answer), **misunderstanding**, **stuck workflows** (tool failures, permission denials), and **policy limits** (should have gone to a human anyway).\n- The fixes differ: fill knowledge gaps in the knowledge base and improve retrieval; for misunderstanding, add evals and prompt fixes, switching models if necessary; for workflow problems, fix tools and policy thresholds; for policy limits, negotiate with the business on what can be loosened.\n- Quantify: manually attribute about 200 unresolved sessions to get each category's share (e.g. knowledge 40% / understanding 20% / workflow 25% / policy 15%), tackle the largest fixable category first, and estimate the ceiling on gains before scheduling.\n- Watch for reliability problems where sessions \"should have been resolved but failed\", measured with pass^k.\n- A/B test every change, comparing within intent strata, and check that handoff rate and policy-violating actions don't get worse.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSwitching straight to the strongest model may improve misunderstanding cases, but knowledge and workflow problems won't change, and cost will double. Attribution makes investment predictable and shows data-driven engineering judgment.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Definition of resolution rate: the user doesn't contact again + no human intervention, calibrated with sampled human review.\n- Proving the gain isn't a traffic shift: concurrent randomized A/B + intent stratification, so a change in the share of easy questions doesn't create a fake improvement.","L6-T6A.4.2":"This deep-dive question asks: a document extraction system processing 1 million pages a day suddenly gets a backlog of 5 million pages; what do you do? It tests the priority, elasticity, and degradation design of a batch system under bursty load.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Prioritize by business value**: e.g. large claim amounts and near due dates go first, with external notifications tiered by SLA.\n- **Scale elastically with APIs as backstop**: expand the self-hosted GPU pool; where compliance allows, switch some hard samples to an API.\n- **Degrade non-critical fields and backfill asynchronously**: extract key fields first and ingest them, then fill in the remaining fields asynchronously.\n- **Idempotency and resumable processing**: the system itself supports idempotency and resuming from checkpoints, so newly added instances pick up directly from the queue without duplicating work or losing progress.\n- **Capacity planning afterwards**: use this backlog's data to set a baseline + elastic ratio, and backlog alert thresholds.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBacklogs are a routine risk for batch pipelines, and queues + idempotency + dead-lettering + priorities are the standard reliability patterns. Scaling isn't instantaneous: loading self-hosted model weights takes minutes, so you need baseline instances and pre-warmed images. Human review can also back up; you can then temporarily raise the confidence threshold, review only the most critical fields, and record this for later spot checks, trading some quality risk for timeliness.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Stop the bleeding in the short term first (priorities, scaling, degradation), then plan for the medium term.\n- When asked \"how long does scaling take\", mention weight loading and pre-warming.\n- Temporary strategies for a human review backlog and the follow-up spot checks.","L6-T6A.4.3":"This deep-dive question asks: if you can self-host only one 8B model, how do you build usable enterprise Q&A? The idea is to acknowledge the small model's weaknesses (long-context reasoning, complex instructions) and **move the difficulty from the model into retrieval and engineering**.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Precise retrieval, small and focused context**: hybrid retrieval + rerank, providing only 3–5 highly relevant chunks so the small model doesn't get lost in a long context.\n- **Short, structured prompts with examples**.\n- **LoRA fine-tune format and refusals**: fine-tune on a few thousand examples for answer format, citation habits, and refusal behavior, so the small model is stable on these behaviors.\n- **Stricter output validation**: check that citations exist and use NLI for consistency checks.\n- **Cascade hard questions to an API**: if compliance allows, escalate the few hard questions to a large API model based on confidence.\n- Evaluation focuses on correct-refusal rate and hallucination rate, not just the average score.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis is a textbook application of \"RAG for knowledge, fine-tuning for behavior\", and a realistic solution where data residency or cost is constrained. An 8B model on a single GPU can serve dozens of concurrent requests with TTFT in the hundreds of milliseconds, so latency is more controllable than with an API; the cost is a lower capability ceiling, requiring more engineering investment and stricter evaluation. Fine-tuning can damage general capabilities, so mix in general data and gate on a regression set.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One line: move the difficulty into retrieval and engineering.\n- Cascading needs a confidence signal; state the compliance precondition.","L6-T6A.4.4":"A meeting transcription system is a real interview question: design the complete flow for streaming transcription, speaker identification, and incremental summarization. The key is splitting real-time responsiveness and final quality into two paths.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Data flow**: the client streams audio in 100–300 ms chunks → streaming STT outputs \"interim results + stable results\" → diarization (speaker separation) labels each segment → text flows into incremental summarization.\n- **Two paths**: the real-time path uses streaming STT's stable results and a lightweight speaker scheme, allowing later corrections; the post-meeting path uses a large offline model for refined transcription and full diarization, producing the final transcript.\n- **Incremental summaries**: rather than triggering per sentence, trigger by time window (e.g. every 1–2 minutes) or topic boundary, rolling forward with \"previous summary + new transcript\" to control tokens; cite only stabilized text so later corrections don't overturn it.\n- Failure modes: accents and proper nouns (hotword lists), overlapping speech confusing speakers, network jitter (client buffering and resumable uploads).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA live transcript + a refined post-meeting transcript is an \"eventually consistent\" approach, trading the cost of processing twice for a low-latency experience and high final quality. Per-minute STT pricing is the biggest cost, window-triggered summaries keep cost under control, and the value of each path should be argued separately.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Evaluation: WER (with keywords scored separately), DER (diarization error rate), summary factual consistency and action-item recall, and user edit volume online.\n- Privacy compliance: recording notice, encrypted storage, retention periods, right to erasure.","L6-T6A.4.5":"Designing a Prompt Playground is a question from Anthropic and OpenAI interview reports: design a tool to debug prompts and simulate full multi-turn conversations (threads); the model service can be abstracted away, and the focus is on the data model, the API, and the product loop.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Three core object types**: Prompt (a versioned template + variables); Run (a given version + variable values + model and parameters → output, tokens, latency); Thread (a multi-turn message sequence that can fork).\n- **Forked conversations as a message tree**: messages carry a parent_id rather than being stored as an array; editing a turn forks from that point, and the current branch is one path through the tree.\n- **API**: create / update prompt versions, start streaming runs (SSE), run multiple models or versions side by side on the same input, save / share / export.\n- **Backend**: quotas and rate limits (to avoid being used as a free model proxy), multi-tenant isolation, sensitive content detection.\n- **Evaluation entry point**: any run can be saved as an eval case in one click, the interface between the playground and the production evaluation loop.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe frontend challenges are streaming rendering, side-by-side comparison, and long editable histories. Reproducibility requires runs to record the model version, all parameters, and the final assembled prompt, otherwise they can't be replayed. Abuse prevention relies on per-user / per-org token quotas, concurrency caps, and limits on overly long inputs, with cost visible in real time.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"How do you store forks?\" A tree structure + parent_id.\n- \"How do you reproduce a run?\" Record the model version, parameters, and final prompt.\n- Emphasize the value of connecting to the evaluation platform.","L6-T6B.1.1":"By 2026 the industry's consensus definition of an agent is plain: agent = model + harness. The harness is the layer of software around the model that decides what the model sees, what it can do, how its work is verified, how it persists midway, and how users extend it. Claude Code's official docs describe it as an *agentic harness*.\n\n**How it works**\nA July 2026 study of 11 open-source coding agents (about 4 million lines of source) grouped the harness into 7 subsystems and identified 29 recurring design patterns:\n- Main loop: when to call the model and when to stop, usually a hand-written loop driven by `stop_reason`;\n- Tool layer: file read/write, search, shell, web, LSP, subagents;\n- Context management: layering, on-demand loading, compaction, reset;\n- Permissions and safety: allowlists, sandboxes, classifiers;\n- Verification: tests, type checking, independent evaluators;\n- Persistence: event logs, checkpoints, progress files;\n- Extensions: CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md, skills, hooks, MCP, plugins.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe most common way to lose points in interviews is conflating product capability with model capability. The way to tell them apart is controlling variables: the gap from swapping harnesses with the same model is the harness's contribution, and the gap from swapping models in the same harness is the model's contribution. The same study had a counterintuitive finding: none of the 11 agents used vector embeddings for code retrieval, and none adopted a general-purpose framework like LangGraph; skills were adopted by 9/11 and MCP by 8/11.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to recite the seven subsystems and use them to break down any agent product item by item.\n- Every harness component is an assumption about the model's limitations and will expire as models improve (e.g. Anthropic dropped sprint decomposition on Opus 4.6).\n- Follow-up \"which components won't expire\": trust-related ones such as permissions, sandboxes, and auditing.","L6-T6B.1.2":"Claude Code's main loop is the skeleton of the whole harness: the official description is three alternating phases, gather context → take action → verify results, until the task is done. The loop itself is minimal; the product's complexity lies outside it.\n\n**How it works**\n- Each iteration sends the system prompt, CLAUDE.md, the conversation, and tool definitions to the model; when it returns `tool_use`, the harness first runs a permission check, then executes the tool, feeds the result back as `tool_result`, and calls the model again; when it returns plain text, control goes back to the user.\n- The stopping condition is driven by the model rather than hard-coded in the harness; the harness only sets max_turns and a budget as safeguards.\n- Users can press `Esc` to interrupt, or \"queue\" a corrective message while a tool is running: the new message doesn't interrupt the running tool, but is inserted into the same turn after the tool returns, so the model sees it when making its next decision.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis is exactly the minimal agent loop, which shows that a mature product's competitiveness lies not in the loop itself but in peripheral components such as context management, subagents, hooks, and permission modes. Message queueing is \"single-threaded + insertable\" rather than multi-threaded, a very low-cost human-in-the-loop design.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why leave the stopping condition to the model? Only the model knows where the task boundary is.\n- Interrupting vs queueing: interrupting cancels the current tool; queueing doesn't cancel it and only affects the next decision.\n- When drawing the diagram, remember to put the permission check before tool execution.","L6-T6B.1.3":"Claude Code's search tools are deterministic: rather than maintaining a vector index, it has the model locate code the way a senior engineer would, using glob, grep (regex content search), and file reads; this approach is called agentic search, with LSP as a \"precise but requires configuration\" complement.\n\n**How it works**\nBuilt-in tools fall into five categories: file operations (read / edit / create), search (glob, regex), execution (shell, running tests, git), web (search and fetch), and code intelligence (LSP: type errors after edits, go to definition, find references; requires installing the code intelligence plugin for the language). There are also orchestration tools such as spawning subagents and asking the user questions. The model iterates over multiple turns: glob to find files, grep to find symbols, then read files and follow imports and call chains.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThree reasons not to build an index: code queries are mostly exact (symbol names, error messages, file names), where grep has higher precision; an index is stateful infrastructure that branch switches, uncommitted changes, and permissions can all make inconsistent, whereas agentic search always reads the ground truth on disk; and multi-turn search is itself reasoning. The cost is latency and tokens from multiple round trips, and in very large repositories it may fail to find code whose naming differs from the question's wording. LSP provides compiler-grade truth and serves as both a retrieval tool and a verification tool.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Don't say \"vector retrieval is useless\": Cursor's hybrid retrieval averages 12.5% better than pure grep; the right answer is routing by query type.\n- Follow-up \"what if agentic search can't find it\": enumerate synonyms first, read the directory structure, or add embeddings as a fallback for semantic queries.","L6-T6B.1.4":"The context window is Claude Code's scarcest resource. It controls standing costs with layered, on-demand loading, handles a nearly full window with compaction, and keeps information that must not be lost outside the window.\n\n**How it works**\n- Layered loading: CLAUDE.md / `.claude/rules/` load in full at session start and occupy space on every request (rules can load on demand via `paths`); skills load only their descriptions at first and the full content when used; MCP tools load only their names at first, with schemas loaded on demand; subagents use separate windows; hooks don't enter the context by default. Auto memory loads the first 200 lines or 25KB of `MEMORY.md`.\n- Compaction: near the limit, older tool outputs are cleared first, then the conversation is summarized, keeping user requests and key code snippets; detailed early instructions may be lost. You can write \"Compact Instructions\" in CLAUDE.md or use `/compact focus on ...` to set the focus; when a single output is so large that the window fills again right after compaction, it reports a thrashing error after a few attempts rather than looping forever.\n- Persistence layer: CLAUDE.md, auto memory, JSONL session logs under `~/.claude/projects/` (supporting resume / fork / rewind), and file checkpoints taken before edits. There's also an explicit todo list that keeps the goal closest to the model's attention, countering forgetting.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCompaction is essentially a lossy rewrite of the context, and summaries accumulate distortion; long tasks are therefore better served by a progress file + context reset.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When discussing compaction, always cover \"the persistence layers beyond compaction\", or you've only told half the story.\n- If the model forgets constraints after compaction: move hard constraints into CLAUDE.md or a hook rather than relying on the conversation to retain them.","L6-T6B.1.5":"Claude Code offers three ways to spin off work in exchange for context isolation and parallelism: subagents, forks, and dynamic workflows.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Subagent**: runs its own loop in a separate context and returns only a summary to the main session. What it sees is its own system prompt (not Claude Code's), the full text of skills preloaded via the `skills:` field, CLAUDE.md and git status (the built-in Explore / Plan subagents omit these two), and whatever the main agent passes in the prompt. Suited to research that reads dozens of files, parallel tasks, and specialized roles.\n- **Fork**: copies the parent session's conversation so far, system prompt, and tools; suited to \"taking all the context into a branch task\".\n- **Dynamic workflow**: the model writes its own script to orchestrate many subagents and returns one result, used for work beyond the scale of a few subagents, such as whole-repo audits, large migrations, or producing proposals from multiple angles; it can also have a second wave of agents cross-check the first wave's findings.\n- Subagents can message each other by name, and there's also cross-session messaging between different sessions.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSubagents buy isolation and parallelism at the cost of reducing information to a summary, and parent and child may end up \"doing their own thing\". The mitigation is for the lead agent to explicitly write the necessary context into the prompt, and to hand tasks that need the full context to a fork.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The difference between a subagent and a fork is \"whether it inherits the parent session's context\".\n- When to use a subagent: when a side task's output would flood the main context.","L6-T6B.1.6":"Claude Code's extension mechanisms have clearly divided roles: CLAUDE.md holds rules the model must always know, skills hold knowledge and procedures loaded on demand, hooks provide deterministic automation, and plugins handle packaging and distribution. The core distinction is \"request\" vs \"enforcement\".\n\n**How it works**\n- **Hooks**: run a shell command, HTTP request, MCP tool call, a prompt, or a subagent on lifecycle events; events include `PreToolUse` / `PostToolUse`, `SessionStart`, user prompt submission, permission requests, compaction, and more.\n- **CLAUDE.md**: loaded in every session; the official recommendation is to keep it under 200 lines and move details into path-triggered `.claude/rules/` or skills.\n- **Skills**: only the description loads at first and the full text loads on use; they can run in the current context or `context: fork` into an isolated context; vague descriptions cause false triggers or missed triggers.\n- **Plugins**: a distribution unit bundling skills + hooks + subagents + MCP, with namespacing. On name conflicts, skill precedence is managed > user > project, while hooks are all merged and executed.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe gist of the official wording: writing \"never edit `.env`\" in CLAUDE.md is only a request; a `PreToolUse` hook blocking that edit is enforcement. Hooks are the only mechanism in the harness that doesn't depend on the model obeying, so guardrails belong at this layer.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The official trigger table: the model gets the same convention wrong twice → CLAUDE.md; you keep pasting the same procedure → skill; a side task floods the context → subagent; it must happen every time → hook; a second repo needs the same setup → plugin.","L6-T6B.1.7":"Claude Code's permission system has to resolve a tension: confirming every action manually causes approval fatigue, while opening everything up (`--dangerously-skip-permissions`) offers no protection at all. The answer is three stacked layers of rules, sandboxes, and classifiers, with auto mode being the classifier layer.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Rules**: allowlists and hooks; deterministic but unable to cover the long tail.\n- **Sandbox**: filesystem and network isolation (the 2025-10 sandboxing approach); strong isolation, but rules must be maintained for each new capability.\n- **Classifier**: in auto mode (released 2026-03-25), a transcript classifier reviews each action before it executes; it covers the long tail but has false positives and false negatives.\n- Key design: the transcript sent to the classifier has assistant messages and tool results stripped, keeping only user intent and the actions themselves.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe motivation came from internal data: users manually approved 93% of permission requests, so manual confirmation had become a formality. Stripping assistant messages is a deliberate trade-off, giving up some source information in exchange for two guarantees: the agent can't \"persuade\" the classifier, and injected text can't reach the classifier. Mature products stack all three layers rather than picking one.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Present rules / sandboxes / classifiers side by side, with one strength and one cost for each.\n- \"What if the classifier itself gets injected?\" Don't send tool output to the classifier; send only user intent and actions.","L6-T6B.1.8":"Claude Code has four permission modes, switched with `Shift+Tab`, that decide whether the model needs human confirmation before acting. They form a spectrum from \"fully automatic\" to \"read-only planning\".\n\n**How it works**\n- **Auto**: a classifier reviews most actions in the background, blocking only dangerous ones without asking about each; since v2.1.283 it is the default starting mode for interactive terminal and VS Code sessions.\n- **Manual**: asks about both file edits and commands.\n- **Accept edits**: file edits and common filesystem commands don't require asking; everything else still does.\n- **Plan**: only explores and proposes, without changing source files; good for aligning on an approach before making changes.\n- In addition, specific commands can be allowlisted in `settings.json`, stacking with the mode.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nManual is the safest but causes severe approval fatigue, and users start clicking approve without thinking; Accept edits leverages the fact that \"in-project edits can be rolled back via version control\", relaxing reversible actions; Plan separates \"thinking\" from \"doing\", reducing the risk of large changes going off track; Auto replaces human review of long-tail actions with a classifier, but has false positives and false negatives, and does not replace human review for high-risk infrastructure.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Choose modes based on the reversibility and risk of actions, not one-size-fits-all.\n- Be able to say that Auto is now the default, and the motivation behind it: \"93% of requests were manually approved\".","L6-T6B.1.9":"Claude Code's auto mode replaces per-action human approval with \"two layers of defense + three tiers\", a classic interview topic for agent permission design.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Input side**: a server-side prompt-injection probe first screens tool output and attaches a warning when it finds suspicious content.\n- **Output side**: a transcript classifier (Sonnet 4.6) evaluates each action before it executes, in two stages: first a fast single-token filter, and only on a hit does it proceed to chain-of-thought reasoning.\n- **Three tiers**: Tier 1 read-only operations go through a built-in allowlist; Tier 2 in-project file edits, reviewable via version control; Tier 3 high-risk actions require the classifier's clearance.\n- The transcript sent to the classifier has assistant messages and tool results stripped.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nNumbers: on 10,000 real internal tool calls, the false-positive rate was 8.5% for the first stage alone and 0.4% for the full pipeline; on 52 real overreaching actions, the miss rate was 17%; on 1,000 synthetic data exfiltration attempts, the miss rate was 5.7%. The first stage is deliberately conservative (high recall), and the second stage uses reasoning to recover precision. The authors state explicitly that it does not replace human review for high-risk infrastructure.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Is a 17% miss rate acceptable? It depends on the threat model: combined with the sandbox and git reversibility, residual risk concentrates on remote irreversible actions; and the confidence interval on 52 samples is very wide.\n- Why two stages: most requests run only the first stage, keeping latency low.\n- After launch, feed cases that users manually allowed or overrode back into the eval set.","L6-T6B.2.1":"By 2026, CLI coding agents have become highly homogeneous in architecture (instruction files, MCP, hooks, subagents, sandboxes), so in a comparative interview answer you only need to explain what makes each one \"different from the rest\".\n\n**What sets each apart**\n- **OpenAI Codex**: bet earlier on async cloud tasks (submit a task, come back to a PR); subagents became generally available in v0.115.0 on 2026-03-16, with up to 6 concurrent; uses AGENTS.md, with sandboxing via macOS Seatbelt and Linux Landlock + seccomp.\n- **Cursor**: IDE + cloud agents, with self-trained embeddings for semantic retrieval mixed with grep, improving Q&A accuracy by 12.5% on average; self-trained Composer-series models serve as fast, cheap workers.\n- **OpenHands**: CodeAct (executable Python / bash as a unified action space) and an event stream.\n- **Aider**: a repo map (tree-sitter symbol extraction, graph building, then PageRank-style ranking) and systematic comparisons of multiple edit formats.\n- **Devin / Devin Desktop**: SWE-grep, a dedicated retrieval model, and Codemaps.\n- **SWE-agent / mini-swe-agent**: the ACI and a minimalist bash baseline.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe differences concentrate along two axes, context acquisition (agentic search / vectors / repo map / dedicated models) and action space (JSON tools / executable code / bash), which is also where interviews discriminate most.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Memorize a five-column comparison table: \"form factor, context acquisition, edit method, autonomy highlights, unique features\".\n- Codex's Smart Approvals and Claude Code's auto mode are two implementations of the same idea (the former is from secondhand reports; say it's unverified when citing it).","L6-T6B.2.2":"OpenHands (formerly OpenDevin) is the most academically oriented open-source coding agent platform (an ICLR 2025 paper), and one of its core designs is the event stream: all actions and observations enter a single time-ordered stream of events.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Event stream**: actions emitted by the model and observations returned by the environment are both appended as events to the same stream, making replay and real-time UI updates easy.\n- **Event sourcing**: the 2025-11 OpenHands Software Agent SDK paper (MLSys 2026 oral) abstracts the design into an event-sourced state model + deterministic replay, immutable agent configuration, and a typed tool system with MCP integration; both the V1 CLI and GUI are built on this SDK.\n- State isn't a variable that gets mutated directly but is \"folded\" from the event sequence; replay re-applies the events using the recorded observations, rather than calling the model again or rerunning tools with side effects.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLong tasks need to be replayable, auditable, and forkable from the middle. Claude Code achieves similar capabilities (resume / fork / rewind) with JSONL session logs + pre-edit file snapshots. The cost is log growth, requiring segmentation + snapshots. Another distinctive feature, CodeAct, lets the model use executable code as a unified action space instead of enumerating JSON tools.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- For \"design a replayable, auditable agent runtime\": an immutable event log, versioned config, deterministic replay, forking from an event point.\n- How to replay tools with side effects: record the observation, and use the recorded value on replay.","L6-T6B.2.3":"Cognition (the company behind Devin) pursued two lines worth citing in 2025–2026: using dedicated small models for context retrieval, and repositioning the IDE as a command center for managing agents.\n\n**Key facts**\n- **SWE-grep / SWE-grep-mini** (2025-10-16): small models dedicated to \"fast context retrieval\"; small, fast models run multiple rounds of search in parallel and feed the results to the main model, claimed to be an order of magnitude faster than frontier models.\n- **SWE-1.5** (2025-10-29): a frontier-scale coding model at up to 950 tok/s; followed by Codemaps (2025-11-04, a code-path map shared by humans and AI), Devin 2.2 (2026-02-24), and SWE-2 (2026-09-10).\n- **Devin Desktop** (2026-06-02): Windsurf was renamed Devin Desktop, with an \"Agent Command Center\" for managing local / cloud agents and PRs in one place.\n- On 2026-09-25 it announced an annualized revenue run rate above $1 billion.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSWE-grep represents the \"dedicated retrieval model\" route among the four approaches to context acquisition: fast, with a clean main-model context, at the cost of one more model to train and maintain. It also shows that \"speed itself is part of the agent experience\". Devin Desktop represents the IDE's repositioning from editor to orchestration panel.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- grep is too slow in a large monorepo: like Cognition, use a dedicated small retrieval model searching in parallel.\n- Cognition's mid-2025 \"Don't build multi-agents\" position is often contrasted with Cursor's swarm approach.","L6-T6B.2.4":"The switch from Gemini CLI to Antigravity is a textbook example of the 2026 consolidation in the CLI coding agent market.\n\n**Key facts**\n- At Google I/O on 2026-05-19, the Antigravity CLI was announced as the replacement: closed-source, implemented in Go, invoked as `agy`, retaining skills, hooks, subagents, and extensions, though initially not at full feature parity by its own account.\n- The consumer-facing Gemini CLI (free accounts and individual AI Pro / Ultra accounts) was retired on 2026-06-18; enterprise Code Assist licenses and paid API keys still work. This information comes from consistent reporting across multiple media outlets; the original Google announcement was not obtained directly.\n- Positioning differences: Gemini CLI's selling points were its large context window and free quota; Antigravity's is being an \"agent-first IDE\", with a visual Agent Manager, reviewable artifacts, and multi-model support.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe move from an open-source CLI to a closed-source agent-first product shows vendors shifting focus from \"an assistant in the command line\" to \"a workbench for managing multiple agents\", the same trend as Windsurf's renaming to Devin Desktop. For users, depending on open-source tools also carries the risk of them being retired, so the interface layer should be swappable.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use it as evidence that \"CLI coding agents have homogenized and the market is consolidating\".\n- When citing it, note that the source is secondhand reporting, showing a fact-checking habit.","L6-T6B.2.5":"Cline and Kiro represent two different orientations for human-AI collaboration: Cline emphasizes process transparency, while Kiro emphasizes writing specs before implementing (spec-driven development).\n\n**How it works**\n- **Cline**: started as a VS Code extension where every diff and command can be reviewed, supporting BYO-model (bring your own model); in 2026 it launched CLI 2.0 and spun off an open SDK (secondhand reports, unverified).\n- **Kiro (AWS)**: first writes the requirements into three documents (requirements, design, tasks), then implements task by task, with steering files and hooks. It represents the school of \"treating the PRD as part of the harness\".\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe essence of spec coding is moving acceptance criteria up front: each item in the requirements becomes the source of the default-FAIL checklist in a long-task harness, the same idea as Anthropic's planner expanding a one- or two-sentence request into a product spec. The cost is the upfront effort of writing documents, which may be too heavy for small changes. Keep the concepts distinct: a spec is just one input to the harness, while the harness is the entire runtime environment around the model (loop, tools, context, permissions, verification, etc.).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"How does spec coding relate to the harness?\" The spec is an input, the harness is the runtime environment; the spec turns acceptance criteria into a verifiable checklist.\n- Cline's per-step review is the productized form of the Manual permission mode: transparent but with high approval cost.","L6-T6B.2.6":"SWE-agent and mini-swe-agent are the best teaching cases for \"harness complexity vs model capability\": the former carefully designs an interface for the agent, while the latter gives it almost nothing.\n\n**How it works**\n- **ACI (Agent-Computer Interface)**: SWE-agent (NeurIPS 2024) proposed that agents need dedicated commands designed for them, such as a file viewer with a line-numbered window and an edit command with syntax checking, rather than a bare shell; ACI quality directly affects the solve rate.\n- **mini-swe-agent**: about 100 lines of Python that give the model a single bash tool, one `subprocess.run` per step, no special tools, and a linear history; its README claims over 74% on SWE-bench Verified (depending on the model).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIn 2024 models couldn't use a bare shell well, so the ACI mattered; from 2025 on, models trained on large volumes of agent trajectories have internalized habits like \"look before editing, verify in small steps\", so bash plus sed, grep, and python is general enough. The conclusion: harness complexity should be re-evaluated as model capability grows, trimming when the time is right. The linear history and lack of special tools also make mini-swe-agent a clean baseline for RL training and evaluation. But it lacks the trust and operations layers production needs: permissions and sandboxing, crash recovery, parallel coordination, observability, and cost control.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The 74%+ figure depends on the model and evaluation configuration; check the specific leaderboard entry.\n- Suited as an evaluation harness and teaching baseline, not a production system.","L6-T6B.2.7":"Context acquisition is the most central and most frequently asked sub-problem for coding agents: how to find the code relevant to a task in a large repository. The industry has four approaches.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Agentic search**: the model itself globs / greps / reads files over multiple iterations. Zero index, always fresh, and it can follow references; the cost is latency and tokens from multiple round trips. Examples: Claude Code, Codex, mini-swe-agent.\n- **Vector index**: chunk → embed → vector retrieval, often mixed with lexical search. Natural-language questions can hit in one hop; the costs are index maintenance (incremental updates, branches, permissions), chunk boundaries that split meaning, and code privacy. Example: Cursor.\n- **Repo map**: build a symbol graph via static analysis and fit the most relevant signatures into a token budget. Cheap, gives a global skeleton; only signatures, no implementations. Example: Aider.\n- **Dedicated retrieval model**: small, fast models run multiple rounds of search in parallel and feed results to the main model. An order of magnitude faster with a clean main context; one more model to train and maintain. Example: Cognition SWE-grep.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe mainstream closed-source CLIs don't build vector indexes: code queries are mostly exact, indexes are stateful and drift out of sync, and iterative search is itself reasoning. But Cursor's data shows hybrid retrieval averages 12.5% better than pure grep, with the biggest advantage on queries describing functionality in natural language. The right answer is routing by query type: exact queries go to grep / LSP, semantic queries to embeddings, and when neither works, let the agent take a few more rounds.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- How to quantify: group by query type and measure rounds to first hit, total tokens, and final correctness.\n- LSP is both a retrieval tool and a verification tool.","L6-T6B.2.8":"Edit formats answer \"how does the model change files\". Formats differ greatly in token cost, robustness, and how diagnosable failures are, and models differ in how well they follow each format.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Whole file**: rewrite the entire file. Simplest and can't misalign; tokens explode on large files, and models tend to get lazy and write `# ... rest unchanged`.\n- **Unified diff**: output `@@` hunks. Compact and readable; a slight offset in line numbers or context makes apply fail.\n- **Search / replace block**: give \"original snippet → new snippet\". Doesn't depend on line numbers and allows fuzzy matching; fails when the snippet isn't unique or whitespace differs.\n- **`str_replace` tool**: `old_string` must be a unique exact match. It turns \"not found / not unique\" into an explicit tool error that the model can self-correct from; requires reading the file first, and large changes need multiple calls.\n- **Apply model**: the main model outputs sketch-style edits, and a small model handles merging. Saves main-model tokens; adds another model, and merge errors are hard to notice.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe text editor tool Anthropic defines for Claude is exactly the `view / create / str_replace / insert` family; Aider's experience shows models vary widely in format adherence, so it defaults to a different format per model. In engineering terms, return the edit result (success, failure reason, new snippet) as a tool_result to close the loop, and snapshot files before editing to allow rollback.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Recommend str_replace as the default (unique exact match + read before editing), falling back to whole file for large rewrites.\n- Whitespace mismatches: on failure, return the closest candidate snippet, or match after normalization.","L6-T6B.2.9":"Coding agents are more reliable than chatbots not mainly because the model is stronger, but because they can verify their own work. The verification loop feeds check results back to the model to drive the next round of changes.\n\n**How it works**\nFrom lowest to highest cost and trustworthiness:\n1. Type checks / lint / LSP diagnostics: seconds, deterministic, run automatically after edits by a hook or LSP;\n2. Unit / integration tests: run first to see the failure, run again after the change, i.e. the TDD workflow;\n3. End-to-end / browser verification: use Playwright (often via MCP) to operate like a user, which is what Anthropic's evaluator agent does;\n4. Independent reviewer: another agent that never saw the generation process, scoring against a default-FAIL standard;\n5. Human review: PR review as the final gate.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTwo often-overlooked principles: first, verification output must return to the context, otherwise the model doesn't know it failed, but large test outputs crowd the window, so truncate, summarize, or return only the failures; second, don't let the generator grade itself: Anthropic observed models confidently praising their own mediocre work, and separating generator and evaluator is much easier than teaching a model to critique itself.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Quality assurance is layered: deterministic checks → independent evaluator → human review, not prompts.\n- Preventing the agent from changing tests to make them pass: make test files read-only, or require separate review of changes to them.","L6-T6B.2.10":"A unit test generation agent is a real interview question that grounds \"context acquisition\" and \"verification\" in a concrete product. The main line of the answer: triage before generating, filter by execution results after generating, then measure \"what was actually tested\".\n\n**How it works**\nA five-step pipeline:\n1. **Entry filtering**: enumerate functions via the AST and compute static signals (I/O, network, time/randomness, concurrency, cyclomatic complexity, etc.), sorting them into tiers: \"unit test directly / create a seam first / switch to integration or characterization tests / skip\";\n2. **Context extraction**: the AST provides the function body, signature, and the enclosing class's constructor and fields; LSP provides cross-file truth (type definitions, callee signatures, caller examples, base class implementations); the test framework, mock library, and fixtures are read from the build config;\n3. **Generate → compile / collect → run in isolation**: run with timeouts, no network, and a temp directory; feed failures back by type, and discard flaky tests after N repeats;\n4. **Coverage feedback**: measure line + branch coverage with `--branch`, and feed uncovered fragments back to the model to add tests;\n5. **Exit gate**: keep only tests that build, pass stably, and add incremental coverage.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCoverage only shows that code was executed, not that results were checked. Use mutation testing (mutmut, PIT, Stryker) and assertion-strength checks to catch tests that \"pass but test nothing\". In one line: quality = entry filtering × context quality × execution feedback × exit gate.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- LLMs are noticeably better at fixing compile errors than runtime AssertionErrors.\n- When an assertion fails, allow the agent to report \"this may be a bug in the code\" rather than automatically changing the assertion to make it pass (the oracle problem).","L6-T6B.2.11":"Anthropic's published long-running task harness addresses a fundamental problem: every new session starts from scratch, and the agent either redoes work or treats half-finished work as done.\n\n**How it works**\n- **First version (2025-11-26)**: an initializer agent breaks the requirements into a structured feature list (each item initially \"failing\") and writes an environment setup script; after that, each coding agent session first reads the progress file and runs the setup script and tests to confirm the current state, implements just one feature, then updates progress and makes a git commit. Between sessions, instead of compaction it uses context reset + a structured handoff (progress file + feature list + git history).\n- **Second version (2026-03-24)**: upgraded to three roles. The Planner expands a 1–4 sentence request into a product spec, focusing on scope and high-level design; the Generator implements feature by feature (sprint decomposition was dropped on Opus 4.6); the Evaluator uses Playwright to actually operate the running application, gives actionable feedback against criteria such as design quality, originality, functionality, and code quality, and negotiates success criteria with the generator before each sprint (the sprint contract).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nNumbers: on a retro game editor task, Opus 4.5 solo took 20 minutes and $9, but the core gameplay was broken; the full harness took 6 hours and $200 and produced a complete, playable game. On a digital audio workstation task, Opus 4.6 + a simplified harness took 3 hours 50 minutes and $124.70 in total. About 20× the cost in exchange for usability.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The model repeats completed work after a reset: run tests and the setup script to confirm the current state before starting.\n- An evaluator only creates value when the task exceeds what the model can reliably do solo.","L6-T6B.2.12":"In long-running task experiments, Anthropic found two model-level problems that directly shaped the structure of the harness: context anxiety and self-evaluation bias.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Context anxiety**: Sonnet 4.5 wraps up prematurely as it approaches what it believes is the context limit, ending tasks hastily; the write-up says Opus 4.5 has largely resolved this on its own. The countermeasure is context reset + a structured handoff file, so each session starts from a clean window instead of struggling on in a nearly full one.\n- **Self-evaluation bias**: generators confidently praise their own mediocre work, especially on subjective tasks like design. The countermeasure is to split generator and evaluator into two roles, with an independent evaluator doing the scoring.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese two problems show that harness components encode assumptions about model limitations: context anxiety has eased on newer models, so the related components can be simplified. Separating generator and evaluator is much easier than teaching a model to critique itself, and the key is context isolation rather than switching models; the evaluator can use the same model. That said, the evaluator itself needs several rounds of tuning, or it will \"talk itself into thinking the problems are minor\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why is context reset better than compaction?\" Summaries accumulate distortion, and reset avoids context anxiety.\n- \"Why doesn't self-evaluation work?\" Self-evaluation bias; use an independent evaluator with fresh context, no write tools, and default-FAIL.\n- Calibrating the evaluator: measure agreement on human-labeled good / bad samples.","L6-T6B.2.13":"Anthropic open-sourced the verification and handoff patterns from its long-running task harness as reusable hooks + a subagent (anthropics/cwc-long-running-agents), built around three patterns.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Default-FAIL contract**: every acceptance criterion starts as false, and the evaluator must find evidence to flip it to passing, rather than assuming a pass and deducting points when it finds problems.\n- **Fresh-context evaluator**: grading is done by an independent agent that has no write tools and never saw the build process; it can only judge by actually running the application (e.g. operating the UI with Playwright, checking API and database state).\n- **Agent-maintained handoff document**: the agent itself updates progress and status so the next session can pick up after a context reset.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nDefault-FAIL puts the burden of proof on \"demonstrating completion\", directly countering the generator's self-evaluation bias; having no write tools ensures the reviewer can't quietly change code to cover up problems; never having seen the build process keeps it from being swayed by the generator's reasoning. The handoff document should have a fixed structure and record only state, not process, to prevent bloat. The cost is an extra agent, which isn't worth it for tasks the model can reliably complete solo.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The acceptance criteria from spec coding are the source of the default-FAIL checklist.\n- The evaluator's value comes from context isolation, not from using a different model.","L6-T6B.2.14":"Cursor published two case studies of building software with large-scale multi-agent systems, using a hierarchical planner / worker / judge swarm architecture; they are the most detailed public material to date.\n\n**How it works**\n- **First post (2026-01-14)**: planners continuously explore the codebase and generate tasks, and can recursively spawn sub-planners; workers claim tasks and execute independently, pushing when done without coordinating with each other; a judge decides after each round whether to continue. Coordination moved from \"locks\" to optimistic concurrency control: if the state has changed at write time, the write fails and is retried; with hundreds of workers pushing to the same branch, conflicts were rare. Results: a browser of about 1 million lines and 1,000 files written in about a week; Cursor's own migration from Solid to React (+266K / −193K) completed in three weeks.\n- **Second post (2026-07-20)**: implementing SQLite from scratch in Rust, the new swarm (Grok 4.5) reached an 80% pass rate in 4 hours; with planners on frontier models and workers on fast models, quality was similar across combinations but cost differed by up to 8×.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLessons: the hierarchical structure avoided the conservatism and lock contention of a flat structure; removing the integrator role actually improved efficiency; \"the prompt matters more than the harness and the model\"; GPT-5.2 performed best on long autonomous work, while Opus 4.5 tended to stop earlier. Failure modes included lock contention, tunnel vision, and spinning idle.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Compared with Anthropic: Anthropic emphasizes single-application quality and independent verification, Cursor emphasizes throughput at scale and coordination.\n- Why remove the integrator: every extra role adds another layer of information loss and another bottleneck.","L6-T6B.2.15":"\"Model economics\" comes from Cursor's 2026-07-20 swarm experiment: the cost of a multi-agent system is determined by \"who makes the decisions\", not by total token volume.\n\n**How it works**\n- Model combinations reach similar quality, but costs differ 8×: GPT-5.5 as both planner and worker cost $10,565; Opus 4.8 as planner + Composer 2.5 as worker cost $1,339.\n- In the Opus + Composer combination, workers account for over 90% of tokens and planners a small share of tokens, yet planners account for about two-thirds of the cost. The reason is cost = tokens × unit price, and frontier models have high unit prices; meanwhile, the moments in a large task that truly need frontier intelligence are few.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe resulting design is routing by role: frontier models for planning, arbitration, and review; fast models for execution, retrieval, and formatting; and within a role, escalate dynamically by task difficulty (number of files, number of test failures). Cursor's 2026-08 Router is the productization of this idea. The precondition is independent verification; otherwise the cheap models' errors get amplified.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Deciding whether a request \"needs frontier intelligence\": heuristics (task type, past failures) + a cascade where a small model tries first and escalates on failure.\n- Will cascading be slower? Optimize both p95 latency and total cost, not just the average.\n- Contrast with Anthropic's \"20× the cost for usability\": one saves money, the other spends money to buy quality.","L6-T6B.2.16":"When hundreds of agents modify the same codebase in parallel, the biggest challenge is coordination: conflicts, duplicated work, and file contention. Cursor's swarm experiment gives both the scale of the problem and the countermeasures.\n\n**Scale of the problem**\nThe old system made about 68,000 commits and over 70,000 merge conflicts in 2 hours, with a single file producing 7,771 conflicts; the new system had fewer than 1,000 conflicts in 4 hours, with 47 on the hottest file. Other details include 54 duplicate crates and 1,173 agents touching the same file (unverified).\n\n**Countermeasures**\n- A custom VCS supporting 1,000 commits per second;\n- Design document conflicts between planners are merged by a reconciler and propagated downward via compile-checked references;\n- A neutral third-party agent arbitrates code merge conflicts;\n- When a file gets too large, a dedicated agent is sent to split the module, reducing contention;\n- Agents jointly maintain a \"Field Guide\" that is automatically injected into every new agent, sharing experience.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhat these measures have in common is \"coordination through data structures, not locks\", turning implicit conflicts into detectable, arbitrable explicit events. Ordinary teams don't need a custom VCS; a merge queue + batching is enough.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Designing dozens of agents modifying the same repository in parallel: split tasks by file set, separate worktrees, optimistic concurrency, an arbitration agent, a Field Guide, CI gates, and rollback.\n- How the arbitration agent stays neutral: it belongs to no worker and sees only the two diffs and the design documents.","L6-T6B.2.17":"Anthropic's and Cursor's approaches to long-running autonomous coding have different goals, but the principles they distill are highly consistent and make a good summary framework for interviews.\n\n**Six principles**\n1. **Context is the primary bottleneck**: context reset + structured handoffs beat compaction; role separation (planners don't implement, workers don't plan) is essentially context specialization.\n2. **Verification must be independent**: a fresh-context evaluator, default-FAIL, and E2E on the actually running system.\n3. **Coordinate through data structures, not locks**: optimistic concurrency, event logs, a shared Field Guide / progress file.\n4. **Cost sits with the planner**: frontier models make a few high-value decisions, fast models do the bulk of execution.\n5. **Harnesses expire**: remove components that no longer bear load as models improve, e.g. Anthropic dropped sprint decomposition on Opus 4.6.\n6. **Public contrast**: Cognition's \"don't build multi-agents\" and Cursor's \"hundreds of agents\" are not contradictory.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nContext reset beats compaction because summaries accumulate distortion and can't be inspected, while progress files, feature lists, and git are inspectable, versionable ground truth on disk. But in short interactive tasks, a reset loses immediate intent, so compaction is more appropriate. The way to decide whether a component should stay is ablation: remove it and compare success rate and cost on the same task set.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Debugging a long task that has gone off track: first check the judge / evaluator decision logs, then whether the progress file matches the code and whether workers are repeating the same task.\n- Small teams should start with an Anthropic-style evaluator, and move to Cursor-style parallelism once throughput demands it.","L6-T6B.2.18":"In 2025 Cognition argued \"Don't build multi-agents\", yet Cursor runs hundreds of agents to write software. Interviewers often ask who is right. The answer is that they don't contradict each other; what matters is whether conflicts can be detected.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Cognition's argument**: when parallel sub-agents don't share context, they make conflicting implicit decisions (e.g., each side picks a different style or interface convention) that only surface at merge time; hence the recommendation of a single thread plus context compression.\n- **Cursor's approach**: turn conflicts into explicit events that can be detected and arbitrated. A hierarchy assigns tasks, optimistic concurrency makes write conflicts fail immediately and retry, planner-document conflicts are merged by a reconciler, code conflicts go to a neutral arbiter agent, and file contention is reduced by splitting modules.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe key variables are shared mutable state and whether conflicts are detectable: if conflicts can't be detected, stay single-threaded; parallelize only when conflicts can be turned into detectable events on a data structure. Hidden costs of parallelism include multiplied tokens, duplicated work, and the burden of reviewing merges. Tasks that are naturally parallel have clear subtask boundaries, nearly disjoint file sets, and can be verified independently.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Being able to explain this is what separates \"memorized the blog posts\" from \"understands the mechanism\".\n- If asked \"which tasks suit parallelism\", give the three conditions: clear boundaries, non-overlapping files, independent verification.","L6-T6B.3.1":"CATGen is a 2026-07 industrial experience paper on unit-test generation. It answers the most basic question: how do you get model-generated tests to compile in the first place?\n\n**How it works**\nCompilation success rate depends mainly on three things:\n- **Hand project dependencies to the model explicitly**: the types the function under test depends on, how to construct them, and its external collaborators must be put into the context explicitly rather than left for the model to guess. Of the paper's 183 industrial focal methods, 57.38% had 3 or more external dependencies.\n- **Generate the test-class skeleton with deterministic code**: boilerplate such as imports, annotations, mock declarations, and setup comes from templates; the model only writes the test body.\n- **Fix compile errors with lightweight static analysis**, instead of having the LLM retry round after round.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis reflects the engineering principle of \"give deterministic work to code, uncertain work to the model\": both the skeleton and compile-error fixes can be expressed as rules, and handing them to the model is slow and unstable. It maps to step two of the unit-test pipeline, \"extract context\": the AST provides the function body and class structure, LSP provides cross-file type definitions and callee signatures, and the build config provides the test framework and mock library. Another study (Panta) also observed that LLMs are clearly better at fixing compile errors than runtime assertion failures.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Compilation rate is the first evaluation metric for a unit-test agent.\n- Be able to state the three lessons and point out that methods with many dependencies are the main difficulty.","L6-T6B.3.2":"CoverUp (FSE 2025) is a coverage-driven unit-test generation method: it feeds \"which code hasn't been tested yet\" back to the model as feedback and iteratively adds tests.\n\n**How it works**\n- Use coverage.py's `--branch` to measure line and branch coverage together. Looking only at line coverage misses the false branch of an `if` that is never taken.\n- Feed the uncovered lines and branch fragments, together with code context, back to the model so it writes targeted tests; run, measure again, and repeat.\n- On its benchmark, the median per-module line + branch coverage was 80%, versus 47% for the CodaMosa baseline.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe authors stress that the gains come from the combination of \"coverage analysis + code context + feedback\", not just the model itself, which again shows that the harness is load-bearing. For functions with high cyclomatic complexity, models tend to cover only the happy path, so driving the next round with uncovered branches is exactly the right remedy. But coverage only shows that code was executed, not that results were checked, so it must be paired with mutation testing and assertion-strength checks to avoid \"pseudo-tested\" code: covered, yet deleting the method body causes no test failure.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why branch coverage rather than just line coverage.\n- Coverage is one signal; mutation score is closer to \"can it catch regressions\". Mutation testing is expensive, so run it incrementally only on files touched by the diff.","L6-T6B.3.3":"The exit gate is the last step of a unit-test generation pipeline: keep only the tests that actually add value. Meta's TestGen-LLM (FSE 2024) is exactly such a filter.\n\n**How it works**\nA candidate test must pass three filters in order: it builds; it passes stably over multiple runs (ruling out flaky tests); and it delivers a measurable coverage increase. Failing any one means it is discarded.\n\n**Data**\nIn the evaluation on Instagram Reels / Stories, 75% of candidates built, 57% passed stably, and 25% increased coverage; in a test-a-thon it improved 11.5% of the classes it was applied to, and 73% of its suggestions were accepted by engineers and landed in production.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese numbers show that most candidates are filtered out, and the filter itself is the product: the model's job is to produce many candidates, deterministic building, repeated runs, and coverage checks guarantee quality, and engineers only see what passes, which is why acceptance is so high. The costs are compute (repeated runs, measuring coverage), and the fact that \"passes stably\" implicitly treats the current implementation as correct. This is a regression-testing setup and cannot find existing bugs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Memorize the three funnel numbers: 75% / 57% / 25%.\n- Beyond the gate, add assertion-strength checks: reject tests with no assertions, tests that only assert `is not None`, and `assert True`.\n- Metrics for evaluating a unit-test agent: compilation rate, stable pass rate, incremental coverage, mutation score, human acceptance rate.","L6-T6B.3.4":"Some code is inherently hard to unit-test, and forcing generation only produces flaky tests or tests that \"restate the implementation\". The remedy is to triage first and generate second, recording a reason for every skip.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Bucket at the entrance by static signals**: use the AST to look for signals such as heavy IO (`open`, `shutil`), network and external services (`requests`, cloud SDKs, `subprocess`), time and randomness (`datetime.now`, `random.*`, `uuid4`), global state (`os.environ`, module-level singletons), concurrency (`threading`, `asyncio.gather`), deep inheritance or framework-managed code, too many collaborators, high cyclomatic complexity, and no return value. Functions are sorted into tiers: `unit / unit+seam / integration / characterization / skip-unit`.\n- **Handling**: for time/randomness, first create a seam (turn the clock, RNG, or client into parameters) and generate only after a human confirms; network code is demoted to integration or contract tests; code with too many collaborators (needing five or six mocks) gets a characterization test instead, i.e., pin down the current behavior first; concurrent code gets no unit tests, with the reason written down.\n- **A second filter after running**: tests whose results differ across runs are flagged as flaky and dropped; tests that only pass with network access or real files reveal a miss at the entrance and are written back as rules.\n- Skipped functions go into the report so they stay visible in the coverage denominator.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOn TestGenEval, the best model, GPT-4o, averaged only 35.2% coverage; typical failures are the model mocking dependencies incorrectly and hallucinating calls that don't exist. Better to move a test up to the integration layer than to pile mocks into a unit test.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why not mock everything: over-mocked tests break on every refactor, and the mocked behavior itself may be wrong.","L6-T6B.3.5":"Pseudo-tested methods are methods that tests execute and that count toward coverage, but whose entire body can be deleted without any test failing. They expose an often-overlooked fact: coverage only shows that code ran, not that its results were checked.\n\n**How it works**: The concept was introduced by Niedermayr et al. in 2016; Vera-Pérez et al. replicated it on 28K+ methods and found it equally common in high-coverage projects. Detection is an extreme form of mutation: replace the method body with an empty implementation or a default return (clear void methods, return a constant for methods with a return value), then run the tests that cover it; if they all still pass, the method is pseudo-tested. The more general complement is mutation testing (mutmut for Python, PIT for Java, Stryker for JS/C#), where the mutation score measures whether tests can \"kill\" small changes.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: LLM-generated unit tests are especially prone to this: the test calls the function but only asserts `is not None`, so coverage looks great but regressions go unnoticed. Mutation testing is expensive, so in practice it is run incrementally only on files changed in this diff or on high-value modules.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- When asked \"how do you measure the quality of a unit-test generation agent\", say you look at mutation score and assertion strength in addition to coverage.\n- If pressed that mutation testing is too slow: run it only on files in the diff and the subset of tests covering them, or, like Meta's ACH, generate a small number of mutants targeting only the fault types you care about.","L6-T6B.3.6":"Assertion-strength checking is a static exit gate in a unit-test generation pipeline: it rejects tests that \"pass but don't actually verify anything\", preventing weak assertions from inflating coverage.\n\n**How it works**: Scan generated test functions with the AST and reject or send back for rewriting any that match these patterns: no assertions at all; only asserting `x is not None` or only asserting a type; tautologies like `assert True`; `try/except` blocks that catch and swallow exceptions without asserting. For tests that use mocks, require at least one assertion on a real output or state change, not merely \"the mock was called\". For functions without a return value, assert on the state they change or their interactions with collaborators.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Code with no return value and no observable output has no natural oracle, so it is where models most easily write weak assertions; on top of that, a coverage feedback loop rewards the model for \"getting the code to run\" rather than checking results. Assertion-strength checks are cheap and deterministic and complement expensive mutation testing: the former blocks obviously empty tests, the latter quantifies \"can it catch regressions\". Overly strict rules may wrongly reject legitimate smoke tests, so they can be relaxed per directory or via markers.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Three lines of defense against \"passes but tests nothing\": static assertion-strength checks, incremental mutation testing, and pseudo-tested method detection.\n- With excessive mocking, the test just restates the implementation and breaks on every refactor; better to move it up to the integration layer.","L6-T6B.3.7":"The test oracle problem is that a test needs to know \"what the correct output is\", but an LLM generating tests can only consult the current implementation, so it implicitly treats existing behavior as correct (a regression-testing setup). If the code itself has a bug, the generated test enshrines the bug as \"expected behavior\".\n\n**How it works**: A 2026-07 replication study found that when the code under test can be assumed bug-free, coverage and mutation score are usable for comparing models; when the code under test may itself be buggy, coverage correlates only weakly with the ability to find real bugs, and models write tests that \"assert the wrong behavior\". So in the pipeline, assertion failures must be classified first: is the test wrong, or might the code have a bug? The agent should be able to output \"possible code bug\" and hand it to a human, rather than automatically editing the assertion to make it pass.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: This defines what a unit-test generation agent is for: it is good at backfilling regression tests and pinning existing behavior (characterization tests), but it does not inherently find defects. Finding bugs requires an external oracle, such as a requirements spec, docstrings, type contracts, property-based tests, or human confirmation. Automatically \"fixing assertions\" can raise the pass-rate metric, but it hides real defects.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Proactively state that generated tests are a regression setup; high coverage does not mean bugs get found.\n- Strategy for assertion failures: classify and feed back; escalate suspected code bugs to a human.\n- Relation to spec coding: moving acceptance criteria up front is exactly how you give the agent an independent oracle.","L6-T6B.3.8":"An AI code review agent takes a PR as input and automatically produces inline review comments; the extended version starts from a Jira ticket, implements the change, and opens a PR. The core metric is comment precision, not volume, because false positives make developers simply turn it off.\n\n**How it works**: A webhook fires → assemble context (the diff, callers and definitions of changed functions via LSP / grep rather than the whole repo; related tests, CODEOWNERS, repo conventions) → run deterministic checks (lint, types, dependency scanning) in parallel with LLM reviews split by dimension (correctness, security, performance, test coverage) → aggregate and dedupe, assign confidence, and don't post low-confidence items → post inline comments and collect accept / ignore feedback. Ticket → PR: clarify first (ask back if information is missing) → in a sandbox, loop through plan, implement, run tests → an independent evaluator reviews the diff → open a PR, which a human merges; escalate to a human if over budget.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: PR titles, comments, and code comments are all untrusted input. The 2026-04 \"Comment and Control\" research (secondhand source) showed that agents in CI can be hijacked through them, so the runner holds no unnecessary secrets, tokens get least privilege, and review results are only written as comments, never executed. Large PRs are sharded by module, reviewed in parallel, then aggregated across shards; over budget, review only high-risk paths.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Evaluate by replaying historical PRs: comment acceptance rate, real defects missed, ticket resolution rate, amount of human edits.\n- When acceptance is low, slice by category to diagnose; hand style-related noise to deterministic tools.\n- Prevent changing tests just to make them pass: make test files read-only or review test changes separately.","L6-T6B.3.9":"The methodology for optimizing a coding agent is \"build evals first, then optimize\": use data to locate which stage failures come from, then change one component at a time. Spec coding means writing requirements as a spec before implementing; it is one input to the harness, not the harness itself.\n\n**How it works**: Step one: build a task set from your own repo's historical PRs, recording pass rate, steps, tokens, latency, and number of human interventions. Step two: attribute failures from trajectories: can't find the code (retrieval: add LSP, semantic search, a repo map), edits are wrong (edit format, read before editing), changes not verified (automatic test / lint hooks), forgets things on long runs (todo, progress file, subagents), too costly (model tiering, caching, truncating tool output). Step three: ablate: fix the model and change only the harness, or fix the harness and swap the model, remembering that harness components go stale as models improve. Quality assurance is layered: deterministic checks → independent evaluator → human review.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: The Scaffold Effect study showed that the same model can differ by up to about 40x in tokens per task across harnesses, so the unit of comparison should be \"harness + model\". Spec coding (e.g., Kiro's requirements / design / tasks) essentially moves acceptance criteria up front, turning them into a default-FAIL checklist in the harness. Rules files should contain only essential conventions; verbose context files actually add cost.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Proving an optimization works: fix the model and random seed, run multiple times, report mean and confidence intervals.\n- Passes tests but code quality is poor: add quality dimensions to the evaluator, complexity gates, and sampled human review.","L6-T6B.3.10":"When building an internal coding agent platform for a company, a sensible path is to proceed in four incremental phases, each with clear metrics, rather than building a complex long-running task system from day one.\n\n**How it works**:\n- Phase 1: use an off-the-shelf CLI (Claude Code / Codex) with a sandbox, an allowlist, and evaluation on an internal task set; metrics are pass rate and number of human interventions.\n- Phase 2: build up CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md instruction files, team skills, and PreToolUse guardrail hooks; the metric is fewer repeated corrections.\n- Phase 3: introduce sub-agents and cloud async tasks and integrate with CI; the metric is throughput.\n- Phase 4: only now build a planner / evaluator long-running task harness and model routing (frontier model plans, fast model executes); the metric is cost per feature.\nEvery phase preserves the ability to \"swap the model without swapping the harness\".\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: This reflects the judgment \"don't build the loop yourself; build the guardrails, evals, and knowledge layer yourself\": generic loops already exist as mature products, and the differentiated value lies in internal data, permissions, and verification. You need evals first to judge whether each subsequent step is worth it; long-running task harnesses are expensive and only pay off on tasks beyond what the model can do solo.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Build or buy: buy the loop; build guardrails, evals, and the knowledge layer.\n- Convincing the security team: layered evidence of sandbox + audit logs + classifier.\n- Give quantifiable metrics for every phase to show an engineering mindset.","L6-T6B.4.1":"A deep research agent autonomously runs dozens to hundreds of searches and reads for an open-ended question and synthesizes a long, cited report, taking roughly 5–30 minutes. Unlike plain RAG's \"retrieve once + generate once\", it is a multi-round planned retrieval loop + evidence management + long-form generation; compared with agentic RAG, it is heavier in scale (hundreds of sources), parallelism, and report generation.\n\n**How it works**: An optional Clarifier asks about scope → the Planner splits the question into 3–8 sub-questions and search strategies → multiple Searchers run in parallel, each with isolated context, iterating search→read→extract from broad to narrow and returning only compressed findings and sources → the evidence pool maps claims ↔ sources, dedupes, and flags conflicts → the Planner reviews coverage and budget to decide whether to search more → the Writer generates section by section (each section with its own evidence subset) → a separate citation stage links each claim back to the source snippet saved at fetch time. Sub-agents don't talk to each other directly; they exchange information via the lead and a shared workspace.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Citation drift (the link exists but doesn't support the sentence) is the most common quality problem, so citation gets its own step. Other failure modes: premature convergence, searchers repeating searches (no shared visited set), self-contradiction, runaway tokens, and prompt injection from web pages. Tasks longer than a minute or two should switch to asynchronous delivery.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- The budget-control trio: caps on rounds, number of searches, and total tokens, with spent / remaining fed to the planner.\n- Evaluation: BrowseComp, GAIA, HLE, and xbench-DeepSearch for capability; report quality via rubric + LLM-as-judge + spot-checking citations.\n- Multi-agent suits breadth search, not tightly coupled tasks like writing code.","L6-T6B.4.2":"The token economics of multi-agent systems says that the quality gain from parallel agents is fundamentally buying wider search coverage with more tokens, not free emergent intelligence.\n\n**How it works**: The multi-agent research system Anthropic published on 2025-06-13 uses an orchestrator–worker architecture. On an internal research eval it scored 90.2% higher than a single-agent Opus 4, but consumed about 15x the tokens of a normal chat (a single agent uses about 4x); token usage explained about 80% of the performance variance. Each subagent has its own context window, effectively enlarging the total usable context, which lets it explore more sources in parallel.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: This gain only applies to parallelizable breadth-search tasks; for tasks that need the full shared context, such as writing code or coherent long-form writing, a single agent is better. Cost controls: the planner sets the number of subagents and call caps by task complexity (otherwise the model will spawn 50 subagents for a simple question); searchers return only compressed findings; a shared visited-URL set; caching fetched results; smaller models for subagents; and writing spent / remaining budget into the planner's context.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- When citing numbers, state the baseline (single-agent Opus 4) and the measurement (internal eval).\n- \"Is multi-agent always better?\": No. It depends on whether the task is parallelizable and whether you can afford roughly 15x the tokens.\n- Quality rises with budget but with diminishing returns; use a \"budget vs quality\" curve to find the knee.","L6-T6B.4.3":"Computer-use and browser agents let a model operate a GUI or web page like a human. The core design trade-off is observation: what the model uses to \"see\" the interface and how it locates the element to act on.\n\n**How it works**:\n- Screenshots + coordinates: a vision model looks at the screen and outputs click coordinates and keystrokes. Universal, works on any GUI (desktop, Canvas, legacy systems); but one image per step is token-expensive, coordinates are error-prone, small fonts and pop-ups are hard, and it's slow.\n- DOM / accessibility tree: read a structured tree and act via element refs. Fast, cheap, precise, and can filter invisible elements; but only works in browsers or apps with an accessibility tree, coverage of Canvas, iframes, and Shadow DOM is incomplete, and trees can be huge and need pruning.\n- Hybrid: tree first, screenshots for verification and fallback, balancing precision and generality, at the cost of engineering complexity and keeping two observation channels aligned.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Security is the first design constraint: web pages are untrusted input, and prompt injection can turn directly into \"place an order / send an email on the user's behalf\". Evaluate with OSWorld (real desktop tasks) and WebArena / Mind2Web (web tasks), and look beyond success rate at steps, latency, and human takeovers.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Be able to compare the three observation approaches and state each one's cost.\n- A third path: WebMCP lets sites declare tools themselves, beating UI-guessing on both reliability and cost.\n- OSWorld 2.0 shows the bottlenecks are constraint tracking, missed information, not verifying, and guessing instead of asking.","L6-T6B.4.4":"Manus's context-engineering blog post, published 2025-07, distills six lessons from a production general-purpose agent. It is a go-to source when discussing \"context engineering\" in interviews; the core idea is to organize context around the KV cache and attention.\n\n**How it works**:\n1. Design around KV-cache hit rate: production agents have an input/output token ratio of about 100:1, so keep the prefix stable (no timestamps in the system prompt), make context append-only, and keep tool definitions in a fixed order.\n2. Mask tools rather than removing them: dynamically adding or removing tools breaks the cache and confuses the model; control available tools with logits masking / constrained decoding instead.\n3. Use the file system as context: offload web pages and file contents to files and keep only paths / URLs, giving restorable compression.\n4. Steer attention through recitation: keep rewriting `todo.md` at the end of the context to counter goal drift on long tasks.\n5. Keep errors: leave failed actions and error messages in the context so the model updates its beliefs.\n6. Avoid few-shot ruts: repetitive examples make the model mimic mechanically, so introduce structured diversity.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Prefix caching directly determines cost and latency; \"restorable compression\" is safer than lossy summarization. The cost is finer-grained engineering of tool management and context layout.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Why not put a timestamp in the system prompt: it breaks prefix caching.\n- File system as context is consistent with the principle \"state you can't lose lives outside the context\".\n- The technical lessons stand independently of company ownership (the Meta acquisition was halted by regulators).","L6-T6B.4.5":"OpenClaw was a breakout open-source project of early 2026: a self-hosted personal agent built by Peter Steinberger that connects to messaging channels such as WhatsApp / Telegram / Discord, can run shell commands on the local machine, manage files, and do web automation, and is extended via SKILL.md.\n\n**History and significance**: First released 2025-11, renamed Moltbot on 2026-01-27 over a trademark issue, and settled on OpenClaw on 01-30; on 02-14 the author announced he was joining OpenAI and the project moved to the OpenClaw Foundation; by early March it had about 247K GitHub stars; 2.0 shipped on 08-30. It turned \"an agent that lives in your chat app, always on, with memory\" into a mass-market product form, and was included in a source-code study of 11 harnesses. Cisco research found third-party skills performing data exfiltration and prompt injection without the user's knowledge (secondhand source).\n\n**How to design the security model**: It exposes both an inbound surface (anyone who can message it) and an outbound surface (local shell, account credentials). Inbound: sender allowlists and injection probes. Outbound: tier actions — read-only needs no confirmation, writes within a project must be reversible, and outbound messages / payments / deletions require confirmation; credentials in a separate keychain, authorized per tool; full audit logs; unknown tools denied by default. This is essentially a personal version of Claude Code auto mode's three permission tiers.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Users find confirmations annoying: use a classifier to bring the confirmation rate down to single digits, and relax on reversible actions.\n- Memory itself is an injection surface and needs provenance tagging and sanitization.\n- Third-party skills are a supply-chain risk.","L6-T6B.4.6":"Customer service is the most commercially mature agent use case. Sierra and Decagon are standalone platforms that sit on top of a company's existing ticketing system and execute branded multi-step actions (refunds, address changes, order lookups); Intercom Fin is embedded in the Intercom help desk and is the fastest to deploy.\n\n**How it works**: Four key design points: (1) write workflows as natural-language SOPs rather than hard-coded decision trees, executed by the model and constrained by guardrails; this is the essential difference between post-2024 customer service agents and the previous generation of bots; (2) actions must be reversible or confirmed, e.g., refunds tiered by amount into automatic, double-confirmed, or handed to a human, with policy enforced in code and the agent only able to \"request\"; (3) escalation to a human is part of the product, not a failure fallback, and the handoff package includes a conversation summary, actions taken, and a suggested next step; (4) evaluation uses resolution rate as the north star, with simulated users for regression.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Natural-language SOPs are flexible and easy to maintain, but key checkpoints (such as identity verification) must be enforced in code, which makes it a compromise. Customer service tasks are highly context-dependent, so they are usually a single agent + intent routing rather than multi-agent. Vendor numbers (e.g., Fin 3's 82% in-scope resolution rate) are all self-reported.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- User manipulation like \"I'm the manager, just refund it\": the policy engine does not trust conversation content.\n- Write operations carry an idempotency key to prevent double charges.\n- Use a τ-bench-style user simulator + database end-state checks, and report pass^k.","L6-T6B.4.7":"The common customer service agent architecture is a layered pipeline distilled from public material on Sierra, Decagon, Intercom Fin, and others; in interviews it is often used as the skeleton for a system design question.\n\n**How it works**: Channels (chat / email / voice) → intent and identity recognition → knowledge layer (help center + policy docs + RAG) → workflow layer (Decagon's AOP, \"Agent Operating Procedures\"; Sierra's Agent OS skills / journeys) → action layer (CRM / order / payment APIs, with permissions and confirmation) → guardrails (scope, compliance, tone, PII) → escalation to a human (with full context) → evaluation (resolution, CSAT, QA sampling, regression tests). The workflow layer is the core: SOPs written in natural language for the model to execute, rather than a hard-coded state machine.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Layering gives each layer a clear responsibility: the knowledge layer answers \"what is the policy\", the workflow layer decides \"what to do next\", and the action layer enforces permissions and limits in code. Guardrails sit in three places: input (is the intent in scope, PII redaction), output (compliant wording, no promises beyond policy), and actions (refund limits, operations requiring double confirmation); use deterministic rules wherever possible, with model judgment only as a fallback.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Be able to draw this pipeline and point out that AOP / Agent OS sit in the workflow layer.\n- The evaluation loop: offline replay of historical conversations + rubric; online resolution rate, CSAT, escalation rate, reopen rate; failure cases flow back into the test set.\n- Multilingual settings (e.g., English / Chinese / Malay in Singapore) need evaluation sliced by language.","L6-T6B.4.8":"Resolution rate is the north-star metric for customer service agents, and outcome-based pricing is the business model built around it: the vendor charges per successful resolution rather than per seat or per call.\n\n**How it works**: Intercom says Fin 3 reaches an 82% resolution rate on in-scope questions and charges about US$0.99 per successful resolution; public case studies from Sierra and Decagon claim about 80% resolution for Airtable and about 80% deflection for Duolingo respectively (all vendor-reported, pending independent verification). Evaluation uses simulated users for regression testing; after launch, combine with CSAT, escalation rate, reopen rate, and repeat-contact rate.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Outcome-based pricing aligns the vendor's incentives with \"actually solving the problem\", and reflects the general principle of \"measure outcomes, not actions\". But it invites a definitional game over \"what counts as resolved\": does it count if the user simply stops replying? If they were deflected to a help article? Is the denominator all conversations or only in-scope ones? Each of these can change the number significantly.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- When you hear \"80% resolution rate\", ask about the denominator and the definition of \"resolved\", and distinguish resolution from deflection.\n- Use reopen rate, repeat-contact rate, and CSAT to check whether resolutions are real.\n- Measuring whether the agent beats humans: randomly split similar tickets in an A/B test and compare stratified by simple / complex.","L6-T6B.4.9":"These are ten principles condensed from cases such as Claude Code, long-running task harnesses, the Cursor swarm, deep research, and customer service, ready to reuse directly in interviews, each backed by a case as evidence.\n\n**The ten principles**:\n1. Agent = Model + Harness, and the harness should shrink as models improve (mini-swe-agent reaches 74%+ on SWE-bench Verified using only bash).\n2. Context is the first bottleneck: isolate first, then compress, and reset last.\n3. State you can't lose goes outside the context (CLAUDE.md, progress files, Manus's file system as context).\n4. Determinism first: anything rules, hooks, or LSP can do isn't handed to the model.\n5. Verification must be independent and fed back (fresh-context evaluator, default-FAIL).\n6. Parallelism buys coverage, hierarchy buys coordination (multi-agent research +90.2% but about 15x tokens).\n7. Cost concentrates in a few high-value decisions, so tier your models (Cursor's planner uses a small share of tokens but about two-thirds of the cost).\n8. Three stacked permission layers: rules + sandbox + classifier.\n9. Measure outcomes, not actions (resolution rate, feature pass rate).\n10. Protocols and formats converge; differentiation lies in data and models.\n\n**Why it matters**: It turns scattered cases into transferable design judgment that you can draw on quickly when answering open-ended system design questions.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Pairing each principle with a case and a number is far more convincing than talking about principles in the abstract.\n- When asked \"how would you design it\", use these principles as the basis for decisions and state their costs.","L6-T6B.4.10":"When citing industry case studies in an interview, use one structure and three rules of discipline, so that cases serve as evidence rather than recitation and you avoid misstating where information comes from.\n\n**How it works**: The structure is \"scenario → mechanism → numbers → trade-off → transfer to your project\". For example, answering \"how do you keep an agent on track on long tasks\": first say Anthropic's harness experiments found context anxiety and self-evaluation bias (scenario); then describe separating planner / generator / evaluator, the evaluator actually testing with Playwright, and default FAIL (mechanism); give solo failing in 20 minutes for US$9 versus the full harness succeeding in 6 hours for US$200 (numbers); point out that the 20x cost is only worth it when the task exceeds solo capability (trade-off); and finally say your own project borrowed the fresh-context evaluator (transfer).\n\n**Three rules of discipline**: Distinguish primary from secondhand sources (official blogs, docs, and papers can be cited directly; for community articles say \"reportedly\"); don't claim to know closed-source internals, and when pressed, say \"public material doesn't say; here's how I would design it\"; always state the basis for any number.\n\n**Why it matters**: Interviewers are assessing judgment and honesty; overstating knowledge of closed-source systems is a common way to lose points.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Prepare a 90-second spoken version of each case, organized in the five-part structure.\n- The \"transfer\" step best shows your personal experience; don't skip it.","L6-T6B.4.11":"\"State the basis for numbers\" is a discipline for citing benchmarks and vendor data: the same metric can differ by more than ten percentage points across sources, and without labeling the source type, numbers can't be compared and are easy to pick apart under interview follow-up.\n\n**How it works**: Before citing, distinguish four kinds of sources: vendor claims, internal evals, official leaderboards, and aggregator sites. The classic example is SWE-bench Verified: in 2026-09 the top of the official leaderboard was 396/500 ≈ 79%, with the top two tied and the top 30 statistically indistinguishable pairwise; meanwhile aggregator sites and vendor self-reports show numbers above 90%. The harnesses and statistical methods differ, so they must not be mixed. The same model can differ by about 30 percentage points with a different scaffold. In addition, Verified is saturated, and an OpenAI audit found roughly 30% of SWE-bench Pro problems to be broken, so move to corrected versions such as SWE-Bench Pro Verified and DeepSWE, plus Terminal-Bench 4.0; scores must state the benchmark version and harness.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Mixing sources leads to wrong conclusions, e.g., believing a model \"leads by 10 points\" when it is just a different harness. The same goes for customer service: for resolution rate, ask about the denominator and the definition of \"resolved\".\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- When citing a score, also say: which version, which harness, and who reported it.\n- How to evaluate once Verified is saturated: corrected public benchmarks + an internal task set from your own historical PRs + process metrics such as steps, tokens, latency, and human interventions.\n- Preventing leakage in the internal task set: use PRs from after the model's knowledge cutoff and hide the original diff.","L6-T6B.4.12":"Candidates moving from research to engineering are often asked \"how does your agent differ from Claude Code\" or \"Claude Code already exists, so why build your own\". A good answer honestly compares the seven subsystems one by one and anchors differentiation in the dimensions that actually add value.\n\n**How it works**: Compare subsystems such as loop, tools, context, permissions, persistence, extensibility, and verification: the loop and tool layers are usually isomorphic; the gaps are mostly in permissions (rules only, no classifier), persistence (no fork / rewind), extensibility (no hooks or plugins), and verification (the evaluator is manual). Then describe what has already been filled in (e.g., JSONL logs, the str_replace feedback loop) and the next steps. The case for building in-house should rest on: domain (retrieval specialized for the internal codebase, domain tools and validators), control and compliance (code stays in-house, auditable, model-swappable), and cost and latency (routing to cheaper models, specialized small models), not \"yet another loop\".\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Admitting gaps actually demonstrates a systems perspective. The right approach is not to rebuild the loop but to build on an existing SDK, spend effort on the differentiating layer, and compare pass rate and cost against Claude Code on the same task set.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- The hardest gap to close is classifier-based permissions, which require data and evals.\n- If evals show the in-house agent is worse: use the off-the-shelf product directly and shrink the in-house work to a plugin / MCP tool / skill.\n- What a research background brings is the habit of designing evals and running ablations.","L6-T6B.4.13":"These are the key 2025–2026 developments related to agent case studies, useful in interviews to show you follow the frontier and to support arguments about \"harness design\".\n\n**Main developments**:\n- Two companies' long-running task harness blogs: Anthropic's effective harnesses (initializer + feature list + progress file + context reset) and the 2026-03 planner / generator / evaluator; Cursor's planner / worker / judge and the agent swarm whose model tiering cut cost by 8x.\n- The 2026-07 source-code study of 11 open-source coding agents: none use vector retrieval, none adopt a general-purpose framework, and skills adoption is 9/11, supporting \"write your own loop + deterministic retrieval\".\n- Computer use: Anthropic acquired Vercept, OSWorld 72.5%; on 2026-08-19 the computer use tool left beta and a browser toolset was added; Claude in Chrome reached GA; on OSWorld 2.0 the best model completes only 20.6%.\n- Others: Anthropic's multi-agent research system (+90.2%, about 15x tokens), Tongyi DeepResearch, the retirement of OpenAI's deep research API snapshots, the archiving of LangChain open_deep_research, and the ETH study on AGENTS.md (context files don't improve success rate overall and raise inference cost by more than 20%).\n\n**Why it matters**: Together these show that the harness matters as much as the model, protocols and formats are converging, and validator quality sets the ceiling.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- For each development, be able to say \"why it matters and how to discuss it in an interview\".\n- Distinguish verified primary sources from secondhand reports.","L6-T6B.4.14":"On 2026-03-31, version 2.1.88 of the Claude Code npm package accidentally shipped with a source map, leaking about 1,900 files and 512K lines of TypeScript source. Anthropic said it was human error in the release packaging step, not a security breach, that no customer data or credentials were involved, and promptly pulled the version.\n\n**What happened**: The subsequent DMCA takedown notice was applied by GitHub across the fork network to about 8,100 repositories (including legitimate forks of the official public repo); after Anthropic retracted it, only 1 repository and 96 named forks remained covered. Reports noted that the bundler generated source maps by default and the release had no allowlist.\n\n**Interview stance**: Start with the public facts, then state your position: you don't read or cite leaked proprietary code. Then discuss innovations only from official docs and engineering blogs, pairing each with its trade-off: a minimal main loop where the model decides when to stop; agentic search without a vector index; layered, progressively loaded context with persistence outside the window; separating \"request\" from \"enforcement\" via PreToolUse hooks; and auto mode's injection probes + two-stage classifier (0.4% false positives, 17% false negatives).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: This question tests technical judgment and professional ethics at the same time. The engineering lesson is to audit the actual list of published files in CI (an allowlist); security must not rely on \"the other side can't see the implementation\".\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Don't comment on rumored \"unreleased features in the leaked code\"; go by official releases.\n- If asked \"would you copy it\": don't rebuild the loop; put differentiation in domain, compliance, and cost.","L6-T6B.4.15":"The mini coding agent is a deliberately minimal reference implementation: using only three tools, bash, read_file, and str_replace, it implements the minimal form of each of the seven subsystems so they can be swapped and upgraded one at a time. It is the best hands-on exercise for understanding the internals of a coding agent.\n\n**How it works**: The main loop is driven by `stop_reason` and ends whenever it is not `tool_use`; the permission layer blocks dangerous bash commands with deterministic rules (equivalent to a PreToolUse hook); `str_replace` requires old_string to appear exactly once, otherwise it returns an ERROR so the model corrects itself, closing the edit-format feedback loop; when context gets too long, it summarizes the first 70% while keeping tool_use / tool_result pairs intact; sessions are written to JSONL to support resume. Even without the SDK installed, `--self-test` runs a self-check of the permission rules (path escapes and bash writes outside the allowed area are blocked).\n\n**Upgrade path**: Add PostToolUse-style automatic lint / test hooks; run bash inside Docker or sandbox-exec; add a read-only evaluator sub-agent that scores the final diff against a default-FAIL standard; JSONL support for resume / fork; regress on 10 SWE-bench Lite instances, recording success rate, average turns, and token cost, and write an ablation report (how much drops when the evaluator / compaction is removed).\n\n**Why it matters**: It turns the \"seven subsystems\" from concepts into demonstrable code, and provides the empirical basis for answering \"how does your agent differ from Claude Code\".\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Explain why str_replace requires a unique match.\n- Use ablation data to show the value of each component.","L6-T6B.5.1":"OpenAI Deep Research (since 2025-02) uses an RL-trained reasoning model that decides end to end when to search, read, and synthesize. It represents one route for deep research: training the harness's planning logic into the model instead of orchestrating an external planner / searcher.\n\n**How it works**: Unlike Anthropic's orchestrator–worker multi-agent architecture, it relies on a single reinforcement-learned model that autonomously calls search and browsing tools in one loop, with decisions such as planning and when to stop internalized by the model. In 2025-07 it was merged, together with Operator, into ChatGPT agent. On the API side, the `o3-deep-research` and `o4-mini-deep-research` snapshots were announced for deprecation on 2026-04-22 and retired on 2026-07-23; the official replacement is a general-purpose model (the deprecations page lists `gpt-5.6-sol`), meaning deep research capability is now handled by a general model + the web search tool.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: The training route gives fast inference and a thin harness, but data and RL infrastructure are expensive and controllability is weak; the harness route iterates fast, is interpretable, and allows swapping models. The snapshot retirement is also a textbook case of vendor deprecation risk: when depending on hosted APIs, the interface layer must be replaceable.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- For \"train an agent model vs build a harness\", be able to cite OpenAI Deep Research and Tongyi DeepResearch.\n- Specialized capabilities gradually folding into general models + tools is a 2026 trend.","L6-T6B.5.2":"Gemini Deep Research / Deep Research Max entered the Gemini API's Interactions API as a preview in 2026-04 (model names carry `preview-04-2026`), marking deep research's shift from a consumer product to an orchestratable API component.\n\n**How it works**: It supports asynchronous long-running execution, reading private files, and generating charts, can connect to your own data sources via MCP, and returns cited reports. Claims that it is based on Gemini 3.1 Pro and runs up to 160 searches come from secondhand reports and are not listed on the official docs page; they are unverified and should be labeled as such when cited.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: It suits teams that don't want to maintain a planner / searcher and just want to call it. Reasons to build your own: you need control over source allowlists and citation format, need to connect internal retrieval systems, need fine-grained budget scheduling, or data must not leave your environment. A common compromise is to use the hosted API for the public-web part and an in-house component for internal knowledge, with a unified citation layer at the end. Evaluate both on the same question set with rubric + citation spot-checks + cost and latency.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Selection criteria for \"hosted deep research API vs in-house\": controllability, compliance, cost predictability, auditable citations.\n- Vendor deprecation risk: OpenAI retiring its deep research snapshots is an example; the interface layer must be replaceable.\n- Distinguish verified information from unverified secondhand numbers.","L6-T6B.5.3":"Anthropic Research and Perplexity Deep Research are two representative deep research product designs: the former emphasizes buying coverage with parallel multi-agent work, the latter emphasizes the speed of iterative retrieval.\n\n**How it works**: Anthropic Research uses an orchestrator–worker architecture: the lead agent explicitly assigns tasks to subagents (objective, output format, tool and source guidance, boundaries) and sets the number of subagents and calls by complexity; each subagent searches in parallel with its own context and returns compressed findings, and finally a citation agent does sentence-level attribution. On an internal eval it scored 90.2% higher than a single-agent Opus 4, but used about 15x the tokens of a normal chat. Perplexity Deep Research is an iterative planner → retriever → synthesizer retrieval loop that emphasizes speed and number of sources.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Multi-agent suits parallelizable breadth questions where the information exceeds a single context, at the cost of multiplied tokens and coordination complexity; the iterative loop is cheaper and faster, but a single context limits coverage. Anthropic's engineering blog is the go-to source for discussing \"multi-agent token economics\" in interviews.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Be able to draw both architectures and explain where each fits.\n- Delegation instructions must be specific, otherwise the model spawns too many sub-agents for simple questions or duplicates work.","L6-T6B.5.4":"LangChain open_deep_research is an open-source deep research reference implementation with configurable models, search backends, and MCP; its structure is the classic planner → parallel searchers → evidence aggregation → section-by-section writing architecture.\n\n**Key points**: The repository was archived as read-only on 2026-08-21, showing how short the lifecycle of an \"open-source reference implementation\" can be; but reading its code is still a shortcut to understanding deep research architecture: you can see how sub-question decomposition, parallel research units, result compression, and report generation are chained together, and how models and search tools are swapped via configuration.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: For teams wanting to build their own deep research, it provides a runnable skeleton, good as a baseline and for learning; but it is no longer maintained after archiving, so production use requires adding budget control, a shared visited set, citation verification, prompt injection defenses, and so on. It is also an example of \"frameworks / reference implementations iterate fast, so keep the interface layer replaceable\".\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- As a hands-on exercise, use it as a reference to build a mini deep research: planner + 3–5 parallel searchers + evidence pool + section-by-section writing + independent citation verification, with a triple budget on rounds / searches / tokens.\n- Acceptance criteria, e.g., randomly sample 20 citations for manual checking with a support rate ≥ 90%, and plot a \"budget vs quality\" curve.","L6-T6B.5.5":"Tongyi DeepResearch is an agent model whose technical report Alibaba published in 2025-10: an MoE with 30.5B total parameters and 3.3B activated per token, trained end to end with \"agentic mid-training + agentic post-training\". It represents the route of \"training an agent model rather than building a harness\".\n\n**How it works**: After standard pretraining, an agentic mid-training stage exposes the model early to large volumes of tool-use and multi-step reasoning trajectories, followed by agentic post-training (including reinforcement learning) for further optimization; data synthesis is fully automated, and the inference-time architecture is plain ReAct. It reaches open-source SOTA on deep research benchmarks such as Humanity's Last Exam, BrowseComp, BrowseComp-ZH, and xbench-DeepSearch.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: It shows that planning and tool-use abilities can be internalized into a mid-sized model, making the harness thin, inference fast, and deployment cheap thanks to few active parameters. The costs are expensive data synthesis and RL infrastructure, and retraining when the task distribution changes. By contrast, the harness route iterates quickly and allows swapping models; the two are complementary.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Choosing: application teams should do the harness first and training later; training is worth it only for platform teams, once the harness is stable, the bottleneck is latency or cost, and a large volume of real trajectories has accumulated.\n- Training data sources: your own agent trajectories + synthetic environments.\n- Get the numbers right: 30.5B total, 3.3B active.","L6-T6B.5.6":"When to stop is the core control problem of multi-round retrieval in deep research: stop too early and you converge prematurely and miss key information; stop too late and you spin and burn tokens. The answer: hard budgets as a backstop + soft signals for normal convergence + thresholds calibrated with data.\n\n**How it works**: Hard budgets are insurance: maximum rounds, number of searches, total tokens, and wall-clock timeout, with spent / remaining fed to the planner. Soft signals: (1) coverage: the planner splits the question into a checklist of sub-questions, and each counts as done only when supported by at least k independent sources; (2) marginal gain: stop when the new facts / new sources added in the latest round fall below a threshold (e.g., fewer than 2 useful claims in two consecutive rounds); (3) conflicts: when contradictory evidence exists, allow extra searches, but with a cap. The planner makes the call in each round's review.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Thresholds shouldn't be set by gut feel; sweep a \"budget vs quality\" curve on a batch of labeled questions to find the knee. Anthropic reports that tokens explain about 80% of performance variance, meaning quality rises with budget but with diminishing returns; then pick the operating point under cost constraints and tier by question difficulty, searching less for simple questions.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Detecting premature convergence: sample-check for missing sub-questions, or use a large-budget reference run as an oracle to measure the recall gap.\n- Diagnosing spinning: is it repeating the same query, or missing a shared visited set? Add deduplication and new-information-gain checks.\n- Recalibrate thresholds after model upgrades.","L6-T6B.5.7":"Claude in Chrome, ChatGPT agent, and Gemini 2.5 Computer Use are major vendors' flagship computer-use / browser agent products; they differ mainly in observation method and runtime environment.\n\n**Each vendor's approach**: Anthropic's computer use relies on screenshots + coordinates and targets general desktops; Claude in Chrome gets both page structure and screenshots inside the browser, a hybrid observation approach. OpenAI merged Operator (2025-01) into ChatGPT agent (2025-07), folding in deep research; it runs in a cloud VM + browser + terminal + connectors, pauses at sensitive steps such as login and payment to hand control back to the user, and is a commercial example of \"general agent = union of coding + research + computer use\". Google released the Gemini 2.5 Computer Use API in 2025-10; the open-source Browser Use takes the DOM-extraction + optional-screenshot route and is a common base for building your own browser agent.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: A cloud virtual browser gives good isolation and never touches the user's main session, but can't directly use the user's logged-in state; running in the user's local browser is smoother but carries greater injection and credential risk, requiring stronger confirmation and classifiers.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Compare which of the three observation approaches (screenshot, DOM, hybrid) each product chooses.\n- Handing sensitive steps back to the user is the standard human-in-the-loop practice.\n- All three vendors offer hosted agent harness / browser APIs.","L6-T6B.5.8":"2026 was the year computer use went from \"usable\" to \"approaching human level\", and several milestones are often used to illustrate the pace of progress and the remaining bottlenecks.\n\n**Main milestones**: On 2026-02-26 Anthropic acquired Vercept, which focuses on perception and software interaction; the announcement said the Sonnet series rose on OSWorld from under 15% at the end of 2024 to 72.5% (Sonnet 4.6), approaching human level. Multiple sources reported that Claude in Chrome became generally available on 2026-08-26 for paid Pro / Max / Team / Enterprise plans (desktop Chrome only). On the API side, the computer use tool left beta on 2026-08-19 with `computer_toolset_20260801` (batched actions, zoom on by default), plus a new `browser_toolset_20260801` that works only within the browser viewport and reads the accessibility tree. OpenAI's GPT-5.4 has a built-in `computer` tool in the Responses API, and the Agents API added hosted-browser computer use on 2026-09-29.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: The key progress came from perception and grounding; but on OSWorld 2.0 (108 long-horizon workflows, about 318 tool calls on average), the best model, Opus 4.8, completes only 20.6%, with failures concentrated in constraint tracking, losing information midway, not verifying, and guessing instead of asking.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- When citing 72.5%, note that the benchmark is (original) OSWorld; long-horizon tasks remain far from solved.\n- The browser toolset reads the a11y tree, reflecting the \"hybrid observation\" trend.","L6-T6B.5.9":"Browser agent security defenses target prompt injection: web pages are untrusted input, and malicious content can turn the agent into a tool that \"places orders / sends emails on the user's behalf\", so security is the first design constraint for browser agents.\n\n**How it works**:\n1. Content as data: pass web content to the model as data and state in the system prompt that instructions within it are not to be executed. This is only the first layer and cannot be relied on alone.\n2. Human confirmation for sensitive actions: payments, sending, deleting, logging in, and granting authorization require user confirmation.\n3. Behavior classifier: an independent classifier checks whether the action sequence deviates from user intent. You can reuse the Claude Code auto mode idea of \"strip tool content before judging\": don't send web content to the classifier, only actions and user intent, so the classifier itself can't be injected.\n4. Domain allowlists and session isolation: a separate browser profile that doesn't share the credentials of the user's main session.\n5. A complete action log for after-the-fact auditing.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Layers are stacked because any single layer can be bypassed; the cost is that confirmations interrupt the experience, so relax them for reversible, low-risk actions.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- User asks to \"complete purchases automatically\": preset amount limits and a merchant allowlist; anything beyond still needs confirmation.\n- What if the classifier gets injected: give it only actions and intent, never the raw page text.","L6-T6B.5.10":"Observation pruning for large DOMs solves the context explosion problem in browser agents: the raw HTML of an e-commerce page can easily run to hundreds of thousands of tokens, while decisions only need the \"visible and interactive\" elements, so never pass the raw DOM straight to the model.\n\n**How it works**: (1) Prune at the observation layer: convert to an accessibility tree, keep only visible, interactive elements and a little context text, and give each element a stable ref; (2) expand on demand: by default provide only the page skeleton (landmarks, headings, forms); when the model needs a region it calls `read(ref)` or fetches page by page, and long lists show only the first N items plus \"M more\"; (3) decay past observations: keep the full tree only for the last one or two steps and replace earlier ones with one-line action summaries (what was done, URL, result), with the originals written to disk for lookup (Manus's file system as context); (4) fall back to screenshots when the tree doesn't fit or there is a Canvas. The more fundamental route is WebMCP (origin trial since Chrome 149), where sites register structured tools themselves and the agent calls them directly instead of guessing the UI.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Over-pruning can drop the target element, which needs a dedicated metric; iframes / Shadow DOM must be explicitly expanded with path-qualified refs and are the main source of incomplete coverage in tree-based approaches.\n\n**Interview notes**:\n- Diagnosing \"can't find the button\": replay trajectories and measure the share of cases where the target element was pruned.\n- Quantifying the benefit: compare average tokens per step, total cost, success rate, and steps; expect tokens to drop by an order of magnitude without a drop in success rate.","L6-T6C.1.1":"A Forward Deployed Engineer (FDE) is an engineering role embedded with a single customer, solving its critical business problems with the company's platform plus open-source tools, and bringing field findings back to the product. The question it answers: LLMs are very capable, but every enterprise's data, permissions, and processes differ, so \"plugging into the enterprise\" needs people who can both write code and talk to customers.\n\n**Origins and positioning**: The term comes from Palantir. Its engineering roles split into Dev (product SWE, \"one capability, many customers\") and Delta (FDE, \"one customer, many capabilities\"); the non-coding role paired with Delta is called Deployment Strategist (Echo, secondhand source) and manages stakeholders and adoption. In 2025–2026 OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google Cloud have all been expanding hiring: OpenAI writes eval-driven product feedback into its success criteria, Anthropic's deliverables explicitly include MCP servers, and Google Cloud announced in 2026-05 a new AI organization within GTM with more FDE hiring.\n\n**How it differs from adjacent roles**: A Solutions Architect delivers architecture designs; a Customer Engineer handles pre-sales solutions and PoCs, measured on winning deals; consulting / PSO delivers reports or one-off projects; a product SWE builds general features. An FDE delivers **code that runs long-term in the customer's environment**, and success is measured by customer business outcomes + production adoption + product feedback. The \"field finding → productization\" loop is the reason the role exists.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One-line analogy: the SA designs the bridge, the consultant advises where to build it, the product engineer improves the materials, and the FDE builds the bridge with the customer, confirms cars can actually cross it, and tells the materials team what broke.\n- If asked \"how is this different from consulting\": emphasize delivering long-running software and the product feedback loop.","L6-T6C.1.2":"The five builder-consultant principles are a working method for FDEs that applies both on site and in interviews, preventing the pattern of \"draw the architecture first, build a demo, and never land it\".\n\n**The five principles**\n1. **Problem first, architecture second**: in the first 15 minutes of an interview, or the first week on site, only clarify who uses it, how it's done today, the cost of getting it wrong, and what counts as success; multiple interview reports show that people who draw the architecture first perform worse.\n2. **Prove value by the shortest path, then productionize**: the prototype uses a small slice of real data and a small group of real users, but from day one maintain a technical debt register listing the shortcuts that must be paid off before the pilot.\n3. **It's only a standard if it can go into CI**: \"accuracy should be high\" doesn't count; \"task success rate Wilson lower bound ≥ 0.85 on golden set v3, zero PII leaks\" does.\n4. **It's only delivered if the customer can maintain it**: the code lives in the customer's repo, runs in the customer's CI, the customer's engineers can change prompts and tools on their own, and there is a runbook.\n5. **Log every piece of field friction**: the friction log is the raw material for product feedback and reusable modules.\n\n**Why it matters**: The five map to five stages—discovery, prototype, eval gates, handover, and product catalyst—and condense the whole methodology; they turn \"consultant-style advice\" into verifiable, hand-over-able engineering delivery.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Opening a scenario question by asking clarifying questions and restating them is a direct demonstration of principle 1.\n- When asked \"what counts as success\", give a measurable, CI-ready standard in the format of principle 3.","L6-T6C.1.3":"The lifecycle and stage gates move an AI project from idea to customer self-operation, with **verifiable** exit criteria at each step, avoiding \"great demo that never goes live\".\n\n**Process**: Discovery (1–2 weeks) → use-case selection and ROI hypotheses → data readiness assessment → [Gate 0] → Prototype (1–3 weeks) → [Gate 1] → Pilot (4–8 weeks) → [Gate 2] → Production (canary to full rollout) → [Gate 3] → Handover / Scale; online metrics then feed back into use-case selection. Durations are only typical orders of magnitude.\n- **Gate 0, project approval**: a one-page scope document, success metrics and a baseline measurement plan, data access requests submitted, risk tiering done; signed off by the business sponsor and the customer's technical lead.\n- **Gate 1, prototype → pilot**: golden set v1 (50–200 items) reaches the feasibility bar, and at least one critical integration path works end to end.\n- **Gate 2, pilot → production**: on a small group of real users (e.g., 5–10% of traffic), business metrics meet the target versus baseline, regression gates are in CI, monitoring and runbooks are ready, and security review has passed.\n- **Gate 3, handover**: SLOs are stable for several weeks, and the customer team has independently made a prompt / tool change that passed the gates.\n\n**Trade-offs**: In regulated industries, security reviews and data approvals often take longer than development, so submit requests in the first week of Discovery to run them in parallel with the prototype. \"Users are satisfied\" can't be an exit criterion; \"the pilot group's handling time is X% lower than the control group's and its QA error rate is no higher than the control's\" can.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked about timelines, point out that security review is on the critical path and give a plan for parallelizing it.\n- For each gate, state who signs off and on what evidence it passes.","L6-T6C.1.4":"The discovery question checklist is the structured set of questions an FDE uses during scoping (usually 10–15 minutes in an interview) to understand business value, process, constraints, and risks before designing anything.\n\n**How to ask**: Go in the order \"business → users and process → success criteria → data and systems → security and compliance → constraints\", pick 1–2 key questions per category, and **restate your understanding after each category** rather than asking everything in one breath. Representative questions:\n- Business: six months from now, what result counts as success—lower cost, higher revenue, lower risk, or customer experience? Who owns this KPI, and what is it today? (Find the sponsor and the baseline.)\n- Process: how is it done step by step today, where is the most time spent, where do errors happen? Which steps are judgment calls and which follow rules? (Rule-based steps use code; only judgment steps are LLM candidates.)\n- Success criteria: what is the cost of a wrong answer versus no answer? How are human experts evaluated today? (Existing QA standards are the starting point for the rubric.)\n- Data and systems: where is the data, who owns it, how fresh must it be? Are there historical samples with correct outcomes? Are there actions that must never happen automatically?\n- Security and compliance: what data classifications and residency requirements apply? How long does security review take?\n- Organization and constraints: who decides, who can veto? What drives the deadline? What has been tried before, and why didn't it work?\n\n**Must-ask closing question**: \"If you only had four weeks, which one workflow would you most want working end to end?\" Use it to narrow down to an MVP together with the customer.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When the interviewer plays the CTO, question quality and restating are themselves scored.\n- The \"cost of errors\" question directly determines the degree of automation and the thresholds, so ask it early.","L6-T6C.1.5":"Use-case selection helps the customer pick **which one to do first** out of a dozen ideas, and is an important part of an FDE's value.\n\n**How it works**: Score each of five dimensions from 1 to 5:\n- **Value**: volume × savings per item × labor cost, or revenue / risk impact (from a few dozen items a month → tens of thousands a month with a clear KPI);\n- **Data readiness**: accessibility, quality, permission metadata, historical labels;\n- **Integration complexity**: number of systems to read from and write to, whether APIs exist (mainframes, no APIs, and cross-department are the hardest);\n- **Risk**: customer-facing, regulated, irreversible, cost of errors (automatic loan disbursement, automatic refunds, and medical advice are the highest);\n- **Sponsor**: has budget, a KPI, and time.\nThe companion script use_case_scorer.py's quadrant() returns \"redesign first, add human approval\" for \"high risk + high automation\" instead of a score.\n\n**When not to use an LLM** (saying this proactively earns points): when the rules are fully deterministic and inputs are structured, use a rules engine or plain code; when 100% correctness is required and there is no human review, redesign the process first or limit the LLM to \"drafting\"; when data volume is tiny or the task rarely occurs; when the bottleneck is really in process or organization.\n\n**Simplified selection tree**: deterministic rules → code; needs private knowledge that changes fast / needs citations → RAG; needs multi-step operations on external systems → agent + tools / MCP with human approval; otherwise prompt + structured output; consider fine-tuning such as LoRA only when format or terminology errors are consistent and you have hundreds of high-quality samples.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"should this requirement use an LLM\", judge first by how rule-based it is, the correctness requirement, and whether there is a review step.","L6-T6C.1.6":"Converging fuzzy requirements is a frequent FDE interview topic: the customer says \"AI-powered compliance\" or \"all five departments want agents\". The requirement itself is vague, and the test is whether you can break it into a concrete, measurable, deliverable first step.\n\n**How it works**\n- **First pin down which thing it is**: \"compliance\" could be five different projects: AML and transaction monitoring, communications surveillance, regulatory reporting, or employee policy Q&A.\n- **Ask about costs and expectations**: who does it, how many cases a month, the cost of missed detections versus false positives (asymmetric); what regulators expect to see—human sign-off, explainability, an audit trail; where the data is and whether it can leave the country; whether there are labeled historical cases to build a golden set.\n- **Narrow to a small MVP**: e.g., \"draft case summaries for analysts\", using current review time and error rate as the baseline, with the compliance team setting sign-off rules from day one.\n- Cross-department scenarios (engineering, sales, customer service, legal, and supply chain at a car company): don't draw a platform architecture first; run short discoveries per department, score by value, data readiness, integration, and risk, and pick one or two use cases that also validate the shared foundation (identity, data access, evals, observability), such as warranty claim triage in customer service; the other departments go on the roadmap.\n\n**Trade-offs**: The highest-value department isn't necessarily first—when data and risk bars are high, start with a more ready use case to build trust and the platform. Legal has a high cost of errors, suits the second wave, and should start with \"clause retrieval and comparison\" rather than \"automatically judging risk\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- If pressed \"they want the system to decide directly\": keep decision rights with humans, present evidence and inference separately, and grant autonomy based on pilot data.\n- If asked \"which regulator\": go by the customer compliance team's list; don't quote rules from memory.","L6-T6C.1.7":"ROI estimation is one of the most heavily probed topics in FDE interviews. The core is getting executives to believe the number: it has a baseline, includes human costs, comes as a range, and gets reviewed.\n\n**Four steps**\n1. **Measure the baseline first**: from existing system logs (handling time, human handoff rate, error rate) or sampled timing; ROI without a baseline is a guess.\n2. **Subtract human review in the formula**:\n   - Monthly gross value = monthly volume × adoption rate × [minutes saved per item − (1 − automation share) × review minutes per item] / 60 × fully loaded labor cost/hour\n   - Monthly net benefit = gross value − running cost − maintenance cost; payback period = one-time investment / monthly net benefit; first-year ROI = (12 × monthly net benefit − one-time investment) / one-time investment\n3. **Give a range**: pessimistic / base / optimistic; use_case_scorer.py's pessimistic case multiplies adoption rate by 0.6, time saved by 0.7, running cost by 2, and build cost by 1.5. A project is solid only if even the pessimistic case pays back.\n4. **Review at days 30 and 90 after launch**, writing down the reasons for any differences.\n\n**Trade-offs**: adoption_rate is a separate term because \"launched but nobody uses it\" is the most common reason ROI fails to materialize. In the example output, two use cases pay back in about 3 months in the base case but take over 20 months in the pessimistic case, showing that ROI is very sensitive to adoption rate and time saved, so the pilot's top priority is measuring those two numbers accurately; \"automatic refunds\" has low per-item value, expensive review, and high risk, and should be recast as \"agent drafts + human one-click approval\" before recalculating.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Proactively mentioning review cost and adoption rate is more credible than just quoting \"hours saved\".\n- First-year ROI is undefined when the one-time investment is 0; the code must handle that edge case.","L6-T6C.1.8":"ROI is not just cost reduction: for many AI use cases the main value is not labor savings but revenue growth, risk reduction, compliance, or employee experience. Counting only hours saved underestimates it, or can't measure it at all.\n\n**Sources of value**\n- Revenue growth: e.g., conversion uplift from a shopping assistant;\n- Risk reduction: e.g., lower losses from missed fraud;\n- Compliance: e.g., shorter audit preparation time;\n- Employee experience: e.g., lower attrition.\n\n**How to quantify**: This kind of value is harder to quantify. The approach is to find **KPIs the customer already tracks** and measure causality with A/B (random splits by agent, store, or time slot); if A/B isn't possible, do a before-after comparison controlling for seasonality. At the same time, explicitly write the \"attribution assumptions\" into the ROI document so decision-makers know what premises the number rests on. Examples from scenarios: shopping-assistant value = (A/B conversion difference × session volume × average order value × gross margin) − cost; in manufacturing the main ROI item is downtime losses; the public sector cares more about wait time, first-response time, and consistency of answers, so turn these non-monetary metrics into KPIs.\n\n**Trade-offs**: Secondary benefits (such as satisfaction from overnight service coverage at a bank) should be listed separately and kept out of the main formula to avoid overestimation; using the customer's own KPIs also makes results easier to accept.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"this project doesn't save headcount, so how do you calculate ROI\": describe the revenue / risk-reduction path, the A/B design, and the attribution assumptions.\n- Proactively distinguish primary from secondary benefits.","L6-T6C.1.9":"A data readiness assessment determines before project approval whether \"the data can support this use case\", because data problems often stall projects earlier than model problems.\n\n**Eight dimensions, each scored 0–3, with red lines**\n- **Availability**: can it be read programmatically (red line: only in personal mailboxes, on paper, or in screenshots);\n- **Access**: under what identity the FDE and the agent read it, and how long approval takes (red line: only via shared or personal accounts);\n- **Quality**: proportion of missing, duplicate, stale, or badly formatted data (red line: key fields widely missing and unrecoverable);\n- **Freshness**: how fresh the business needs it, and the sync lag (red line: needs real time but the source system only exports nightly);\n- **Permission metadata**: whether document- / row-level ACLs can travel with the data (red line: retrieval results can't be filtered by user permissions);\n- **Labels and benchmarks**: whether there are historical samples with correct answers (red line: no SME willing to label);\n- **Sensitive data**: distribution of PII, financial, and health information and whether it can be de-identified;\n- **Lineage and ownership**: who is accountable for correctness (red line: no owner, and the upstream schema can change at any time).\n\n**Trade-offs**: A low total score doesn't mean it can't be done; it means **the prototype's scope must shrink to the slice where the data is good**, with fixing the data listed as pre-pilot work; any red line must be resolved first. ACL metadata is often overlooked, but it determines whether RAG can filter by user permissions, making it a security issue rather than a quality issue.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"the data is terrible, do we still do it\": say narrow the scope + a data remediation plan, rather than a flat no.\n- Point out that permission metadata and identity/access are the two items most likely to delay the timeline.","L6-T6C.1.10":"A metric tree breaks business goals down layer by layer into metrics an FDE can own and measure; scope management keeps the project from being dragged down by constantly added requirements during delivery.\n\n**Metric tree**: Business KPIs (cost per item, handling time, human handoff rate, NPS, fraud losses) → operational metrics (automatic completion rate, review pass rate, rework rate, adoption rate) → quality metrics (task success rate, factuality, tool-call correctness, correct refusal rate) → system metrics (p95 latency, availability, cost-per-request); plus **guardrail metrics** (PII leaks, policy-violating actions, complaint rate) that must never get worse and trigger a rollback when breached. Each layer has at least one metric with a baseline and a target; targets written as \"relative to baseline\" are easier to agree on; review leading indicators weekly and lagging indicators monthly; A/B whenever possible.\n\n**Scope management**: Creep comes from customers adding requests in passing, sales over-promising to win the deal, and your own perfectionism. Tools include:\n- A one-page scope document (goals, users, in / out of scope, success criteria, dependencies, risks, timeline, signed by both sides);\n- A change log (requester, value, cost, impact on timeline, decision);\n- A parking lot (good ideas not in this phase, written down so the customer feels heard);\n- Timeboxing (when the prototype's time is up, review it against Gate 1; if it can't make it, cut scope rather than extend).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Say no by framing a \"trade\" rather than \"no\": adding it now delays us two weeks; should it go to phase two, or replace something currently in scope?","L6-T6C.2.1":"Connective tissue is the layer that connects the agent to the enterprise's existing systems: identity, networking, APIs, data replication, and tool wrapping. It often determines timelines and success more than the model itself.\n\n**Reference architecture**: Users enter via SSO into a Cloud Run frontend → an ADK agent on Agent Runtime → calls Gemini at a regional endpoint; tool calls go through Agent Gateway (policy + Model Armor) to a custom MCP server on Cloud Run, then over Interconnect / VPN / PSC to the on-prem Apigee facade and legacy systems; legacy databases flow into BigQuery via replication / CDC; all traces go to Cloud Trace, and everything sits inside a VPC Service Controls perimeter.\n\n**Choosing integration patterns**\n- **API facade** (Apigee): use when REST / SOAP services already exist; APIs can be exposed as MCP tools. Pitfalls: old APIs are not idempotent, lack pagination, and have non-standard error codes, so wrap them.\n- **Custom MCP server**: design \"intent-level\" tools for the agent; dedicated tools are easier to permission and audit than a catch-all `run_sql`.\n- **Event-driven** (Pub/Sub, Eventarc): handle duplicate delivery with idempotency.\n- **CDC replication to the warehouse**: for analytics and RAG corpora, without connecting directly to production databases; permissions must move with the data.\n- **Read replicas**: for low-latency queries; don't let the agent hit the primary database.\n- **UI automation (RPA / computer use)**: a last resort when there's no API at all; brittle and hard to audit, use only as a stopgap with a written exit plan.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"what if the legacy system has no API\": first look for existing business interfaces or a replication path; UI automation comes last.\n- Explain the tool-granularity trade-off: intent-level dedicated tools vs general-purpose tools.","L6-T6C.2.2":"Knowledge Catalog is Google Cloud's unified data catalog and governance service, formerly named Dataplex Universal Catalog and renamed on 2026-04-10. It handles metadata, discovery, and governance for data assets, and in agent scenarios often serves as the source of \"semantics and metadata\".\n\n**Key points**\n- The rename is branding only: **API, client library, and IAM names are unchanged**, so existing code and permission configurations are unaffected; SAP BDC-related blog posts already use the new name.\n- Its place in agent architecture: the semantic layer of a data analysis agent can combine metric definitions, verified queries, and Knowledge Catalog metadata to help the model understand tables and fields; the open-source MCP Toolbox for Databases also lists Knowledge Catalog (formerly Dataplex) as a supported source, letting agents query catalog metadata through MCP tools.\n- In integration choices, it pairs with the replication / CDC-to-BigQuery pattern: once data is in the warehouse, the catalog handles discovery, lineage, and governance, filling the gap of \"who owns this data, and who gets notified when it changes\".\n\n**Why it matters**: Google Cloud renamed many products in 2026 (Vertex AI → Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, Agent Engine → Agent Runtime, etc.), and interviewers will check whether you keep up with the current product lineup. Using the new name shows you're up to date; adding the old name avoids confusion.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Phrasing: \"Knowledge Catalog, formerly Dataplex Universal Catalog\".\n- If asked about the impact of the rename: API, client library, and IAM names are unchanged, so migration cost is zero.","L6-T6C.2.3":"The four kinds of boundaries are the four dimensions where FDEs most often stumble when connecting agents to regulated enterprises: identity, network, data residency, and change processes. If any one of them is mishandled, the project gets stuck in security review or before launch.\n\n**Identity**: Distinguish **user delegation** (the agent acts on behalf of an employee with that employee's permissions, OAuth 3LO, requires consent) from **the agent's own identity** (batch jobs use 2LO / service accounts, with least privilege); mixing them is the most common source of privilege escalation. Enterprise SSO is connected via identity federation; the MCP spec explicitly forbids token passthrough. Pitfall: the customer hands over an \"all-powerful\" service account to get things running—acceptable for a prototype, but log it as tech debt and replace it with per-tool least privilege before Gate 2.\n\n**Network**: VPC Service Controls put BigQuery, Cloud Storage, and Agent Runtime inside the same perimeter to prevent exfiltration; Interconnect / VPN and Private Service Connect let cloud MCP servers reach on-prem APIs without exposure to the public internet. Pitfalls: firewall change windows come once every week or two, and enterprise TLS-intercepting proxies cause SDK certificate errors, so run an end-to-end connectivity smoke test in the first week.\n\n**Data residency**: Consider the location of data at rest and the location of ML processing separately; the global endpoint provides no residency guarantee.\n\n**Change processes**: Architecture review, security review, privacy impact assessment, vendor risk assessment, CAB. Pitfall: two weeks of development, two months of review; the remedy is to get the review templates at Gate 0, invite the security team to the prototype demo, and treat review comments as requirements.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"whose identity does the agent use\": distinguish delegation from its own identity by scenario.","L6-T6C.2.4":"In Google Cloud generative AI, data residency is split into two things: **where data at rest is stored** and **where ML processing happens**. Failing to separate them leads to mistakes in compliance review.\n\n**Rules**\n- Data at rest stays in the location you chose when creating the resource, regardless of which endpoint you call.\n- The ML processing location is determined by the endpoint: regional / multi-regional endpoints restrict inference to the corresponding jurisdiction, while **the global endpoint provides no residency guarantee** and may process in any region.\n- Whether Singapore (asia-southeast1) is supported must be **checked model by model**; new models often launch on global or in the US / EU first.\n- A concrete pitfall: the v1.6 series (release candidate) of Document AI's custom extractor notes that it uses the Gemini global endpoint, and requests to US / EU endpoints may also be routed anywhere in the world. Looking only at the product name without reading the version notes will burn you.\n\n**How to do it**: Build a table of \"component × region × processing location × stage (GA / Preview)\", including logs and traces since they count as data too, and have the customer's compliance team sign it. If a needed model isn't available in-region, give the customer three options to choose from: use an in-region model and accept the quality gap; self-host an open model (such as Gemma) in-region; or have legal approve processing in a neighboring region.\n\n**Why it matters**: Residency constraints often directly determine model choice; and residency is not the same as cross-border transfer, so the legal interpretation must come from the customer's legal team.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- For a question about \"processing within Singapore\", first explain the two-part split, then the per-model check and the sign-off table.\n- Don't recommend workarounds; if necessary, minimize data and send out only non-personal content.","L6-T6C.2.5":"Not getting production credentials is everyday life for an FDE and a common interview question: the customer's security team won't grant production access, so how do you still move the project forward? The core attitude is **accept the constraint and find another path**, rather than waiting or working around it.\n\n**Five approaches**\n1. **Change the data and how things run**: use de-identified datasets or synthetic data, or have the customer run your code in their own environment—you provide containers and scripts, they execute them and send back metrics.\n2. **Contract tests**: write mocks and contract tests from the interface docs, so that when the real interface opens up, you only need to switch configuration.\n3. **Record / replay**: ask the customer to record real requests and responses in a test environment (de-identified) for offline evaluation.\n4. **Split access requests into small pieces**: don't ask for \"production read/write\" all at once; first ask for \"read-only on the test environment\", then \"read-only on one production table\"; small requests are easier to approve.\n5. **Make the security team co-designers**: ask them to define the agent's permission boundaries and audit requirements, which often speeds up approval.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: For regulated customers, security review often takes longer than development, and these approaches let development and approval run in parallel. The cost is that offline data may differ from the real distribution, so the pilot stage still needs retesting on real data; compromises made during the prototype go into the tech-debt register.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Show respect for the security team in your answer, treating their requirements as requirements rather than obstacles.\n- Mentioning contract tests and record/replay shows you have engineering tools, not just communication.","L6-T6C.2.6":"Layered evaluation and golden sets are the foundation of the production excellence an FDE builds on site: layered tests cover everything from functions to real traffic, and a trustworthy labeled set serves as the basis for releases.\n\n**Five layers of evaluation**\n- Unit: tool functions, parsers, permission filtering, run on every commit;\n- Component: retrieval recall (recall@k), tool selection, structured output, trajectory matching;\n- End to end: golden set + a calibrated LLM judge + deterministic assertions, as the pre-merge gate;\n- Human: SMEs spot-check edge cases and domain correctness, with double labeling to compute agreement;\n- Online: run the judge on sampled real traffic, user feedback, business metrics, and A/B.\nAgent Platform's Gen AI evals provide trajectory metrics and adaptive rubric metrics; if the customer already uses LangSmith, Langfuse, etc., don't push a switch—the eval logic itself is the asset.\n\n**How to build a golden set**: Three sources—typical and hard cases written by SMEs, samples from production / historical logs, and adversarial cases (jailbreaks, injection, privilege escalation, unanswerable). For a prototype, 50–200 items surface the big problems, but a gate needs enough samples per slice (with 5 samples the Wilson lower bound is only 0.23); tag by intent, language, channel, and difficulty; version it in git, recording sources and annotators; keep the dev set separate from the held-out gate set to **prevent contamination**; add from live failures monthly and retire stale cases.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Emphasize slice tagging: overall averages hide local regressions.\n- Mention contamination prevention so the gate can't be \"gamed\".","L6-T6C.2.7":"LLM judge calibration means verifying that an LLM grader agrees with domain experts (SMEs) before using it, otherwise the gate's conclusions can't be trusted. The principle is \"calibrate before you trust\".\n\n**Steps**\n1. **Write the rubric**: translate the customer's QA standards into binary, judgeable items; for example, \"does it cite the correct clause number\" is easier to judge than \"is the answer professional\".\n2. **Measure agreement**: sample 50–100 items, have SMEs and the judge score independently, and compute Cohen's kappa or simple agreement; review disagreements one by one and revise the rubric or switch judge models.\n3. **Counter bias**: in pairwise comparisons, run each pair twice with positions swapped to cancel position bias; use a judge from a different model family than the model under test where possible, or at least a different version, to reduce self-preference.\n4. **Pin the judge version**: once the judge is upgraded, historical scores are no longer comparable, so upgrades require rerunning the baseline.\n5. **Determinism first**: don't hand the judge anything rules can decide (whether a forbidden tool was called, whether PII leaked, whether the JSON is valid).\n\n**Trade-offs**: Judges are cheap and scalable but have position bias, self-preference, and version drift; rule-based checks are reliable but narrow in coverage. In practice combine them: deterministic assertions for safety and format, a calibrated judge for semantic quality, and human spot-checks for edge cases.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Common follow-up: what if the judge disagrees with SMEs 30% of the time? Review disagreements item by item, turn items into binary judgments, and switch models if needed; don't use it as a gate until agreement meets the bar.\n- Mentioning the two details \"pin the version\" and \"swap positions\" best shows experience.","L6-T6C.2.8":"A regression gate requires that any change to prompts, models, tools, or retrieval configuration be compared with the current production version on the same golden set, and not be merged unless it meets the bar. The companion script is eval_gate.py.\n\n**Gate rules (example thresholds)**\n- The **Wilson 95% lower bound** of the overall pass rate must be ≥ 0.50, not the point estimate: with small samples, 4/5 and 80/100 mean very different things. Wilson lower bound = (p + z²/2n − z·sqrt(p(1−p)/n + z²/4n²)) / (1 + z²/n).\n- **Judge slice regressions separately**: no slice may drop more than 10 percentage points versus the baseline; overall unchanged while the Chinese slice drops to 0 is the most common incident for multilingual customers.\n- **Zero tolerance on safety**: a single forbidden-tool call or PII leak fails the gate and is not averaged in; the regex for Singapore NRIC/FIN numbers must use `[STFGM]` for the first letter, because FINs starting with M have existed since 2022-01-01.\n- **Fail closed on an empty set**: an empty golden set fails outright—no evidence, no release.\n- **Gate latency and cost too**: gate p95 latency and cost-per-request together, preventing \"+2 points of quality, double the cost\" from slipping through.\n\nIn the example run, 3 of 5 samples passed, the Wilson lower bound was 0.23, two slices regressed, and there was one safety violation, so the gate FAILED.\n\n**Trade-offs**: A strict gate can block urgent fixes, so agree in advance on a written override process (usually the customer's product owner plus the FDE), with a rerun afterwards and a deadline for the fix.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why you use a confidence lower bound rather than the mean.\n- The gate covers four categories: quality, safety, latency, and cost.","L6-T6C.2.9":"An observability framework answers \"what happened in production\", and an SLO agreement answers \"what counts as good\". Only together can you be accountable for quality on site.\n\n**Five layers of observability**: Traces (one per request, including agent / LLM / tool / retrieval calls, tokens, latency, model and prompt versions; ADK has native OTel → Cloud Trace, and the MCP 2026-07-28 version specifies passing `traceparent` in `_meta`); metrics (TTFT, p50/p95/p99, cost-per-request, tool error rate, human handoff rate, online judge scores); logs (structured, with PII redacted before writing to disk); online evals (run the judge on sampled traces, thumbs-down goes into the labeling queue); alerts (immediate alerts on guardrail metrics, trend-based alerts for quality and cost). The core principle: **traces are evidence, evals are the verdict**—traces alone are just expensive logs, and evals alone can't pinpoint failures.\n\n**Agreeing targets with the customer**: This is a negotiation, not a unilateral announcement. First have employees run the same golden set to measure the **human baseline**, and set the target as \"no worse than the human baseline\" or a percentage of it; tier by **cost of errors**, with stricter targets or mandatory human review for high-cost categories; set targets per slice; break down the latency budget (e.g., for voice, split it into ASR, retrieval, first token, TTS); finally write it into an SLO document: Accuracy via the Wilson lower bound, Safety at 0, Latency at p95, plus Cost and Adoption, stating the measurement method and the action on violation (block, roll back, degrade).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"what accuracy target should we set\": measure the human baseline first; don't give an absolute number off the top of your head.","L6-T6C.2.10":"Tiered automation for high-stakes decisions addresses the situation where the customer wants full automation (e.g., automatic loan approval), but evals show a non-trivial error rate on edge cases. The answer is neither \"can't do it\" nor \"just ship it\", but granting autonomy in tiers based on evidence.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Show errors by slice**: break eval results down by slice so the customer sees where errors concentrate.\n2. **Asymmetric error costs**: wrongly approving a loan and wrongly rejecting one have different costs and different fairness implications; thresholds come from cost analysis, not intuition.\n3. **Tiered design**: the model executes automatically only on slices where it has been proven reliable; the rest go to humans (e.g., underwriters), accompanied by an evidence summary prepared by the model.\n4. **Governance sign-off**: credit decisions carry regulatory expectations on explainability and fairness and must be signed off by the bank's model risk and compliance functions.\n5. **Continuous monitoring**: track approval quality and drift, and keep a rollback path.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: This design balances automation rate against regulatory risk and lets the scope of automation expand gradually with pilot data. The same idea appears in fraud prevention (the assistant gathers evidence, the investigator decides) and compliance (the system drafts, the compliance officer signs).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- If pressed \"competitors are already fully automated\": we can reach the same rate, but open it up gradually based on evidence; the cost of one regulatory incident far outweighs a few months' gap.\n- Mentioning model risk management sign-off shows familiarity with bank governance processes.","L6-T6C.3.1":"Product catalyst means the FDE turns field friction into product value: identifying patterns that recur across customers, writing them up as feedback the product team can adopt, or distilling them into maintained reusable modules.\n\n**Spotting patterns**: Every time you're stuck on site for more than 30 minutes, add a friction log entry (date, anonymized customer, component and version, what you were trying to do, what actually happened, the workaround, time spent, business impact), and review weekly. The same workaround recurring at ≥ 3 customers → reusable module; docs not matching reality → docs feedback; a product gap blocking launch → feature request; a customer-specific process → stays in the project, not productized; a reproducible bug → bug report. To decide whether to productize, ask three things: will a second customer need it? Is it cheaper for the product team to build than for FDEs to do it one by one? Will customers switch to a competitor if it's not built?\n\n**A high-quality feature request**: reproducible, quantified, bounded, and states the cost of the workaround. The template includes Problem (who, what they're trying to do, what happens today + a minimal repro), Impact (how many customers, frequency, severity), Current workaround and its cost, Proposed outcome (describe the desired result rather than prescribing an implementation, with acceptance criteria and out of scope), Evidence, and Contact. The title states what the user wants to accomplish, not the implementation you want.\n\n**Reusable modules**: Reference architecture + Terraform, ADK agent templates, MCP server templates, eval harnesses, discovery toolkits; success depends on **someone maintaining them** (owner, version number, changelog, support channel), otherwise within six months they become the new legacy system.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Examples must be reproduced and confirmed yourself; hypothetical numbers must not be submitted as real gaps.","L6-T6C.3.2":"Regional leadership requires FDEs to mentor, co-build with customer teams, and exert cross-functional influence. The end goal is that after you leave, the customer organization can carry on by itself and even teach others.\n\n**Mentoring and co-building**: Four-step mentoring—I do, you watch → we do it together → you do, I review → you do it alone—with a clear deliverable at each step (e.g., step three: they independently write an MCP tool that passes the gates), and code reviews that explain \"why\". When co-building, the code lives in the customer's repo, customer engineers open PRs from week one, customer engineers present the weekly demo, and before handover the customer team completes \"change prompt → run evals → ship\" independently at least once.\n\n**Enablement plan**\n1. **Measure a capability baseline**: five capabilities (prompt and tool development, evals, observability and troubleshooting, security and permissions, release and rollback), each with four levels (can understand, can modify, can deliver independently, can teach others), confirmed by the customer's technical lead and updated every two weeks.\n2. **Define \"graduation\" as verifiable actions**: e.g., a customer engineer independently completes \"change prompt → pass gate → canary → locate a problem from traces → write a postmortem\".\n3. Assets (runbooks, eval sets and labeling guidelines, scaffolding, decision records) live in the customer's repo; develop one or two champions with a backup.\n4. **Measure capability, not training sessions**: share of independent changes, number of issues needing your involvement within 30 days after handover, days for a new hire to reach their first independent release.\n\n**Cross-functional influence**: Align with account managers, Customer Engineers, the product team, and partners (SIs); the core is letting data speak—friction logs, eval reports, ROI reviews. Google FDE engagements are short and handed over to partners, so being teachable and hand-over-able is itself a core deliverable.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use only real experiences for STAR examples; if you don't have one, tell a smaller-scale but true story.","L6-T6C.3.3":"The same solution has to be presented once to each of three audiences, and each has a different \"currency\": executives care about money, risk, and time; security teams care about attack surface, data flow, and auditability; engineering teams care about interfaces, ownership boundaries, and operational burden.\n\n**For executives**: The structure is conclusion → numbers (as ranges) → risks and mitigations → the decision you need from them. Example: the pilot group handles tickets 22% faster than the control group with no increase in QA errors; full rollout saves a range per year, and even the pessimistic case pays back within a few months; the main risk is adoption, so the next phase adds training; today we need your decision on whether to expand to two more regions. Avoid technical jargon, single-point numbers, and omitting \"what needs to be decided\".\n\n**For security / risk**: Open with the data flow → identity and permissions → controls → residual risk → audit. For example: data doesn't leave the Singapore region, the project is inside the VPC-SC perimeter, the agent uses the logged-in user's identity, write operations require confirmation and carry idempotency keys, and every tool call logs user, parameters, and result; proactively hand residual risks (such as indirect prompt injection via email) to them for assessment, and invite red-team testing. Avoid saying \"the model is safe\" or \"Google handles all that\".\n\n**For engineering**: Interfaces and contracts → deployment and operations → evals and gates → handover plan. Spell out each MCP tool's typed schema, timeouts, and error format; who owns the backend APIs and who owns the tool wrappers; the gate rules; where alerts go and which failures the runbook covers. Avoid handing over only an architecture diagram without a runbook.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- In role-play questions, first identify which audience the other person represents, then choose the structure.\n- Security teams fear being kept in the dark most; proactively stating residual risks actually builds trust.","L6-T6C.3.4":"These three general communication techniques apply to every conversation with customers and interviewers, and help avoid answering the wrong question, making up answers, and mishandling bad news.\n\n**1. Restate before answering**: First say \"So the concern is X — is that right?\" to confirm the question, then answer. This avoids answering the wrong question and buys you thinking time; restating your understanding after each category of questions in discovery is the same principle.\n\n**2. If you don't know, say so, and give a time to follow up**: e.g., \"I'm not sure of the exact quota in that region; I'll confirm with the product team and get back to you by Thursday.\" In a Google context, making up an answer is far worse than admitting you don't know—the customer may make architecture or compliance decisions based on it, and being wrong is very costly. The same goes for regulatory provisions: go by the customer compliance team's list and don't quote rules from memory.\n\n**3. Lead bad news with facts and impact, then the cause**: Open with \"launch moves from the 15th to the 6th of next month, three weeks; pilot results are unaffected\", and only then explain why, distinguishing dependencies on their side from problems on ours without shifting blame; bring options and a recommendation, and end with next steps and a communication cadence. Explaining the cause first makes it sound like you're making excuses.\n\n**Why it matters**: FDE interviews make heavy use of role play, and interviewers assess not just technical skill but credibility. All three techniques build the sense that \"what this person says can be trusted\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- For the behavioral question \"deliver bad news\", apply the structure of technique 3 directly.\n- When asked about product details you're unsure of, admitting it and giving a path to verify scores higher than being vague.","L6-T6C.3.5":"These three scenario drills are typical FDE interview role plays in which the interviewer plays the customer: facing a CTO over a delivery delay, being grilled by a security lead, and a business owner challenging the ROI.\n\n**Scenario 1: a three-week delivery delay (CTO)**: Open directly with the facts and impact (launch slips three weeks; pilot results are unaffected), then give the causes, distinguishing a dependency on their side (the order system needs to return idempotent responses) from a reasonable requirement (security review added a penetration test), and make clear it isn't an agent quality issue; then bring options: A is to launch read-only features on the 15th and add order changes on the 6th, B is to launch everything on the 6th; recommend A (delivers value earlier, gathers production data) and name its risks; finally commit to sending a one-paragraph summary to the board that day and updating every Friday.\n\n**Scenario 2: CISO grilling**: Don't say \"it's very secure\"; cover six things—data flow, identity (delegated user identity), controls (send tool requires user confirmation + idempotency key, tool allowlist, input screening), residual risk (prompt injection via inbound email), audit (logs stored in-region, PII redacted first, every tool call logs user / version / parameters), and incident response (a kill switch that disables the send tool without redeploying, plus an incident runbook); proactively invite red-team testing.\n\n**Scenario 3: an operations VP challenges the ROI (\"why not just hire five people\")**: Take the alternative seriously and compare hiring cost with system cost on the same basis; admit that a four-week pilot is not enough evidence, propose expanding to two more teams for six weeks with a control group and reviews at days 30 and 90, and stop and hire if the numbers don't hold; finish with a concrete ask (two champions, 30 minutes every two weeks to review the numbers).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Common structure: facts → options and a recommendation → exit criteria → concrete next steps.","L6-T6C.3.6":"Translating a research background means turning what you did at a research institution into customer value in an FDE context, and honestly answering the challenge \"you have no customer delivery experience\".\n\n**Translation table**\n- Designing eval sets, running ablations → building golden sets and launch gates for customers, using data to decide whether to ship;\n- Error analysis → locating regressions by slice after launch, writing incident postmortems;\n- Reproducing papers, comparing baselines → measuring human and existing-system baselines first so ROI is credible;\n- The \"generate → execute → feed errors back → regenerate\" loop → an agent's tool calls, error recovery, and retry limits;\n- Model compression and fine-tuning → cost and latency optimization, and knowing when not to fine-tune;\n- Writing papers, reviewing → writing design docs and feature requests, explaining complex conclusions to different readers.\n\n**A three-step answer**\n1. **Acknowledge, don't make excuses**: don't dress up research as customer delivery; the interviewer will probe the details.\n2. **Give the closest evidence**: a project with real users that shipped and ran into problems; explain what you were responsible for, what happened after launch, and how you fixed it.\n3. **Give a plan to close the gap and what you've already done**: e.g., an end-to-end project built on Google Cloud (eval gates, traces, OAuth).\n\n**Common traps**: Leading with papers and model architecture; using \"we\" to hide your personal contribution; treating lab metrics as business metrics; dodging post-launch problems. The real strength of a research background is **rigor in evaluation and sensitivity to failure modes**, and those are exactly what FDE interviews probe again and again: evals and failure handling.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Placeholders in talking points must be replaced with real projects and numbers; don't make anything up.","L6-T6C.3.7":"Latest developments covers the 2025–2026 regulatory and product news directly relevant to FDE interviews (especially for Singapore and Google Cloud roles); citing them in scenario questions shows your information is current.\n\n**Regulation**\n- **MAS consultation paper on Guidelines on AI Risk Management** (2025-11-13): covers generative AI and AI agents, requiring an AI inventory, risk materiality assessment, and lifecycle controls (data, fairness, transparency and explainability, human oversight, third parties, evaluation, monitoring, change management), applied proportionately to materiality, with a proposed 12-month transition period after issuance; as of 2026-08 it is still a proposal. It must be mentioned in banking / insurance scenarios, and the FEAT principles continue to apply.\n- **IMDA Model AI Governance Framework for Agentic AI** (published 2026-01-22, v1.5 updated 2026-05-20): a shared vocabulary for discussing agent risk with Singapore customers.\n\n**Products**\n- **Next '26** (2026-04-22): Vertex AI evolved into Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, Agent Engine → Agent Runtime, Vertex AI Search → Agent Search, with new Agent Gateway / Registry / Identity; alongside a US$750 million partner commitment. Use the new names in interviews.\n- **The new MCP 2026-07-28 spec**: removes protocol-level sessions and the initialize handshake, each request declares the protocol version in `_meta`, and DCR is marked deprecated; MCP server deliverables should be designed for the new version.\n- Others: MedLM was discontinued on 2025-09-29; the Health Information Act 2026 was published on 2026-02-12.\n\n**Trend judgment (inference)**: FDE deliverables are standardizing into \"agent + MCP server + eval harness\"; vendors treat FDEs as product feedback sensors; handover and enablement capabilities are gaining weight.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Call a proposal a \"proposal\"; don't present it as already in force.","L6-T6C.3.8":"The companion code and exercises turn the FDE methodology into runnable scripts and demonstrable work, which can serve as \"I've done this\" evidence in interviews.\n\n**Three scripts (code/ch45/, pure Python)**\n- **use_case_scorer.py**: use-case selection and ROI. Computes monthly net benefit, first-year ROI, and a feasibility score using the formula that subtracts review time, and outputs pessimistic / base / optimistic payback periods with a recommendation; \"high risk + high automation\" gets a direct recommendation to add human approval first.\n- **eval_gate.py**: regression gate. Wilson 95% lower bound, slice regression ≤ 10 percentage points, zero tolerance on safety, fail closed on an empty set, and gating on p95 latency and per-request cost; the stub deliberately injects a missed Chinese answer and an email leak to demonstrate a FAIL.\n- **sql_repair_loop.py**: Text-to-SQL execute-and-repair loop (sqlite3 demo). Generate → static check → execute with a row limit → feed the raw error back, with a retry cap; policy violations (write operations) are not retried and are blocked immediately; an empty result is fed back once as a suspicious signal; repeated failures go to needs_human.\n\n**Exercises**\n1. Discovery toolkit: make up 3 use cases and run the scorer, write a one-page scope document for the top scorer, and explain which parameter most affects payback.\n2. A BigQuery data agent with a gate: swap the stub for Gemini and sqlite for BigQuery (dry run + maximum_bytes_billed), build a 30–50-question sliced golden set, run the gate in GitHub Actions, and evaluate by execution accuracy rather than SQL strings.\n3. Spoken scenario drills: pick 3 scenarios and self-test with English recordings following scoping 15 minutes → architecture 20 minutes → productionization and follow-ups 20 minutes.\n4. Friction log and feature request; 5. An MCP tool with confirmation tokens and idempotency keys.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Put real numbers in your resume outcomes, not placeholders.","L6-T6C.4.1":"The common scenario skeleton is the reference structure shared by all 10 customer scenario drills: in an interview, apply the skeleton first, then adapt it to industry constraints, so you can produce a complete solution quickly in the 20-minute architecture segment.\n\n**The skeleton**\n- **Channels / frontend** → **agent orchestration** (ADK on Agent Runtime, or low-code CX Agent Studio)\n- → **tool layer** (MCP servers / Apigee, fronted by Agent Gateway + Model Armor)\n- → **enterprise systems** (core systems, data warehouse, document stores)\n- **Cross-cutting concerns**: identity (user delegation vs the agent's own identity), eval gates, traces, human approval, data residency.\n\n**Answer order**: background and pain points → clarifying questions → solution design (choices + architecture diagram) → prototype to production → evaluation and observability → security and compliance → risks and trade-offs → ROI → handling follow-ups. Timing is roughly scoping 15 minutes, architecture 20 minutes, productionization and follow-ups 20 minutes; **ask clarifying questions and restate first, then give the solution**.\n\n**Why it matters**: The differences between scenarios mostly fall on the cross-cutting items—banking emphasizes identity and compliance, healthcare emphasizes human sign-off and evidence, telecom emphasizes approvals and the autonomy ladder, multinationals emphasize residency. The skeleton lets you focus on those differences instead of drawing from scratch each time. Agent Gateway and the MCP tool layer are also where structural security controls (permissions, confirmation, idempotency) live, which is more reliable than relying on prompts.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Name at least one stage-gate exit criterion, one structural security control (not a prompt), and one ROI formula.\n- When pressed on timelines, name the critical dependencies (security review, data access).","L6-T6C.4.2":"Scenario 1 is a Singapore retail bank that wants to launch a customer-facing service agent in its contact center: hundreds of thousands of inquiries a month (balances, transactions, lost cards, fee disputes, product terms), high labor costs, long overnight queues, and a compliance team with major concerns.\n\n**Solution**: Phase one handles only \"information queries + a low-risk action (reporting a lost card)\"; disputes and refunds only collect information and hand off to a human. The architecture: the bank's existing authentication (step-up OTP) → ADK on Agent Runtime or CX Agent Studio (the evolution of Dialogflow CX, more low-code, suited to existing Dialogflow assets) → Gemini at a regional endpoint + Agent Search for product terms → Agent Gateway / Model Armor → a read-only account MCP and a card-operations MCP → Apigee → the core banking system.\n\n**Key structural controls**\n- `customer_id` **comes from the authentication context, not model output**; the model has no power to choose whom to look up, preventing unauthorized access to other people's accounts.\n- **The fee-waiver capability simply doesn't exist** in the tool layer; promissory wording is caught by output detection, and an adversarial set specifically tests \"coaxing a fee-waiver promise\" and social engineering (\"I'm the customer's husband\").\n- Reporting a lost card requires user confirmation in the UI; the tool layer validates the confirmation token and carries an idempotency key.\n\n**Evaluation and compliance**: The golden set is sliced by intent × language (including Singlish code-mixing) × whether authentication is needed; zero tolerance for false promises. Compliance involves MAS's proposed AI risk management guidelines, FEAT, the technology risk and outsourcing guidelines, and PDPA; confirm that the chosen model supports in-region processing in asia-southeast1, and don't use the global endpoint.\n\n**Trade-offs**: Better to accept lower containment than let the agent touch disputes and fees. ROI = monthly inquiries × additional containment rate × cost per human contact − running cost − review and QA cost.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why not let the model decide which customer to look up\": it's a structural control against unauthorized access, not reliant on prompts.","L6-T6C.4.3":"Scenario 2 is claims document processing at an insurer: tens of thousands of claim forms, medical documents, invoices, photos, and police reports each month in every format imaginable; claims handlers spend much of their time on data entry and cross-checking, and fraudulent claims slip through.\n\n**Solution**: The pipeline is \"extract → validate → check against the policy → generate a claims-handler summary\", and **the decision is still made by the claims handler**. Documents land in Cloud Storage (CMEK) → Pub/Sub → Document AI classification → Custom Extractor for fields, Layout Parser to chunk long documents → rule-based validation of amounts / dates / duplicates → a claims agent checks terms via a read-only policy MCP → the handler's workbench shows the summary, evidence, and risk flags, with feedback flowing into BigQuery.\n- Custom Extractor starts zero-shot / few-shot with the base model, then is fine-tuned once there is more data (the docs require at least 50 training and 50 test documents).\n- Amounts, dates, and policy numbers get **deterministic validation** by rules; the LLM only makes reading-comprehension judgments like \"is this covered by the terms\", and must quote the policy text.\n\n**Evaluation**: Field-level precision / recall / F1; an SME-labeled set for term checking; recall and false positives for fraud flags. **The claims handler's edit rate** is the best online quality signal, and the edits themselves are labels.\n\n**Compliance and trade-offs**: Medical documents are sensitive data; some Preview processor versions use the global endpoint and don't meet residency, so production should use GA versions with explicit regional processing. Straight-through processing rate vs missed-detection risk: automate low-value, low-risk types first, and leave photo-based damage assessment out of phase one.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Is 95% accuracy per field good enough\": it depends on the field and the cost of errors; amount fields need a rule backstop or mandatory human review at low confidence, with per-field thresholds.\n- New document types: the classifier outputs \"unknown\" and they go straight to the human queue.","L6-T6C.4.4":"Scenario 3 is a Southeast Asian e-commerce company launching an in-app conversational shopping assistant and automating after-sales (shipment tracking, returns and exchanges); during big sales, traffic is **more than ten times** normal.\n\n**Solution**: A router agent + three specialists—a shopping assistant agent (Agent Search for Commerce for product retrieval and recommendations, read-only inventory / pricing MCP with a short-TTL cache, add-to-cart requires user confirmation), an orders and returns agent (order system MCP, creating a return requires confirmation + an idempotency key), and a policy Q&A agent (RAG over the policy knowledge base). The Shopping agent in Google's Gemini Enterprise for CX is in Preview; build your own with ADK when you need deep customization or already have in-house search.\n\n**Why split into multiple agents**: The shopping assistant and after-sales have completely different tools, permissions, and evals, and splitting them allows independent iteration and evaluation; if it's only to \"look advanced\", a single agent with tools is enough.\n\n**Prototype to production**: Start with after-sales (ROI is easy to measure, risk is controllable), then the shopping assistant, measuring conversion with an A/B test randomized by user; before big sales, run load tests and degradation drills (model downgrade, disabling non-critical agents, query-only mode).\n\n**Evaluation and trade-offs**: For after-sales, look at task success rate, return correctness, and zero duplicate creations; for the shopping assistant, A/B conversion rate and average order value, with zero \"hallucinated products\" (nonexistent SKUs); dynamically switch models by budget during big sales. Returns and loyalty points can be abused: per-user rate limits, handoff to a human above an amount threshold, and Model Armor against injection. Better to degrade than time out.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The model provider rate-limits you during a big sale: provisioned quota / reserved throughput, routing to a backup model, degrading non-critical features, serving after-sales queries from cache, all rehearsed in advance.","L6-T6C.4.5":"\"Never let the model generate prices or discounts\" is a structural safety rule for e-commerce shopping assistants: prices and promotions are factual data with commercial and legal consequences, and must never be generated or inferred by an LLM.\n\n**How it works**\n- Prices and promotions are **read from the system in real time** (inventory / pricing MCP tool, read-only, optionally with a short-TTL cache) and **displayed verbatim** in the answer;\n- **The answer rendering layer verifies the source of price fields**: prices may only come from tool-result fields, and a price not returned by a tool cannot be displayed;\n- Output detection blocks any price or discount statement not backed by a tool result;\n- Adversarial evals specifically test inputs that coax the model to \"give me a discount\".\n\n**Why it matters**: Once the model makes up a discount, a customer screenshot can lead to complaints, disputes, or even legal liability; writing \"don't make up prices\" in the prompt is unreliable because prompt injection or hallucination can bypass it. Putting the constraint into the data flow (source verification) and the rendering layer is a textbook example of \"structural controls beat prompt controls\". The same idea appears in the banking scenario (the tool layer has no fee-waiver capability) and the airline scenario (refund amounts are computed by a deterministic rules service, and the model only proposes actions).\n\n**Trade-offs**: Prices must be accurate in real time, which is in tension with latency, so a short-TTL cache strikes the balance; during big sales when prices change frequently, shorten the TTL or re-verify at checkout.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- If asked \"how do you stop the model from inventing discounts\": structural control—prices can only come from tool fields, verified at render time; output detection as a backstop; adversarial evals.","L6-T6C.4.6":"Scenario 4 is an equipment manufacturer with plants in Singapore and Malaysia: technicians troubleshoot by flipping through thousands of pages of PDF manuals and asking veterans, and historical records in the work-order system (something like SAP PM or Maximo) are free text; new technicians' MTTR is markedly higher than senior technicians', and the senior ones are gradually retiring.\n\n**Solution**: Phase one is a \"**troubleshooting assistant with citations + work-order drafts**\" that **does not touch the OT network** (no connection whatsoever between PLC / SCADA and the agent; IT / OT separation is a red line).\n- Manual retrieval must filter on \"machine model + hardware revision\" metadata, otherwise it will give revision B's torque values to a revision A question; Layout Parser preserves the structure of tables and diagrams.\n- Historical work orders are first cleaned and deduplicated, then \"symptom → root cause → action → resolved or not\" quadruples are extracted for similar-case retrieval; this is often more valuable than manual retrieval because it captures the veterans' experience.\n- Safety-critical content (lockout/tagout LOTO, live electrical work) may only be quoted verbatim from the manual; the model is not allowed to rewrite the steps.\n\n**Evaluation**: SMEs write 150 questions with correct page numbers to measure recall@5 and citation accuracy; business metrics are MTTR, first-time fix rate, and repeat-failure rate.\n\n**ROI**: The main item is usually **downtime losses** rather than labor—monthly value ≈ monthly failures × hours of MTTR reduction × downtime loss per hour + technician hours saved × labor cost − running cost, with the downtime loss figure provided by the customer's finance team.\n\n**Trade-offs**: Better to start with 2,000 cleaned work orders than dump in 200,000 raw ones.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Who is responsible if a technician follows it and gets hurt\": safety steps are only quoted verbatim with version and page number, the tool is positioned as lookup and suggestion, and the customer's EHS signs off before launch.","L6-T6C.4.7":"Scenario 5 is a Southeast Asian carrier's network operations center (NOC): tens of thousands of alarms a day, and during alarm storms, manual correlation and root cause analysis (RCA) take hours. The customer wants the agent to do triage and RCA suggestions first, and partial automatic remediation in the long run.\n\n**Solution: hierarchical delegation**\n- Alarms / KPIs / logs → Pub/Sub → **deterministic correlation and compression** (rules + ML), compressing tens of thousands of alarms into a few dozen \"incidents\", and the LLM only handles incidents;\n- A **triage agent** correlates alarms and dispatches tickets; **domain-expert agents** (RAN / core network / transport) diagnose, querying a topology digital twin on Spanner Graph and KPIs and runbooks in BigQuery;\n- **All change actions go through NOC engineer approval**, and only pre-approved runbook steps are executed. Topology is queried from the graph database, never guessed by the model.\n\n**Prototype to production**: The prototype replays the past 6 months of incidents (backtesting) without connecting to production; the pilot runs in shadow mode, where suggestions are shown but not executed; in production, read-only diagnosis is fully enabled, while automatic execution is opened only for pre-approved, small-blast-radius, reversible steps with a blast-radius cap—this is the \"autonomy ladder\".\n\n**Evaluation**: RCA top-1 / top-3 accuracy and time to RCA on a historical incident set; fault injection in a lab network or the digital twin; zero tolerance for erroneous automatic execution. The read plane and write plane use different identities and network paths.\n\n**Trade-offs**: Too many expert agents bring delegation overhead and buck-passing, so start with one domain; during alarm storms, apply a budget cap and degrade to clustering and summarization only.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why not let the agent fix things automatically at 3 a.m.\": only for pre-approved, reversible, capped actions; everything else earns autonomy step by step after accumulating evidence in shadow mode.","L6-T6C.4.8":"The Autonomous Network Operations framework is Google Cloud's public reference framework for telecom network operations. In a telecom NOC scenario, it is a starting point for \"first see which components can be reused directly\", showing product awareness.\n\n**Timeline and contents**\n- First released in 2025-06;\n- On 2026-03-05 it added the Autonomous Data Steward and the Core Network VoLTE Agent, and One NZ has put the VoLTE agent into operation;\n- A 2026-09 report said its **network digital twin sits on Spanner Graph**, with a graph neural network layer and an agent layer on top.\n\n**Relation to the scenario design**: This matches the architecture of scenario 5—topology and dependencies are expressed in a graph database, and expert agents query the graph rather than letting the model guess \"which site hangs off which device\". Another key principle is that **alarms must not be fed directly to the LLM**: first use deterministic rules and ML to deduplicate, compress, and correlate by topology, shrinking tens of thousands of alarms into a few dozen incidents for the LLM to handle; this step determines whether cost and latency stay under control, and prevents token spikes during alarm storms. The industry's common language for autonomy levels is TM Forum's autonomous network levels.\n\n**Why it matters**: Telecom is one of the priority industries in Google Cloud FDE job descriptions; citing the official framework shows product familiarity and helps the customer avoid reinventing the wheel. But in interviews treat it as a reference rather than something to \"apply directly\"; whether specific components fit must be assessed.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Phrasing: \"I'd first look at which components of the Autonomous Network Operations framework can be reused directly.\"\n- Explain why deterministic compression comes before the LLM: cost, latency, and explainability.","L6-T6C.4.9":"Scenario 6 is clinical documentation (discharge summaries, referral letters, outpatient notes) at a Singapore hospital cluster: information is scattered across progress notes, lab results, medications, and imaging reports, and doctors spend a lot of time writing. The customer wants AI to draft and doctors to review, edit, and sign.\n\n**Solution**: \"**Retrieve evidence from the medical record → draft in a structured way → attach evidence to every sentence → doctor reviews, edits, and signs**\"; the system only produces drafts, and a draft is never sent out without clinician review.\n- The EMR flows via FHIR / HL7v2 into a FHIR store in the Cloud Healthcare API (CMEK); development and evaluation use only de-identified copies;\n- Drafting is structured by \"department template + required-item checklist\": allergy history, changes to discharge medications, and follow-up arrangements are required, and when missing the draft explicitly says \"not found in the record\" rather than letting the model fill it in;\n- The review interface highlights low-confidence sentences, and doctors' edits flow back into the eval set.\n\n**Model choice**: MedLM was retired on 2025-09-29; today's options are Gemini on Agent Platform (regional endpoint), or self-hosting the open MedGemma in a customer-controlled environment. The choice depends on residency, control, and eval results, not on whether the name contains \"Med\".\n\n**Evaluation and compliance**: The main metrics are **citation precision** and **critical-item omission rate**; hallucinated drugs or doses are severity-1 incidents; online, watch how much doctors edit and time before signing. Compliance involves the Health Information Act 2026 (obligations taking effect in phases), PDPA, and MOH cybersecurity requirements; whether it counts as a medical device is for the customer's regulatory team to determine.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Can we fine-tune on medical records\": first confirm consent and governance, build a baseline with retrieval + templates, and consider fine-tuning only when evals show consistent format / terminology problems.","L6-T6C.4.10":"Automation bias is when people, after long use of an automated system, gradually over-trust its output and stop checking carefully. In clinical documentation, it shows up as doctors gradually signing system drafts without reading them, so \"human in the loop\" exists in name only.\n\n**Why it's dangerous**: The system design relies on doctors' review as the last line of defense; once review becomes a formality, a hallucinated drug name or a missing allergy history enters the official medical record, while signing responsibility still rests within the clinical process. The higher the accuracy and the more doctors trust it, the stronger this bias becomes.\n\n**Countermeasures**\n- **Highlight low-confidence sentences**: make sentences where the model is uncertain or evidence is thin stand out, to direct attention;\n- **Random audits**: periodically spot-check signed documents to detect a \"sign without reading\" pattern;\n- **Mandatory confirmation of critical items**: critical items such as allergies and medication changes require the doctor to confirm each one individually, with no one-click sign-all;\n- Combined with \"evidence for every sentence\": the medication section may only come from structured medication records, with each sentence citing its source.\n\n**Trade-offs**: Efficiency vs safety—better a shorter draft with more blanks than fabrication; mandatory confirmation adds signing time, but that is the price of preserving the value of human oversight. Use \"doctor edit magnitude\" and \"time before signing\" as monitoring signals: if both keep trending toward zero, it may be a sign of bias.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The concept applies equally to every \"AI drafts + human approves\" process, including claims handlers, investigators, and NOC engineers.\n- If asked \"a doctor signed a summary with a hallucinated drug name\": responsibility in the clinical process doesn't change, but the system should make this hard to happen; afterwards, replay from the trace and add it to the regression set.","L6-T6C.4.11":"Scenario 7 is government agency Q&A: a backlog of inquiries about subsidies, eligibility, and application procedures, and internal officers also have to dig through many circulars and guidelines to answer. The agency wants a public-facing Q&A assistant and an internal officer copilot.\n\n**Solution: two tiers**\n- **Public side**: answers only on the basis of **published policies**, **always with citations**, and makes no case-specific judgments (e.g., \"am I eligible\" is directed to the formal application process);\n- **Officer side**: can read internal circulars and guidelines controlled by classification-level ACLs; individual case data is accessed through **read-only MCP tools** in sessions where the user **has completed national digital identity authentication (e.g., Singpass)**.\n- The policy index carries **effective dates and versions**; answers cite the version currently in force, and older versions are kept for \"which version applied at the time of application\".\n\n**The deployment environment is determined by data classification**: In 2025-08 Google announced that Singapore government agencies can use Gemini 2.5 Flash and Vertex AI Search on Government on Commercial Cloud (GCC), and GovTech, HTX, and CSIT can use Gemini on Google Distributed Cloud air-gapped. Compliance follows government ICT standards such as IM8, and public-sector data governance can't simply borrow the enterprise PDPA framing; IMDA's agentic AI governance framework can serve as a shared vocabulary for risk assessment.\n\n**Rollout and evaluation**: Launch the officer copilot first (low risk, fast feedback), with the public side on a small-traffic canary; evaluate policy accuracy, citation accuracy, correct refusals on individual-case / legal / politically sensitive topics, the multilingual gap, and detection of \"stale answers\" after policy updates.\n\n**Trade-offs**: Wrong answers directly damage public trust, so coverage gives way to accuracy; better to hand off to humans more often.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Policy changes overnight: incremental index updates + effective-date fields + cache invalidation; update the index before the announcement goes out.","L6-T6C.4.12":"Scenario 8 is an internal data analysis agent for a regional retail group: the data is already in BigQuery, some metrics are defined in Looker, and business users want to ask questions in natural language to relieve a weeks-long backlog of data requests.\n\n**Two routes; evaluate before choosing**\n- **A data agent via the Conversational Analytics API**: use when questions focus on already-modeled data and you want the fastest launch; it is GA for BigQuery, Looker, and Data Studio, and you can provide authored context (including verified queries and instructions) to improve accuracy; the cost is less room for custom workflows and evaluation.\n- **A custom ADK agent + the BigQuery managed MCP server / MCP Toolbox**: use when you need custom tools, multi-step analysis, an execute-and-repair loop, and fine-grained evaluation; development and maintenance cost more.\n\n**The execute-and-repair loop (the core engineering point)**: generate SQL → **static check** (read-only, single statement) → **dry run** to estimate bytes scanned (over budget goes back to generation) → execute with `maximum_bytes_billed` → **feed the raw error back** to the model to regenerate → check the result (empty, anomalous magnitude) → output answer + SQL + definition notes. Three details: set a retry cap; policy violations (write operations) are not retried but reported immediately; an empty result is fed back once as a suspicious signal. Repeated failures go to a human.\n\n**Security**: Create the BigQuery client with the end user's OAuth credentials so row-level security and column-level policy tags apply per user; a highly privileged service account effectively flattens everyone's permissions to the highest level. Free text in query results may contain indirect prompt injection and is always treated as data.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- A 50 TB full-table scan: dry-run budget, `maximum_bytes_billed`, mandatory partition filters, per-user quotas and alerts.\n- A 2-week prototype: one business domain, 30 high-frequency questions, starting with verified queries.","L6-T6C.4.13":"\"Wrong numbers that look right\" is the biggest risk for a data analysis agent: the SQL runs, the result is nicely formatted, and the magnitude is plausible, but the metric definition or filter is wrong, and the number makes its way into executive reports and board materials. This is far more dangerous than an outright error.\n\n**Countermeasures**\n- **Key metrics go only through verified queries**: core metrics like revenue use pre-certified queries rather than letting the model generate them on the spot;\n- **Answers show the SQL and the definition**: let users see how the number was produced; during the pilot, label answers as \"certified metric / exploratory result\";\n- **Semantic layer**: metric definitions, verified queries, and Knowledge Catalog metadata give the model the definitions, and changes to metric definitions go through a semantic-layer review process;\n- **Result checks**: empty results and anomalous magnitudes are treated as suspicious signals.\n\n**Evaluation method**: **Compare execution results (execution accuracy), not SQL strings**—the same question can have several correct SQL queries, so string comparison misjudges; conversely, SQL queries that look similar as strings can produce completely different results. Slices include multi-table joins, time windows, ambiguous definitions, and questions that require a follow-up; also test \"did it ask a follow-up when it should have\".\n\n**Trade-offs**: Flexibility vs consistency. The more verified queries, the more reliable but the narrower the coverage; decide together with the data team which metrics must be certified.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- If pressed \"the CFO got a wrong revenue number from the agent\": first determine whether it's a definition problem or an SQL problem; force revenue-type metrics through verified queries; add the question to the regression set; in external communication, show the fix and the measures to prevent recurrence.","L6-T6C.4.14":"Scenario 9 is legacy system integration: a regional financial or manufacturing company whose core transactions run on an IBM mainframe (COBOL, CICS, DB2) and whose finance and supply chain run on SAP; operations staff checking order status have to switch between green-screen terminals and the SAP GUI. The customer wants an agent that can check status, explain exceptions, and initiate routine operations, but the mainframe team is wary of additional load.\n\n**Core principle**: **Separate reads from writes and real-time from analytics, and never write directly to the database**.\n- Analytical and historical questions use data replicated to BigQuery: mainframe data via the Mainframe Connector (EBCDIC conversion), SAP data via the BigQuery Connector for SAP or SAP BDC Connect (zero-copy);\n- Real-time status uses existing APIs behind an Apigee facade (mainframe CICS transactions, SAP OData / BAPI), with caching, read replicas, and rate limiting;\n- Write operations only call existing interfaces that already include business validation, with **idempotency keys and human confirmation**;\n- SAP table names (such as VBAK) and copybook fields are unfriendly to models, so translate them into business semantics in the MCP tool layer, e.g., `get_order_status(order_id)`.\n\n**Rollout**: The prototype is read-only on replicated data; the pilot connects one real-time read-only API and monitors MIPS together with the mainframe team; production opens the first write operation (e.g., resubmitting failed orders), with human confirmation at first. Evaluation includes reconciliation against the system of record, idempotency tests, and monitoring mainframe load during load tests.\n\n**Trade-offs**: The agent may query ten times as often as a human, so caching and quotas are needed to protect the mainframe; modernization is a multi-year effort, and the agent layer can deliver value first and act as a stable interface during migration.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why not let the agent drive the green screen directly\": UI automation is only a stopgap when there's no API; it's brittle and hard to audit, and needs a written exit plan.","L6-T6C.4.15":"Scenario 10 is a multinational headquartered in Singapore that wants one unified employee services agent (HR policies, IT support, expense claims) covering 8 languages across Southeast Asia, Japan, and Europe; each country's legal team has different requirements on where employee data is stored and processed, and local teams worry about poor answer quality in non-English languages.\n\n**Architecture: a global control plane + regional data planes**\n- Shared globally: code, prompt templates, the eval framework, synthetic eval data, CI/CD and eval gates, and the Agent Registry (no personal data);\n- Kept in-region: personal data, local policy indexes, sessions, traces, and logs (traces and logs are personal data too; log buckets are in-region, and data is redacted before being written);\n- Employees are routed by home region to regional agent instances and regional model endpoints.\n\n**Residency**: Distinguish the location of data at rest from the ML processing location; the global endpoint has no residency guarantee, and each region's available models must be checked model by model. When a region lacks a model, give the customer three options to decide among: use an in-region model and accept the quality gap; self-host an open model (such as Gemma) in-region; or have legal approve processing in a neighboring region. Residency is not the same as cross-border transfer, and the legal interpretation comes from the customer's legal team.\n\n**Multilingual**: Generating directly in the target language usually reads more naturally than translating back and forth, but it must be evaluated per language; maintain a glossary so policy names aren't paraphrased; slice the golden set by \"language × country × intent\", calibrate the judge with native-speaker raters, and cap the gap between the worst language and English—eval_gate.py's slice regression check is designed precisely for this.\n\n**Trade-offs**: N regions = N times the running cost; version drift is handled by a single unified pipeline, with independent gates per country and per-region canaries.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Japanese answers are noticeably worse\": first use the slices to determine whether it's a retrieval or a generation problem, add glossary entries and examples, and switch models if necessary.","L6-T6C.4.16":"A fraud investigation assistant is a classic design question in bank risk scenarios: it covers transactions, chat records, historical cases, and AML reports, and often comes with the follow-up \"do you need a graph database\".\n\n**Core principle: the assistant gathers evidence, the investigator rules**\n- The assistant collects and organizes evidence, and **the investigator makes the conclusions**; summaries separate \"evidence\" from \"inference\", with inferences carrying a confidence note;\n- **Every conclusion cites source records** (transaction IDs, document passages, and the SQL that produced the result); conclusions without a source are dropped in post-processing rather than displayed;\n- Structured data goes through read-only SQL tools with row-level security and a dry-run byte cap; unstructured data goes through retrieval with case-level ACLs;\n- Investigator isolation is enforced at the tool and data layer via the case ID in the session and identity propagation, not via prompts;\n- All queries, retrieval records, model outputs, and human decisions go into an append-only audit log, recording model and prompt versions, and plug into the bank's existing model risk process.\n\n**Do you need a graph database**: Introduce one only when investigators need **multi-hop traversal** that is painful in SQL (rings sharing devices, layered transfers) with interactive response times; **first validate the value with recursive SQL on a small sample**, then consider graph options such as Spanner Graph (availability and fit need verification). If existing capabilities suffice, don't add components.\n\n**Evaluation**: Use closed cases as the gold set to measure evidence recall, citation accuracy, and the rate of misleading conclusions; zero tolerance for cross-case data leaks; business metrics are time to close and investigator-rated usefulness.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"What if it misses fraud\": measure recall on historically labeled cases and compare with the current process; the assistant is additive, and the high-risk process itself doesn't change."});
